        },
        {
          "section_id": "34eafd6a-927d-422a-9eca-b5e1051607fb",
          "key": "3",
          "md": "**Action:** Set a re-check for Q2 2026 when the extraordinary MEPC session is expected to reconvene.\n\n**Why now:** As a FACT: \"The MEPC met on 14-17 October and agreed to adjourn the meeting for one year until 2026.\" The adoption of the Net-Zero Framework — and the legally binding pricing mechanism — is now contingent on the 2026 session outcome.\n\n*Operational implication:* The adjournment creates a planning window. Workspaces with multi-year ocean freight contracts negotiated now should include clauses anticipating GHG-related surcharges once the Framework enters force.\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "34efa1f5-f2f2-459a-9db5-8ac6fa610cd6",
          "key": "2",
          "md": "**Action:** Map which client contracts include sustainability or Scope 3 emissions reporting requirements that reference ocean shipping emissions, and assess whether current carrier data flows can satisfy those requests.\n\n**Why now:** The IMO Net-Zero Framework introduces a new \"IMO GFI Registry\" tracking each ship's compliance status including GHG Fuel Intensity calculations. *Operational implication:* As this registry becomes operational, clients asking for verified ocean emissions data will be able to cross-reference against IMO records, raising the evidentiary bar for Scope 3 ocean shipping disclosures.\n\n**Cost impact:** Current cost of data gap not publicly available. Gap-filling via third-party emissions calculators carries a cost that is specific to vendor and contract scope.\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "371249fd-645e-4f57-a3a9-d9aa3384cc65",
          "key": "11",
          "md": "## 1 — Carrier Emissions Data Pipeline\n\n**Scope:** Build or verify a data pipeline from ocean carriers to the workspace's sustainability reporting system that captures per-voyage fuel consumption, GFI values (once available from the IMO registry), and CII ratings.\n\n**Gap from current baseline:** *Operational implication:* Workspaces currently receiving only aggregate carrier sustainability reports (annual carbon reports, fleet-average figures) will not be able to provide voyage-level or shipment-level GHG data to clients once the GFI Registry is operational. The gap between fleet-average data and voyage-specific GFI data is likely to become visible to clients who cross-reference IMO registry records.\n\n**Deadline:** No mandatory deadline on the workspace. The GFI Registry establishment date is pending the 2026 adoption outcome.\n\n**Action:** Engage ocean carriers now to confirm their data-sharing capability at voyage or booking level.\n\n## 2 — Contract Clause Review — GHG Surcharge Pass-Through\n\n**Scope:** Review existing and template ocean freight contracts for language covering GHG-related surcharge pass-through.\n\n**Gap from current baseline:** *Operational implication:* The IMO Net-Zero Framework's pricing mechanism, once in force, will generate new carrier cost items that will be passed through as surcharges. Contracts that do not reference GHG or carbon pricing surcharges may create disputes over who bears the cost.\n\n**Deadline:** No mandatory deadline on the workspace. *Analytical inference:* Given the 2026 adoption target and the subsequent 16-month entry-into-force window, contracts with terms extending into 2028 are the immediate scope for clause review.\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* Whether specific contract language adequately allocates GHG surcharge risk requires legal review of each contract under applicable law.\n\n## 3 — Scope 3 Emissions Accounting for Ocean Mode\n\n**Scope:** Ensure the workspace's Scope 3 Category 4 (upstream transportation) and Category 9 (downstream transportation) accounting methodology is capable of incorporating vessel-level GFI data when the IMO registry goes live.\n\n**Gap from current baseline:** *Operational implication:* Current ISO 14083 and GLEC methodology for ocean shipping typically uses fuel-based or distance-based estimation in the absence of carrier-provided activity data. The IMO GFI Registry's well-to-wake methodology will provide a higher-resolution, verified data source. Workspaces that do not adapt their accounting methodology to use registry data when it becomes available will face credibility challenges in client and regulatory reporting.\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "3c5524bd-08e3-4ce3-8c2e-7f8576f1c98b",
          "key": "7",
          "md": "## Q1 — Does the workspace fall within the direct scope of MARPOL Annex VI?\n\n**Decided (factual basis):** MARPOL Annex VI addresses vessel operators and flag states. The EPA source confirms obligations apply to \"U.S. flagged ships wherever located and to non-U.S. flagged ships operating in U.S. waters.\" A freight forwarding workspace that does not operate vessels is outside the direct scope.\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* In specific transaction structures where the workspace charters a vessel or acts as operator for a voyage, the question of whether it becomes an obligated party under Annex VI requires legal review specific to that transaction.\n\n*Source: MARPOL Annex VI and the Act To Prevent Pollution From Ships (APPS), U.S. EPA, current. https://www.epa.gov/enforcement/marpol-annex-vi-and-act-prevent-pollution-ships-apps\n\n## Q2 — Does the 5,000 GT threshold exclude any vessels the workspace commonly uses?\n\n**Decided (factual basis):** As a FACT: \"The IMO Net-Zero Framework applies to all oceangoing ships over 5,000 gross tonnage (GT) - these ships are responsible for over 85% of global shipping emissions.\" Ships under 5,000 GT are currently not covered.\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n*Operational implication:* For the workspace's ocean cargo — which may include project freight, fine art, or automotive via specialised vessels — verifying whether the carrier vessel exceeds 5,000 GT is necessary to determine Net-Zero Framework applicability to that voyage.\n\n## Q3 — Are ships between 400 GT and 5,000 GT likely to be brought into scope?\n\n**In progress:** As a FACT: \"Ships under 5,000 GT are currently not covered, but discussions are underway to possibly include ships between 400 GT and 5,000 GT in the future.\"\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n*Analytical inference:* If the workspace uses smaller specialised vessels for certain cargo verticals (e.g., roll-on/roll-off for high-value automotive on shorter routes), those vessels may be brought into scope in a future revision. Monitor the MEPC session outcomes in 2026.\n\n## Q4 — Does the GHG pricing mechanism constitute a carbon tax under any national law?\n\n**Decided (factual basis):** As a FACT confirmed by IMO: the pricing mechanism \"works very differently\" from a carbon tax and \"functions as a global incentive and funding framework for shipping decarbonization, not as a uniform global carbon tax.\"\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* Whether any national tax authority characterises IMO GHG compliance fees as a taxable transaction or deductible cost under applicable national law requires jurisdiction-specific legal advice.\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "400a8866-93a7-4ebd-a149-3e2ffe12a159",
          "key": "1",
          "md": "**Action:** Verify the CII (Carbon Intensity Indicator) ratings of ocean carriers the workspace currently uses on tertiary ocean lanes (transatlantic, trans-Pacific, Americas-Asia).\n\n**Why now:** CII entered into force in 2023. Carriers rated D for three consecutive years or E in any year are required to submit corrective action plans. *Operational implication:* Carriers with persistently low CII ratings may face operational restrictions or cost increases that affect service reliability and surcharge levels for the workspace's ocean-routed cargo.\n\n**Cost impact:** Current rate not publicly available at carrier-specific level. *Analytical inference:* Carriers passing through Annex VI compliance costs via bunker adjustment factors and low-sulphur fuel surcharges have already embedded some cost in freight rates; additional GHG pricing under the Net-Zero Framework will add further cost pressure from the framework's enforcement date onward.\n\n**Cargo verticals affected:** All ocean-shipped verticals, with greatest impact on high-value automotive and humanitarian cargo where ocean is a viable primary or secondary mode.\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "5ce7b899-1340-4fee-9356-936462bae8ed",
          "key": "14",
          "md": "| Date | Event | What the workspace must have done |\n|---|---|---|\n| **19 May 2005** | MARPOL Annex VI entered into force | In force as of this date. Carrier compliance baseline established. |\n| **1 January 2015** | New ECA sulphur standards entered into force (North American ECA, 0.1% S) | In force as of this date. Carriers on U.S.-touching ocean lanes already subject to these limits. |\n| **1 January 2020** | Global 0.5% sulphur cap entered into force | *Specific entry-into-force date for the 2020 global sulphur cap: Specific date not available as verbatim span from the corpus source blocks; the EPA source references \"new international standards for sulfur emissions from ships that went into effect on Jan. 1, 2015\" for the ECA. The 2020 global cap date is noted in the EPA penalty policy reference but not as a verbatim span.* |\n| **2023** | EEXI and CII entered into force | In force. Carriers operating vessels on the workspace's ocean lanes are now subject to energy efficiency design limits (EEXI) and annual carbon intensity ratings (CII). Workspace: verify CII ratings of active ocean carriers. |\n| **No later than 1 January 2026** | Mandatory revision of EEXI and CII | Workspace: monitor revision outcome; revised measures may tighten carrier operating costs. |\n| **October 2025 (adjourned to 2026)** | Extraordinary MEPC session — formal adoption of IMO Net-Zero Framework | As a FACT: \"The MEPC met on 14-17 October and agreed to adjourn the meeting for one year until 2026.\" Workspace: set re-check for Q3–Q4 2026. |\n| **2026 (expected)** | Extraordinary MEPC reconvenes — adoption vote | Workspace: ensure contract clause review for GHG surcharge pass-through is complete before this session. Monitor for two-thirds majority outcome and tacit acceptance period opening. |\n| **~16 months post-adoption** | IMO Net-Zero Framework entry into force | As a FACT: \"regulations are expected to enter into force 16 months later.\" *Analytical inference:* If adopted in late 2026, entry into force would be approximately early–mid 2028. Workspace: GFI Registry operational, carrier surcharge pass-through active. |\n| **By or around 2050** | Net-zero GHG emissions target for international shipping | IMO 2023 GHG Strategy strategic endpoint. |\n\n**Primary deadline (required slot):** The headline compliance deadline for the IMO Net-Zero Framework's GHG pricing mechanism is contingent on the 2026 adoption outcome. As a FACT: \"Once adopted, under the tacit acceptance procedure, the regulations are expected to enter into force 16 months later.\" *Analytical inference:* No fixed calendar deadline is in force as of July 2025 for the Net-Zero Framework's pricing mechanism; the EEXI and CII are already in force from 2023.\n\n**Penalty summary (required slot):** As a FACT for the existing Annex VI: The EPA penalty policy \"applies to violations of new international standards for sulfur emissions from ships\" and \"violations may result in criminal and/or civil liability\" under APPS and Annex VI. For the Net-Zero Framework: \"Vessels that exceed those limits must pay tiered compliance fees\" — the specific fee tiers are stated in the IMO FAQ as having been set, but the exact values are stated as subject to the adopted legal text which has been adjourned. *Specific penalty fee amounts for the IMO Net-Zero Framework's pricing tiers not available as verbatim figures from the corpus source blocks; these are conditional on the adopted legal text in 2026.* For the workspace as freight forwarder: no direct penalty provision under MARPOL Annex VI is identified in the corpus for a non-vessel-operating freight forwarder.\n\n*Source: MARPOL Annex VI and the Act To Prevent Pollution From Ships (APPS), U.S. EPA, current. https://www.epa.gov/enforcement/marpol-annex-vi-and-act-prevent-pollution-ships-apps\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "805e7277-7a7b-40b6-a067-c6bc81a41987",
          "key": "10",
          "md": "## Obligations on Vessel Operators (Indirect workspace relevance)\n\nAs a FACT: Vessel operators holding IAPP certificates issued by flag state authorities (in the U.S., by USCG) are the registered parties. \"U.S. flagged vessels are subject to inspection for compliance with Annex VI. Non-U.S. flagged ships are subject to examination under Port State Control while operating in U.S. waters.\"\n\n*Source: MARPOL Annex VI and the Act To Prevent Pollution From Ships (APPS), U.S. EPA, current. https://www.epa.gov/enforcement/marpol-annex-vi-and-act-prevent-pollution-ships-apps\n\n## IMO Data Collection System (DCS)\n\nThe IMO source references \"IMO Data Collection System (DCS)\" as a component of the improving energy efficiency of ships regime, indicating that fuel data collection reporting already exists for qualifying vessels. *Specific DCS reporting format and timeline not available as verbatim spans from the corpus source blocks.*\n\n*Source: Climate action and clean air in shipping, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/ourwork/environment/pages/decarbonization%20and%20clean%20air%20in%20shipping.aspx\n\n## IMO GFI Registry (Pending)\n\nAs a FACT: \"A new digital IMO GFI Registry will be established to track each ship's compliance status, including: Surplus units banked and remedial units acquired, GFI calculations, Transfer and cancellation records.\"\n\n*Operational implication:* Once the GFI Registry is operational, the workspace can, in principle, verify the GFI compliance status of specific vessels used for ocean routing — supporting Scope 3 client reporting with verifiable, ship-level data.\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n## Workspace Reporting Obligations\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* Whether the workspace has any direct registration or reporting obligation to IMO, USCG, EPA, or any other regulatory authority under MARPOL Annex VI — in its capacity as freight forwarder, importer of record, or logistics manager — requires legal confirmation. No direct registration obligation on freight forwarders is identified in the corpus source blocks.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "899e0d62-2f67-43ef-9403-1d4dad886716",
          "key": "4",
          "md": "## Direct Obligations\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* MARPOL Annex VI, as written, places direct obligations on vessel operators, shipowners, flag states, port states, and engine manufacturers. The EPA source confirms: \"Annex VI requirements comprise both engine-based and fuel-based standards, and apply to U.S. flagged ships wherever located and to non-U.S. flagged ships operating in U.S. waters.\" The workspace, as a freight forwarder, is not a vessel operator, flag state, or port state. Whether any Annex VI obligation directly attaches to the workspace in any transaction requires legal confirmation specific to each jurisdiction.\n\n*Source: MARPOL Annex VI and the Act To Prevent Pollution From Ships (APPS), U.S. EPA, current. https://www.epa.gov/enforcement/marpol-annex-vi-and-act-prevent-pollution-ships-apps\n\n## Indirect Chain Position\n\n*Operational implication:* The workspace sits downstream of the compliance obligation in the ocean freight chain. The vessel operator (carrier) holds the IAPP certificate, maintains fuel records, and is subject to port state inspection. The workspace's exposure is:\n\n1. **Cost pass-through:** Low-sulphur fuel surcharges (since 2015 ECA requirements) and future GHG pricing surcharges.\n2. **Carrier selection risk:** Choosing carriers with poor CII ratings may result in service disruption if those carriers face enforcement-driven operational restrictions.\n3. **Reporting chain:** For client Scope 3 reporting, the workspace depends on carriers to provide accurate activity data (fuel consumption per voyage, GFI values once the registry is active).\n4. **Contract exposure:** *Legal Confirmation Required:* whether any client contract or sustainability certification framework (e.g., CDP, GHG Protocol) creates a contractual obligation on the workspace to use only Annex VI-compliant carriers requires legal review of each specific contract.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "8f0fdcb4-5193-412f-950e-c16bb607a960",
          "key": "6",
          "md": "## MONITORING | Extraordinary MEPC Session — Adoption of IMO Net-Zero Framework (2026)\n\n**Event type:** Treaty amendment adoption session (extraordinary MEPC).\n**Issuing body:** IMO Marine Environment Protection Committee (MEPC).\n**Status / current phase:** As a FACT: \"The MEPC met on 14-17 October and agreed to adjourn the meeting for one year until 2026.\"\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n**Expected timing:** *Analytical inference:* Based on the one-year adjournment from October 2025, the reconvened extraordinary session is expected in October 2026. Re-check window: Q3–Q4 2026.\n**What happens next:** As a FACT: \"Once adopted, under the tacit acceptance procedure, the regulations are expected to enter into force 16 months later at which point governments will be responsible for enforcing them.\"\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n*Operational implication:* For the workspace negotiating multi-year ocean freight contracts in 2025–2026, the Net-Zero Framework's GHG pricing mechanism is not yet legally binding but its adoption trajectory is now defined. Contract clauses that anticipate GHG surcharge pass-through are advisable ahead of the 2026 vote.\n\n*Analytical inference:* ## MONITORING | EEXI and CII Mandatory Revision — No Later Than 1 January 2026\n\n*Analytical inference:* **Event type:** Mandatory regulatory review.\n**Status:** ICCT confirms: \"both measures are set to be revised by no later than January 1, 2026.\" Revision outcome not confirmed in corpus as of this generation.\n\n*Source: IMO's newly revised GHG strategy: What it means for shipping and the Paris Agreement, ICCT, July 7, 2023. https://theicct.org/marine-imo-updated-ghg-strategy-jul23/\n\n*Analytical inference:* If the revision strengthens CII (for example, by introducing consequences for persistent D/E-rated ships or extending coverage to well-to-wake emissions), carriers with low-rated fleets may face additional operating cost increases that pass through to freight rates on the workspace's ocean lanes.\n\n## MONITORING | IMO GFI Registry — Establishment Timeline\n\n**Event type:** New digital compliance infrastructure.\n**Issuing body:** IMO.\n**Status:** As a FACT: \"A new digital IMO GFI Registry will be established to track each ship's compliance status, including: Surplus units banked and remedial units acquired, GFI calculations, Transfer and cancellation records.\"\n\n*Specific launch date for the IMO GFI Registry not available from primary sources as of July 2025.*\n\n*Operational implication:* Once the Registry is live, ocean emissions data for specific voyages will be verifiable by external parties. Workspaces providing Scope 3 client reporting will face pressure to align their ocean emissions figures with Registry data.\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "bfcb7bcc-1a9e-437e-9fb2-98ea8133e0b1",
          "key": "13",
          "md": "## ICCT Assessment — 2023 GHG Strategy vs Paris Agreement\n\n*Industry interpretation:* The ICCT analysis finds: \"the revised strategy is not compatible with 1.5°C but is compatible with well below 2°C.\" The estimate is that \"international shipping will exceed its current share of the world's 1.5°C carbon budget by approximately 2032.\" Under the revised strategy, \"if shipping can get to zero WTW CO₂e emissions by 2050 along this pathway, it will not exceed its well below 2°C budget.\"\n\n*Source: IMO's newly revised GHG strategy: What it means for shipping and the Paris Agreement, ICCT, July 7, 2023. https://theicct.org/marine-imo-updated-ghg-strategy-jul23/\n\n*Operational implication:* For the workspace managing client sustainability commitments aligned to 1.5°C pathways, the IMO strategy's compatibility gap with 1.5°C is a material disclosure point. Claiming ocean shipping is Paris-aligned at 1.5°C on the basis of IMO regulatory compliance alone is not supported by ICCT's analysis.\n\n## ICCT Assessment — EEXI Stringency\n\n*Industry interpretation:* ICCT finds the EEXI \"is not stringent enough — it's expected to avoid about 1% of future emissions by 2030.\" This is a significant qualification on the value of EEXI compliance as a sustainability credential for ocean cargo.\n\n*Source: IMO's newly revised GHG strategy: What it means for shipping and the Paris Agreement, ICCT, July 7, 2023. https://theicct.org/marine-imo-updated-ghg-strategy-jul23/\n\n## GHG Fuel Standard — Required Intensity Reduction (ICCT Modelling)\n\n*Industry interpretation:* ICCT presented analysis showing \"aligning with 1.5°C would require a 38% reduction in the WTW GHG intensity of marine fuels by 2030, 97% by 2040, and 100% by 2050.\"\n\n*Source: IMO's newly revised GHG strategy: What it means for shipping and the Paris Agreement, ICCT, July 7, 2023. https://theicct.org/marine-imo-updated-ghg-strategy-jul23/\n\n## FuelEU Maritime and EU ETS — Regional Alternatives\n\n*Analytical inference:* ICCT notes that \"the European Union did this under FuelEU Maritime, which is similar to the proposed IMO GFS, and by incorporating shipping into its Emissions Trading System.\" For the workspace's ocean cargo moving on EU-touching trade lanes, the EU-level regime is a parallel — and in some respects more stringent — compliance environment affecting carrier costs before the IMO Net-Zero Framework enters force.\n\n*Source: IMO's newly revised GHG strategy: What it means for shipping and the Paris Agreement, ICCT, July 7, 2023. https://theicct.org/marine-imo-updated-ghg-strategy-jul23/\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c054d88b-adab-4473-9af2-c5235688c6c3",
          "key": "12",
          "md": "## Ships Under 5,000 GT\n\nAs a FACT: \"Ships under 5,000 GT are currently not covered\" by the IMO Net-Zero Framework. The classical Annex VI air pollution provisions (NOx, SOx) apply to ships above different thresholds (NOx: engines above 130 kW; SOx: no explicit GT floor stated in the corpus for the global cap).\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n## Scrubber Equivalence (SOx)\n\nAnnex VI recognises exhaust gas cleaning systems (scrubbers) as an equivalent compliance pathway for the sulphur limit. *Operational implication:* The workspace's ocean carriers may be operating on open-loop or closed-loop scrubbers as an alternative to low-sulphur fuel. This has implications for port access (some ports have banned open-loop scrubber discharge into port waters) and for the workspace's sustainability claims about its ocean supply chain.\n\n*Source: Climate action and clean air in shipping, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/ourwork/environment/pages/decarbonization%20and%20clean%20air%20in%20shipping.aspx\n\n## Fuel Oil Non-Availability (FONAR) Procedure\n\nAs a FACT: \"ship owners and operators of vessels heading to ports in the United States that are unable to purchase compliant fuel oil may satisfy the MARPOL Annex VI Regulation 18.2.4 requirement to notify the competent authority of the relevant port of destination by notifying the cognizant U.S. Coast Guard Captain of the Port (COPT).\" This is the formal non-availability exemption mechanism under Regulation 18.2.4.\n\n*Source: MARPOL Annex VI and the Act To Prevent Pollution From Ships (APPS), U.S. EPA, current. https://www.epa.gov/enforcement/marpol-annex-vi-and-act-prevent-pollution-ships-apps\n\n## Voting and Tacit Acceptance Threshold\n\nAs a FACT: \"an amendment must be adopted by a two-thirds majority of the Parties to the Convention that are present and voting. For issues relating to the IMO Net Zero Framework, this means only Parties to MARPOL Annex VI (the 1997 Protocol) may vote. There are currently 108 such Parties, representing over 97% of the world's merchant shipping fleet by tonnage.\" Once adopted, the amendment \"is deemed accepted at the end of a minimum period of ten months, unless within that period objections are made by no less than one third of the Parties OR by Parties with a combined merchant fleet tonnage of not less than 50% of world tonnage.\"\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "cac96109-1789-4301-af96-a590da4dc691",
          "key": "8",
          "md": "## A — NOx Emission Limits (Direct obligation on vessel operators)\n\nAs a FACT: Annex VI establishes \"Limits on NOx emissions from marine diesel engines with a power output of more than 130 kW.\" The 130 kW threshold is the scope qualifier; engines below this output are not subject to the NOx limits. \"The standards apply to both main propulsion and auxiliary engines.\"\n\n*Source: MARPOL Annex VI and the Act To Prevent Pollution From Ships (APPS), U.S. EPA, current. https://www.epa.gov/enforcement/marpol-annex-vi-and-act-prevent-pollution-ships-apps\n\n*Specific NOx Tier I/II/III g/kWh threshold values and construction-year triggers not available as verbatim spans from the corpus source blocks. Retrieve from enacted Annex VI text (Regulation 13).*\n\n## B — SOx and Fuel Sulphur Content (Direct obligation on vessel operators)\n\nAs a FACT: Annex VI establishes \"Limits on the sulfur content of marine fuels.\" ECA-specific requirements apply to ships operating within 200 nautical miles of U.S. shores. A penalty policy for violations \"applies to violations of new international standards for sulfur emissions from ships that went into effect on Jan. 1, 2015, and violations under the previous standards.\"\n\n*Source: MARPOL Annex VI and the Act To Prevent Pollution From Ships (APPS), U.S. EPA, current. https://www.epa.gov/enforcement/marpol-annex-vi-and-act-prevent-pollution-ships-apps\n\n*Specific sulphur percentage cap values (global 0.5% from 2020, ECA 0.1% from 2015) not available as verbatim spans from the corpus source blocks. Retrieve from enacted Annex VI text (Regulation 14).*\n\nEquivalence pathway: Annex VI provides for scrubbers (\"SOx scrubber, etc.\") as an equivalent compliance pathway for the sulphur limit. This is listed explicitly in the IMO source as an \"Equivalents\" subject under the Annex VI framework.\n\n*Source: Climate action and clean air in shipping, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/ourwork/environment/pages/decarbonization%20and%20clean%20air%20in%20shipping.aspx\n\n## C — Certificate and Recordkeeping Requirements (Direct obligation on vessel operators)\n\nAs a FACT: Each regulated diesel engine in U.S.-flagged vessels requires an EIAPP certificate issued by EPA. Certain vessels also require an IAPP certificate issued by USCG. \"Ship operators must also maintain records on board regarding their compliance with the emission standards, fuels requirements and other provisions of Annex VI.\"\n\n*Source: MARPOL Annex VI and the Act To Prevent Pollution From Ships (APPS), U.S. EPA, current. https://www.epa.gov/enforcement/marpol-annex-vi-and-act-prevent-pollution-ships-apps\n\n## D — GHG Fuel Intensity (GFI) Reporting — Annual (IMO Net-Zero Framework, pending adoption)\n\nAs a FACT: \"Ships must report their GHG Fuel Intensity (GFI) to the IMO each year.\" The GFI is expressed in \"grams of CO₂ equivalent per megajoule (gCO₂eq/MJ).\" Coverage is \"well-to-wake\" — \"from extraction and production to use on board the ship.\"\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n*Specific annual GFI reduction trajectory (the year-by-year tightening schedule) not available as verbatim threshold values from the corpus source blocks. The IMO FAQ references \"set GFI limits, which gets stricter every year\" and \"two tiers of compliance\" but the specific threshold values and dates are stated as contingent on the adopted legal text, which has been adjourned to 2026.*\n\n## E — GHG Fuel Intensity Verification (IMO Net-Zero Framework, pending adoption)\n\nAs a FACT: \"To make sure the data is accurate, traceable and credible, fuel emissions and sustainability must be independently verified through a Sustainable Fuel Certification Scheme recognized by the IMO's main environmental body, the Marine Environment Protection Committee (MEPC).\"\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n## F — IMO 2023 GHG Strategy Targets (Strategic framework, not yet binding law for each target)\n\n*Analytical inference:* As a FACT from the ICCT analysis: \"member states just agreed to (1) reach net-zero GHG emissions by or around 2050 and (2) 'indicative checkpoints' that call for reducing total GHG emissions by 20% and striving for 30% by 2030 and 70% and striving for 80% by 2040, both relative to 2008.\"\n\n*Source: IMO's newly revised GHG strategy: What it means for shipping and the Paris Agreement, ICCT, July 7, 2023. https://theicct.org/marine-imo-updated-ghg-strategy-jul23/\n\n*Analytical inference:* ICCT characterises the revised strategy as: \"While this revised strategy is not legally binding, the measures used to implement it can be.\" The strategy is a directional framework; binding implementation occurs through MARPOL amendments such as EEXI, CII, and the pending Net-Zero Framework.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "d35e9577-5a53-46ff-a98b-29840ea57570",
          "key": "5",
          "md": "## MARPOL Annex VI — Core Air Pollution Provisions (Synthesised from EPA and IMO Sources)\n\n### SOx and Fuel Sulphur Content\n\nAs a FACT: Annex VI establishes \"Limits on the sulfur content of marine fuels.\" Ships operating in the North American ECA (within 200 nautical miles of the U.S. coast) \"must meet the most advanced standards for NOx emissions and use fuel with lower sulfur content.\"\n\n*Source: MARPOL Annex VI and the Act To Prevent Pollution From Ships (APPS), U.S. EPA, current. https://www.epa.gov/enforcement/marpol-annex-vi-and-act-prevent-pollution-ships-apps\n\nAnnex VI contains equivalence provisions: the IMO source lists \"Equivalents (SOx scrubber, etc.)\" as a subject under Annex VI, indicating that exhaust gas cleaning systems (scrubbers) are recognised as an alternative compliance pathway for the sulphur limit, subject to IMO approval.\n\n*Source: Climate action and clean air in shipping, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/ourwork/environment/pages/decarbonization%20and%20clean%20air%20in%20shipping.aspx\n\n*Specific sulphur percentage thresholds (global cap and ECA cap values) not available as verbatim spans from the primary source blocks in this corpus. The EPA source references \"new international standards for sulfur emissions from ships that went into effect on Jan. 1, 2015\" and subsequent revisions. Full threshold trajectory (including the 2020 global 0.5% cap) requires retrieval from the MARPOL Annex VI enacted text.*\n\n### NOx Emission Limits\n\nAs a FACT: Annex VI establishes \"Limits on NOx emissions from marine diesel engines with a power output of more than 130 kW. The standards apply to both main propulsion and auxiliary engines and require the engines to be operated in conformance with the Annex VI NOx emission limits.\"\n\n*Source: MARPOL Annex VI and the Act To Prevent Pollution From Ships (APPS), U.S. EPA, current. https://www.epa.gov/enforcement/marpol-annex-vi-and-act-prevent-pollution-ships-apps\n\n*Specific NOx tier thresholds (Tier I, II, III) and their applicable construction-year triggers are not available as verbatim spans from the corpus source blocks. Full threshold trajectory requires retrieval from the MARPOL Annex VI enacted text.*\n\n### Ozone-Depleting Substances, VOC, and Shipboard Incineration\n\nThe IMO source confirms Annex VI covers: \"SOx, NOx, ODS, VOC shipboard incineration.\" Detailed provisions for each are listed as distinct subject areas on the IMO's climate and clean air page but are not further elaborated in the corpus source blocks.\n\n*Source: Climate action and clean air in shipping, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/ourwork/environment/pages/decarbonization%20and%20clean%20air%20in%20shipping.aspx\n\n*Specific ODS, VOC, and incineration threshold values and exemptions not available as verbatim spans from the corpus. These require retrieval from Annex VI enacted text or MEPC guidance circulars.*\n\n## Certificate and Record Requirements\n\nAs a FACT: \"Each regulated diesel engine in U.S. flagged vessels must have an Engine International Air Pollution Prevention (EIAPP) certificate, issued by EPA, to document that the engine meets Annex VI NOx standards. Certain vessels are also required to have an International Air Pollution Prevention Certificate (IAPP), which is issued by the United States Coast Guard (USCG). Ship operators must also maintain records on board regarding their compliance with the emission standards, fuels requirements and other provisions of Annex VI.\"\n\n*Source: MARPOL Annex VI and the Act To Prevent Pollution From Ships (APPS), U.S. EPA, current. https://www.epa.gov/enforcement/marpol-annex-vi-and-act-prevent-pollution-ships-apps\n\n*Analytical inference:* ## EEXI and CII — Energy Efficiency and Carbon Intensity\n\nAs a FACT: The IMO Net-Zero Framework \"is intended to achieve the goals of the IMO's 2023 GHG Strategy, working alongside earlier GHG reduction measures (adopted in 2011 and 2021) which were focused on energy-efficient ship design, operational improvements and carbon intensity ratings.\"\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n*Analytical inference:* The ICCT analysis confirms: \"Two of these [short-term measures] entered into force in 2023: the Energy Efficiency Existing Ship Index (EEXI) and the Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII). These measures are legally binding because they are incorporated into an international treaty, the International Convention for the Prevention of Pollution from Ships (MARPOL, for short).\"\n\n*Source: IMO's newly revised GHG strategy: What it means for shipping and the Paris Agreement, ICCT, July 7, 2023. https://theicct.org/marine-imo-updated-ghg-strategy-jul23/\n\n*Operational implication:* The EEXI and CII measures directly govern the vessels carrying the workspace's ocean cargo. CII grades ships A–E annually. ICCT notes: \"ships that receive a D for three consecutive years or an E in any year are required to draft and implement a plan of corrective actions. However, there are no requirements for what must be included in these plans, and there is never a time when a ship's environmental certificates are revoked, no matter how many times the ship fails.\"\n\n*Source: IMO's newly revised GHG strategy: What it means for shipping and the Paris Agreement, ICCT, July 7, 2023. https://theicct.org/marine-imo-updated-ghg-strategy-jul23/\n\n*Analytical inference:* ICCT's assessment that the EEXI \"is expected to avoid about 1% of future emissions by 2030\" and that CII \"merely grades ships\" without hard consequences suggests the current iteration of these measures has limited operational teeth. The Net-Zero Framework's GHG pricing mechanism is intended to be the driver of actual fuel transition.\n\n## IMO Net-Zero Framework — GHG Fuel Intensity Standard and Pricing Mechanism\n\nAs a FACT: \"The IMO Net-Zero Framework refers to a new set of international regulations aimed at reducing greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions from ships, in line with IMO's 2023 GHG Strategy. It has two main parts, which are linked: A global fuel standard that requires ships to gradually reduce how polluting its ship fuel can be (i.e. how much greenhouse gas is emitted for each unit of energy used, across a fuel's life cycle); and A pricing mechanism with set prices on the GHG ships emit, to encourage the industry to lower emissions to comply with the global fuel standard.\"\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n### GHG Fuel Intensity (GFI) Metric\n\nAs a FACT: \"Ship emissions are measured using a metric called GHG Fuel Intensity (GFI). GFI calculates how much greenhouse gas is emitted for each unit of energy used, expressed in grams of CO₂ equivalent per megajoule (gCO₂eq/MJ). 'CO₂ equivalent' means that not only carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions are covered, but also other greenhouse gases such as methane (CH4) or nitrous oxide (N2O). The framework covers emissions from the entire fuel lifecycle - from extraction and production to use on board the ship - known as the 'well-to-wake' approach.\"\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n### Compliance Mechanism\n\nAs a FACT: \"Ships must report their GHG Fuel Intensity (GFI) to the IMO each year. Each ship must keep its emissions below set GFI limits, which gets stricter every year. If a ship exceeds set thresholds (there are two tiers of compliance), it must balance the excess 'deficit' by using: Surplus units (transferred from other over-compliant ships or banked from previous years); or Remedial units (acquired by paying into the IMO Net-Zero Fund). If a ship emits below a certain threshold, it can bank or trade its surplus units. If a ship fully switches to zero or near-zero emission fuels, it qualifies for financial rewards.\"\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n### ZNZ Fuels Definition\n\nAs a FACT: \"ZNZs stand for 'zero or near-zero' emission fuels, technologies or energy sources. Generally, ZNZs have a GHG Fuel Intensity (GFI) of no more than 19.0 gCO₂eq/MJ, which is 80% lower than the current average of 93.3 gCO₂eq/MJ.\"\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n### IMO Net-Zero Fund\n\nAs a FACT: \"The IMO Net-Zero Fund will receive, manage and disburse collected revenue from GHG pricing contributions. These revenues will then be used to: Reward low-emission ships; Support innovation, research, infrastructure and just transition initiatives in developing countries; Fund training, technology transfer and capacity building to support the IMO GHG Strategy; and Mitigate negative impacts on vulnerable States, such as Small Island Developing States and Least Developed Countries.\"\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n### What the IMO Net-Zero Framework Is NOT\n\nAs a FACT: \"The IMO's greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions pricing mechanism has been widely mischaracterized as a global carbon tax, but it works very differently. Rather than imposing a single, fixed tax rate on emissions that feeds into consolidated revenue used for other purposes, the IMO system sets performance-based GHG-intensity targets for ships. Vessels that exceed those limits must pay tiered compliance fees, while those that outperform can earn tradeable credits and be eligible for financial rewards.\"\n\n*Source: The IMO Net-Zero Framework - FAQs, IMO, current as of 2025. https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/faqs-the-imo-net-zero-framework.aspx\n\n---"
        },
