        {
          "section_id": "284399e5-daf6-41e5-9839-3090643145e2",
          "key": "6",
          "md": "**What the workspace can credibly say:**\n\n- ZEMBA has completed two competitive tender rounds. Two carriers — Hapag-Lloyd and North Sea Container Line — have committed to deploying hydrogen-derived e-methanol and a second e-fuel pathway from 2027, with a total committed abatement of at least 120,000 tCO₂e over three years across the second tender. *Source: Aspen Institute — ZEMBA Second Tender Winners, aspeninstitute.org, December 17 2025. https://www.aspeninstitute.org/news/zemba-second-tender-winners/\n- The first tender delivered more than 82,000 tCO₂e avoided via biomethane on the Singapore–Rotterdam corridor in 2025–26, using a book-and-claim mechanism. *Source: Aspen Institute — Inaugural ZEMBA Tender Completion, aspeninstitute.org, 2024. https://www.aspeninstitute.org/news/zemba-inaugural-tender-2024/\n- Membership is no-fee and open to freight buyers; as of early 2026 the alliance has more than 45 members. *Source: ZEMBA Official Website, shipzemba.org, 2026. https://www.shipzemba.org/\n- ZEMBA has been named a 2026 Earthshot Prize Nominee. *Source: ZEMBA Official Website, shipzemba.org, March 19 2026. https://www.shipzemba.org/\n\n**What to avoid saying:**\n\n- Do not claim that routing cargo through Hapag-Lloyd automatically generates a ZEMBA Scope 3 reduction credit for the shipper. Scope 3 credit flows through the ZEMBA membership and book-and-claim structure, not through a carrier relationship alone. *Legal Confirmation Required:* how emission reduction certificates are allocated to specific shippers under the ZEMBA book-and-claim mechanism requires confirmation directly from ZEMBA programme materials or counsel.\n- Do not quote a cost premium or cost savings figure — none has been published in the source material available.\n- Do not represent ZEMBA as a regulatory requirement. It is a voluntary procurement coalition.\n\n**Questions to pose to clients:**\n\n- Is your company a ZEMBA member or evaluating membership? If not, are your major ocean carriers ZEMBA participants?\n- Does your sustainability reporting require Scope 3 ocean freight emission reduction credentials, and what standard (e.g., Science Based Targets initiative, GHG Protocol) governs how you count them?\n- Do your major ocean trade lanes (particularly Asia–Europe) overlap with the ZEMBA-contracted corridors Hapag-Lloyd is deploying on from 2027?\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "2c770292-8775-42d1-a9d6-0365f7db1486",
          "key": "1",
          "md": "MONITORING\n\nZEMBA is described by its secretariat as \"a first-of-its-kind buyers group working to accelerate the most sustainable, scalable, and economically viable solutions for the maritime sector.\" *Source: ZEMBA Official Website, shipzemba.org, 2026. https://www.shipzemba.org/\n\nThe triggering condition is a structural market failure: 99% of vessels on the water today operate on high-emission fuels, yet no single cargo buyer commands enough freight volume to move the carrier market alone. *Source: ZEMBA Official Website, shipzemba.org, 2026. https://www.shipzemba.org/ ZEMBA's design is an aggregated demand commitment — a collective buyers group — that concentrates offtake signals to levels carriers can finance against.\n\nThe alliance was co-founded by the Aspen Institute, Amazon, Patagonia, and Tchibo. *Source: Aspen Institute ZEMBA Launch Announcement, aspeninstitute.org, 2023. https://aspeninstitute.org/news/zero-emission-maritime-buyers-alliance-launch As of early 2026, membership has grown to over 45 companies, including Amazon, IKEA, and Nike. *Source: Hapag-Lloyd ZEMBA Second Tender Press Release, hapag-lloyd.com, December 17 2025. https://www.hapag-lloyd.com/en/company/press/releases/2025/12/hapag-lloyd-wins-zemba-s-second-tender-for-pioneering-e-fuel-dep.html\n\nThe alliance has run two competitive tender rounds, both won by Hapag-Lloyd, producing binding e-fuel deployment commitments — moving ZEMBA from a policy signal into a live procurement instrument. *Source: Aspen Institute — ZEMBA Second Tender Winners, aspeninstitute.org, December 17 2025. https://www.aspeninstitute.org/news/zemba-second-tender-winners/ *Source: Aspen Institute — Inaugural ZEMBA Tender Completion, aspeninstitute.org, 2024. https://www.aspeninstitute.org/news/zemba-inaugural-tender-2024/\n\nIn March 2026, ZEMBA was named a 2026 Earthshot Prize Nominee. *Source: ZEMBA Official Website, shipzemba.org, March 19 2026. https://www.shipzemba.org/ *Analytical inference:* Earthshot Prize nomination is a mainstream sustainability-credibility signal; it increases ZEMBA's visibility with corporate sustainability and procurement teams, which could accelerate membership growth beyond 45 companies.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "305c0cd8-1b15-4791-a787-2a4cc370a44f",
          "key": "5",
          "md": "**Carrier stratification is already underway:**\nHapag-Lloyd has won both ZEMBA tenders — the only carrier to do so. *Source: Hapag-Lloyd ZEMBA Second Tender Press Release, hapag-lloyd.com, December 17 2025. https://www.hapag-lloyd.com/en/company/press/releases/2025/12/hapag-lloyd-wins-zemba-s-second-tender-for-pioneering-e-fuel-dep.html *Source: Aspen Institute — Inaugural ZEMBA Tender Completion, aspeninstitute.org, 2024. https://www.aspeninstitute.org/news/zemba-inaugural-tender-2024/ North Sea Container Line is the second-tender co-winner. *Source: Aspen Institute — ZEMBA Second Tender Winners, aspeninstitute.org, December 17 2025. https://www.aspeninstitute.org/news/zemba-second-tender-winners/\n\n*Analytical inference:* A freight forwarder with established Hapag-Lloyd relationships and routing capability on the trans-oceanic lanes covered by the ZEMBA tenders (the first tender covered Singapore–Rotterdam) is positioned to offer clients a meaningful Scope 3 credential that forwarders without those relationships cannot match today.\n\n*Analytical inference:* Carriers not participating in ZEMBA face a growing risk that sustainability-motivated cargo owners route away from them — at present a marginal risk at 45 members, but one that scales with membership and regulatory pressure. Workspaces building carrier panels should note this stratification is active and citable.\n\n**Named competitor positioning:**\n*Specific competitor freight-forwarder positioning relative to ZEMBA not available from primary sources as of 2026-05-28.* No sourced reporting on named competitors' ZEMBA affiliation or exclusion has been found in the source blocks provided.\n\n**ZEMBA as a client-facing differentiator:**\n*Analytical inference:* For workspaces serving clients who are ZEMBA members or whose sustainability teams track ZEMBA (Amazon, IKEA, Nike are named publicly), the ability to articulate ZEMBA's mechanism, name the deployed fuels, and route cargo through the winning carriers is a commercial differentiator in RFP and tender responses.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "4acbcaf6-646e-4cb1-8a33-c39a5d350a85",
          "key": "7",
          "md": "**1. Map client exposure to ZEMBA membership — within 60 days.**\n*Operational implication:* Identify which current or prospective clients are ZEMBA members or have named Amazon, IKEA, or Nike-equivalent sustainability targets. For those clients, the workspace's ability to route ocean freight through ZEMBA-contracted carriers is a commercial capability gap or advantage to be assessed and communicated.\n\n**2. Establish a Hapag-Lloyd commercial relationship review — within 90 days.**\nHapag-Lloyd is the only carrier to win both ZEMBA tenders. The second tender's e-methanol deployment begins 2027 on a trans-oceanic lane. *Source: Hapag-Lloyd ZEMBA Second Tender Press Release, hapag-lloyd.com, December 17 2025. https://www.hapag-lloyd.com/en/company/press/releases/2025/12/hapag-lloyd-wins-zemba-s-second-tender-for-pioneering-e-fuel-dep.html *Operational implication:* Confirm whether commercial terms with Hapag-Lloyd on relevant trans-oceanic lanes (Asia–Europe in particular) can be structured to pass through or reference ZEMBA-contracted e-fuel volumes for clients with Scope 3 reporting needs.\n\n**3. Evaluate ZEMBA membership or observer status — within 90 days.**\nMembership is no-fee. *Source: ZEMBA Official Website, shipzemba.org, 2026. https://www.shipzemba.org/ *Analytical inference:* A freight forwarder participating as a member — if the workspace qualifies under ZEMBA's eligibility criteria — would gain early visibility into future tenders, carrier pricing signals, and the mechanics of book-and-claim accounting. *Legal Confirmation Required:* whether the workspace, in its role as freight forwarder or NVOCC rather than cargo beneficial owner, qualifies for ZEMBA membership under the alliance's current eligibility rules requires direct confirmation from ZEMBA.\n\n**4. Track RFP 3 issuance — monitor from Q3 2026.**\n*Analytical inference:* Based on the 2024–2025 cadence, a third tender is plausible within 2026 or 2027. Early visibility into the RFP would allow the workspace to brief clients in advance of the tender window and position relevant cargo volumes. The ZEMBA website's \"The Latest\" section and the Aspen Institute's news channel are the tracking points.\n\n**5. Connect ZEMBA developments to EU regulatory monitoring — ongoing.**\n*Analytical inference:* FuelEU Maritime enforcement begins 2025; the EU ETS covers shipping from 2024. ZEMBA's e-fuel volumes may interact with both instruments in ways that affect how carriers price and allocate clean-fuel capacity. Workspaces advising clients on EU-regulated trade lanes should monitor whether ZEMBA-contracted fuel volumes generate FuelEU compliance credits and how that changes carrier cost structures on EU routes. *Specific confirmation not available from primary sources as of 2026-05-28.*\n\n---\n\n## New Sources Identified\n\n| Source Name | URL | Tier estimate (1–7) | Why this source matters |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ZEMBA Official Website (shipzemba.org) | https://www.shipzemba.org/ | 2 | Primary participant site operated by the alliance; carries mission, membership count, tender history, Earthshot Prize nomination, and links to full RFP documentation — authoritative for current status and structure. |\n| Aspen Institute – ZEMBA Second Tender Winners Announcement | https://www.aspeninstitute.org/news/zemba-second-tender-winners/ | 2 | Secretariat-issued announcement confirming second tender outcome, named winners, 120k tCO₂e commitment, e-methanol and e-ammonia pathways, and 2027 deployment date — primary source for Phase 2 facts. |\n| Aspen Institute – Inaugural ZEMBA Tender Completion | https://www.aspeninstitute.org/news/zemba-inaugural-tender-2024/ | 2 | Secretariat-issued announcement of first tender completion — primary source for Phase 1 facts including 82k tCO₂e avoided, Singapore–Rotterdam corridor, biomethane fuel, and book-and-claim mechanism. |\n| Hapag-Lloyd – ZEMBA Second Tender Press Release | https://www.hapag-lloyd.com/en/company/press/releases/2025/12/hapag-lloyd-wins-zemba-s-second-tender-for-pioneering-e-fuel-dep.html | 2 | Carrier-side official confirmation of second tender win; provides e-methanol specs, 90% lifecycle carbon intensity figure, 2027 deployment start, net-zero 2045 target, and CEO quote — authoritative carrier-side corroboration of Aspen Institute announcement. |\n| Center for Green Market Activation (GMA) – Maritime Program | https://gmacenter.org/program/maritime/ | 3 | Key technical partner page confirming GMA's role in ZEMBA tender design and administration, and carrying the 17-inaugural-contracts figure and two e-fuel pathways detail — needed to confirm figures referenced in corroborator leads but not yet span-verified in available source blocks. |\n| Ellen MacArthur Foundation – ZEMBA Case Study | https://www.ellenmacarthurfoundation.org/circular-examples/zemba-a-buyers-alliance-to-unlock-zero-emission-shipping | 3 | Independent authoritative case study providing governance model detail, neutral third-party structure, Lloyd's Register and GMA roles, 40+ member growth trajectory, and antitrust safeguard design — adds structural depth not available from participant-issued sources alone. |\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "9bcd5394-2161-4427-a61f-b7ade964e3ff",
          "key": "2",
          "md": "**Founding and secretariat:**\nThe Aspen Institute serves as institutional convenor and secretariat host. The alliance is described as a \"non-profit buyer's group.\" *Source: Hapag-Lloyd ZEMBA Second Tender Press Release, hapag-lloyd.com, December 17 2025. https://www.hapag-lloyd.com/en/company/press/releases/2025/12/hapag-lloyd-wins-zemba-s-second-tender-for-pioneering-e-fuel-dep.html\n\n**Cargo owner members (demand side):**\nThe 45+ member companies are cargo owners and brand-name shippers. Named in sourced material: Amazon, IKEA, Nike, Patagonia, and Tchibo. *Source: Hapag-Lloyd ZEMBA Second Tender Press Release, hapag-lloyd.com, December 17 2025. https://www.hapag-lloyd.com/en/company/press/releases/2025/12/hapag-lloyd-wins-zemba-s-second-tender-for-pioneering-e-fuel-dep.html Their stated interest is to \"credibly reduce your company's Scope 3 emissions, and lower long-term costs of the clean energy transition by channeling investment toward the most scalable fuels and technologies.\" *Source: ZEMBA Official Website, shipzemba.org, 2026. https://www.shipzemba.org/\n\n**Winning carrier (supply side):**\nHapag-Lloyd has won both the inaugural 2024 tender and the second 2025 tender. *Source: Hapag-Lloyd ZEMBA Second Tender Press Release, hapag-lloyd.com, December 17 2025. https://www.hapag-lloyd.com/en/company/press/releases/2025/12/hapag-lloyd-wins-zemba-s-second-tender-for-pioneering-e-fuel-dep.html The carrier's stated interest is its net-zero fleet operations target by 2045. *Source: Hapag-Lloyd ZEMBA Second Tender Press Release, hapag-lloyd.com, December 17 2025. https://www.hapag-lloyd.com/en/company/press/releases/2025/12/hapag-lloyd-wins-zemba-s-second-tender-for-pioneering-e-fuel-dep.html\n\n**Second tender co-winner:**\nNorth Sea Container Line (NCL) is a co-winner of the second tender. *Source: Aspen Institute — ZEMBA Second Tender Winners, aspeninstitute.org, December 17 2025. https://www.aspeninstitute.org/news/zemba-second-tender-winners/\n\n**No-fee membership structure:**\nThe alliance operates with no membership fee, described on its own site as \"an easy yes.\" *Source: ZEMBA Official Website, shipzemba.org, 2026. https://www.shipzemba.org/ *Analytical inference:* Zero cost of entry is a deliberate growth strategy; the alliance's leverage scales with aggregate member freight volume, so low friction to join serves the coalition's commercial negotiating power.\n\n*Specific membership breakdown by freight volume, sector, or geography not available from primary sources as of 2026-05-28.*\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "a5766f1e-fdcc-458a-9896-bd508bc4ce70",
          "key": "3",
          "md": "MONITORING — re-check Q3 2027\n\n**Phase 1 completed — Inaugural tender (2024):**\nHapag-Lloyd won ZEMBA's first tender, with \"greenhouse gas emissions reduction over 90%\" delivered via biomethane, covering \"1B+ container-miles Singapore–Rotterdam 2025–26\" using a book-and-claim mechanism. *Source: Aspen Institute — Inaugural ZEMBA Tender Completion, aspeninstitute.org, 2024. https://www.aspeninstitute.org/news/zemba-inaugural-tender-2024/ The first tender resulted in \"17 inaugural contracts.\" *Specific contract count for RFP 1 not independently confirmed in the body text available from primary sources as of 2026-05-28* — *Analytical inference:* the 17-contract figure circulates in GMA programme materials referenced in corroborating source leads; it is listed under New Sources Identified below for retrieval and confirmation.\n\n**Phase 2 completed — Second tender (December 2025):**\nZEMBA members committed to \"at least 120,000 metric tons of CO₂e over a minimum of three years,\" with Hapag-Lloyd deploying e-methanol and North Sea Container Line deploying a second hydrogen-derived e-fuel pathway beginning in 2027. *Source: Hapag-Lloyd ZEMBA Second Tender Press Release, hapag-lloyd.com, December 17 2025. https://www.hapag-lloyd.com/en/company/press/releases/2025/12/hapag-lloyd-wins-zemba-s-second-tender-for-pioneering-e-fuel-dep.html *Source: Aspen Institute — ZEMBA Second Tender Winners, aspeninstitute.org, December 17 2025. https://www.aspeninstitute.org/news/zemba-second-tender-winners/\n\nThe e-methanol deployed by Hapag-Lloyd \"will deliver a carbon intensity reduction of at least 90% on a lifecycle basis relative to conventional high emission fuel.\" *Source: Hapag-Lloyd ZEMBA Second Tender Press Release, hapag-lloyd.com, December 17 2025. https://www.hapag-lloyd.com/en/company/press/releases/2025/12/hapag-lloyd-wins-zemba-s-second-tender-for-pioneering-e-fuel-dep.html\n\n**Phase 3 forward — Third tender and beyond:**\n*Specific date, scope, or fuel type for a third ZEMBA tender not available from primary sources as of 2026-05-28.* The ZEMBA site references a section titled \"Beyond the Tenders,\" indicating the alliance is planning activities beyond tender rounds, but substantive content from that section was not available in the source blocks provided. *Source: ZEMBA Official Website, shipzemba.org, 2026. https://www.shipzemba.org/\n\n*Analytical inference:* Based on the cadence of tenders — one in 2024, one awarded December 2025 with deployment from 2027 — a third tender would plausibly be issued in 2026 or 2027, to layer additional fuel volumes or carriers into the programme. This is an inference from observed cadence, not a stated date.\n\n**Conversion triggers — what turns this signal into commercial norm:**\n\n- E-methanol and e-ammonia on-water deployment starts in 2027. If those deployments prove operationally reliable and cost-competitive at scale, ZEMBA members will have an auditable Scope 3 reduction credential that non-member shippers will lack. *Analytical inference:* that gap becomes a commercial differentiator in client-facing sustainability reporting and RFP responses.\n- IMO's revised GHG strategy (net-zero by or around 2050, with interim 2030 and 2040 checkpoints) creates a regulatory floor that ZEMBA's demand-side action anticipates. *Analytical inference:* When IMO tightens the Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) regime or if the FuelEU Maritime regulation creates fuel-blend obligations for vessels calling EU ports, ZEMBA-contracted e-fuel volumes become a carrier compliance asset — not only a shipper sustainability credential. This intersection deserves monitoring; see the No-Vacuum / regulatory link section below.\n- Member count growth beyond 45 to a point at which non-members face disadvantage in carrier capacity allocation or sustainability-driven client requirements. *Analytical inference:* the threshold at which exclusion from ZEMBA becomes commercially costly is not established in any source and cannot be asserted.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "b3f19289-b1a6-452e-9196-1461071480f1",
          "key": "4",
          "md": "**Ocean freight — primary exposure for workspaces with ocean-dependent cargo:**\n\nZEMBA operates exclusively in the ocean shipping sector. *Source: ZEMBA Official Website, shipzemba.org, 2026. https://www.shipzemba.org/ The mechanism for member shippers is collective commitment to e-fuel offtake, which enables ZEMBA to issue tenders to carriers. The workspace, as freight forwarder, does not book freight directly in its own Scope 3 category as a shipper in most transaction structures — *Legal Confirmation Required:* whether a freight forwarder acting as NVOCC or as agent for cargo owners would qualify as a ZEMBA member and whether Scope 3 emission reductions flow to the forwarder or to the cargo owner requires counsel and ZEMBA programme-team review.\n\n*Operational implication:* For workspaces whose clients are or aspire to become ZEMBA members, the workspace's ability to route cargo through ZEMBA-contracted carriers (currently Hapag-Lloyd and NCL on specific trans-oceanic lanes) becomes a service capability, not merely a preference.\n\n*Operational implication:* The book-and-claim mechanism used in the inaugural tender means the emission reduction credential does not require physical cargo to travel on the exact e-fuel-powered vessel; the certificate is separated from the voyage. This is operationally significant for workspaces handling complex multi-modal routings where physical fuel traceability is not possible.\n\n*Specific cost premium for ZEMBA-contracted e-fuel freight vs. conventional bunker freight not available from primary sources as of 2026-05-28.* The alliance describes a goal to \"lower long-term costs of the clean energy transition,\" but no per-unit or per-TEU cost figure is disclosed in the source blocks available. *Source: ZEMBA Official Website, shipzemba.org, 2026. https://www.shipzemba.org/\n\n**Air freight — no direct applicability:**\nZEMBA is a maritime-specific initiative. No ZEMBA activity applies to air-freight operations. Workspaces whose primary mode is air are unaffected operationally; the relevance is commercial and reputational, as described under the client-conversation section below.\n\n**Regulatory link — FuelEU Maritime and EU ETS:**\n*Analytical inference:* FuelEU Maritime (in force from 1 January 2025 for vessels ≥5,000 GT on EU routes) sets greenhouse gas intensity reduction targets for ship fuels. E-methanol qualifying at ≥90% lifecycle carbon intensity reduction, as committed in the ZEMBA second tender, would likely count toward FuelEU intensity targets. Hapag-Lloyd's ZEMBA deployment is thus simultaneously a demand-side coalition output and a carrier-side FuelEU compliance asset. This intersection means ZEMBA-contracted volumes may become structurally more valuable as FuelEU intensity targets tighten toward 2030 and 2035 checkpoints — but this is an inference; FuelEU's specific methodology for counting e-methanol under ZEMBA's book-and-claim structure requires verification against the FuelEU implementing acts. *Specific confirmation not available from primary sources as of 2026-05-28.*\n\n---"
        }
      ],
