      "sections": [
        {
          "section_id": "0679769f-1a0f-4330-94ff-2475e6343709",
          "key": "4",
          "md": "## Direct Obligations Under CORSIA\n\n*Analytical inference:* CORSIA places direct monitoring, reporting, verification, and offsetting obligations on **aircraft operators** (airlines) operating international flights between participating State pairs. The framework is implemented through national aviation authorities of ICAO member states, which in turn inform individual airlines of their offsetting requirements. *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n\"The emissions data collected or estimated from 138 member states was used to calculate the global growth of emissions from international civil aviation for 2024, the start year of the voluntary First Phase of the scheme.\" *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n## Workspace Role: Freight Forwarder and Shipper\n\n*Analytical inference:* Workspaces operating as freight forwarders, or as shippers contracting air capacity from ICAO-regulated aircraft operators, sit outside the direct CORSIA compliance chain. The compliance obligation runs to the aircraft operator, not to the freight forwarder or cargo principal.\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* Whether any national implementation of CORSIA by a participating state — particularly within the EU via the EU ETS Directive — creates secondary obligations on freight forwarders, customs brokers, or shippers of record on specific routes is a legal determination. The EU ETS aviation obligations are documented separately (see the European Commission source at https://climate.ec.europa.eu/eu-action/transport-decarbonisation/reducing-emissions-aviation_en) and *Legal Confirmation Required:* as to whether they extend to cargo principals.\n\n## Cost-Chain Position\n\n*Analytical inference:* The workspace's commercial exposure is as a downstream absorber or pass-through entity for CORSIA compliance costs. Airlines facing $1.3–$1.7 billion in annual compliance spend for 2025–2026 have incentive to recover costs through rate structures. Workspaces moving high-margin, time-sensitive cargo verticals (live events, luxury goods, fine art, film and television, humanitarian) on air lanes crossing CORSIA-participating State pairs are the point of commercial impact.\n\n## EU ETS Interaction\n\nThe EU ETS covers \"all airlines operating in Europe, European and non-European alike\" for intra-EEA flights through at least the start of 2027, with CORSIA implemented via the EU ETS Directive for international flights departing from or arriving at airports within the EEA. *Source: European Commission, Climate Action, \"Reducing emissions from aviation,\" undated. https://climate.ec.europa.eu/eu-action/transport-decarbonisation/reducing-emissions-aviation_en.\n\n*Analytical inference:* For workspaces whose European air freight moves between EEA airports (intra-EEA) and on routes departing EEA airports internationally, both EU ETS and CORSIA cost components are active in carrier pricing simultaneously. Understanding which cost component applies to each route segment is a condition of accurate commercial forecasting.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "0baaa3d1-187f-4622-8444-5768d6fd9a74",
          "key": "8",
          "md": "## Scope of Coverage\n\nCORSIA applies to international civil aviation between CORSIA-participating State pairs. The scheme does not cover domestic aviation. *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n## Baseline Determination\n\n*Analytical inference:* The baseline for the First Phase (2024–2026) is 85% of total 2019 CO₂ emissions for all applicable State pairs. The 2024 baseline was 305,522,071 tonnes of CO₂. *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n## Sector Growth Factor and Offsetting Calculation\n\n*Analytical inference:* By applying the Sector's Growth Factor to emissions data submitted by each airline, governments calculate the amount of CO₂ each airline is required to offset. The 2024 Sector's Growth Factor is 0.15405257. *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325. Total 2024 emissions for applicable State pairs reached 361,159,641 tonnes. *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n## Eligible Emission Units\n\n*Analytical inference:* Airlines meet offsetting obligations through the purchase and cancellation of CORSIA Eligible Emission Units (EEUs). Approved registries for the Second Phase (2027–2029) include Gold Standard and Verra (VCS Program), both newly approved by ICAO. Isometric has also been approved to issue verified carbon removal credits under CORSIA. *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n*Analytical inference:* The first large-scale retirement of CORSIA Phase 1 credits was completed in March 2026: \"A total of 180,000 Gold Standard carbon credits were retired by Shell, on behalf of Japan Airlines, to meet its obligations under Phase 1 of CORSIA.\" *Source: Gold Standard, 24 March 2026. https://www.goldstandard.org/news/first-large-scale-corsia-retirement-of-gold-standard-carbon-credits-by-a-commercial-airline.\n\n## CORSIA Eligible Fuels as an Offsetting Alternative\n\nCORSIA allows aircraft operators to reduce offsetting requirements through use of CORSIA eligible fuels. These are defined in Annex 16, Volume IV and fall into two categories: CORSIA sustainable aviation fuel (\"a renewable or waste-derived aviation fuel that meets the CORSIA Sustainability Criteria under this Volume\") and CORSIA lower carbon aviation fuel (\"a fossil-based aviation fuel that meets the CORSIA Sustainability Criteria under this Volume\"). *Source: ICAO CORSIA Eligible Fuels page, ICAO, November 2025. https://www.icao.int/CORSIA/corsia-eligible-fuels.\n\nEligible fuels must come \"from fuel producers that are certified by a Sustainability Certification Scheme (SCS) approved by the ICAO Council to perform this certification.\" *Source: ICAO CORSIA Eligible Fuels page, ICAO, November 2025. https://www.icao.int/CORSIA/corsia-eligible-fuels.\n\n## Monitoring, Reporting, and Verification (MRV)\n\nCORSIA MRV standards are defined in Annex 16, Volume IV, specifically:\n- Section 2.2.4: Monitoring of CORSIA eligible fuels claims\n- Section 2.3.3: Reporting of CORSIA eligible fuels\n- Section 2.4.3: Verification of CORSIA eligible fuels\n\n*Source: ICAO CORSIA Eligible Fuels page, ICAO, November 2025. https://www.icao.int/CORSIA/corsia-eligible-fuels.\n\n*Analytical inference:* The CORSIA Central Registry (CCR) is the primary data repository: \"Information on emissions and other data submitted by Member States through the CORSIA Central Registry (CCR), along with CORSIA documentation, is published on the ICAO website.\" *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n## Aggregate Cost Obligations on Airlines\n\nIATA projects total CORSIA compliance cost of $1.7 billion for 2026, rising from $1.3 billion for 2025. IATA anticipates airlines will need to purchase \"upwards of 200 million EEUs for cancellation and compliance by late 2027, costing an estimated $4-5 billion, and increasing to nearly 2 billion EEUs through to 2035.\" *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n## Penalty Provision\n\n*Specific penalty / enforcement provision for airline non-compliance with CORSIA is not available from primary sources as of 2026-06-06.* CORSIA is implemented through national aviation authorities of ICAO member states; enforcement mechanisms are set at the state level and are not described in the source pool. *Legal Confirmation Required:* Enforcement consequences for airlines operating on routes the workspace uses are a carrier-level legal question.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "28a4e18c-7fb6-47e7-867f-c43e38137f40",
          "key": "11",
          "md": "*No direct CORSIA system obligations fall on the workspace as a freight forwarder or cargo shipper.* The following operational requirements are identified as preparatory actions driven by CORSIA's commercial consequences, not as legal compliance mandates on the workspace.\n\n## Carrier Cost Transparency Tracking\n\n*Operational implication:* The workspace should build a simple tracking mechanism to identify, for each primary air carrier and trade lane, whether:\n- CORSIA-derived surcharges have been introduced or are announced\n- The carrier participates in SAF programs that may affect the offsetting/surcharge ratio\n- The carrier's verified Phase 1 EEU retirement is underway (publicly traceable via the CCR and CORSIA Central Registry)\n\nThis is not a CORSIA legal obligation on the workspace. It is a commercial intelligence requirement driven by confirmed $1.3–1.7 billion annual compliance costs at the airline level passing toward freight rates in 2026–2027.\n\n## Client Reporting Capability for Emissions Data\n\n*Analytical inference:* Clients in the live events, film and television, and luxury goods verticals increasingly request Scope 3 emissions data for shipped freight. CORSIA data on per-flight CO₂ emissions (publicly available via CCR) is relevant to Scope 3 air freight calculations. The workspace's ability to reference verifiable CORSIA/ICAO CO₂ data for specific carriers and routes supports credibility in client sustainability conversations.\n\n*Specific figures required from carriers for accurate Scope 3 allocation are carrier-specific and not publicly available at per-shipment granularity as of 2026-06-06.*\n\n## SAF Supply Chain Monitoring for Carrier Procurement\n\n*Analytical inference:* As CORSIA eligible fuels allow aircraft operators to reduce their offsetting obligations, carriers with access to SAF at the airports the workspace uses may face lower net compliance costs. ICAO's November 2025 update to eligible fuel lifecycle emissions values (Eighth Edition) and feedstock classifications expands the SAF pathway pool. *Source: ICAO CORSIA Eligible Fuels page, ICAO, November 2025. https://www.icao.int/CORSIA/corsia-eligible-fuels. Monitoring which primary carriers are uplifting CORSIA-eligible SAF on key routes informs rate negotiation strategy.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "2a1bfe75-3218-4fe8-a408-9e959b605f91",
          "key": "15",
          "md": "| # | Title | Type | Publisher | Date | URL |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | CORSIA Homepage | Intergovernmental body position (Tier 3) | ICAO | Undated | https://icao.int/environmental-protection/CORSIA/Pages/default.aspx |\n| 2 | ICAO releases first-ever growth factor for airlines' CORSIA offsetting requirements | News reporting (Tier 5) | GreenAir News | 18 December 2025 | https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325 |\n| 3 | First large-scale CORSIA retirement of Gold Standard carbon credits by a commercial airline | Industry body / operator release (Tier 4) | Gold Standard | 24 March 2026 (updated 12 May 2026) | https://www.goldstandard.org/news/first-large-scale-corsia-retirement-of-gold-standard-carbon-credits-by-a-commercial-airline |\n| 4 | CORSIA Eligible Fuels | Intergovernmental body position (Tier 3) | ICAO | November 2025 | https://www.icao.int/CORSIA/corsia-eligible-fuels |\n| 5 | Reducing emissions from aviation | Regulator guidance (Tier 2) | European Commission, Climate Action | Undated | https://climate.ec.europa.eu/eu-action/transport-decarbonisation/reducing-emissions-aviation_en |\n| 6 | ICAO Carbon Offsetting and Reduction Scheme for International Aviation (CORSIA) | Regulator page (Tier 2) | EASA | Undated | https://www.easa.europa.eu/en/domains/environment/icao-carbon-offsetting-and-reduction-scheme-international-aviation-corsia |\n| 7 | IATA CORSIA Fact Sheet | Industry body interpretation (Tier 4) | IATA | Undated | https://www.iata.org/en/iata-repository/pressroom/fact-sheets/fact-sheet-corsia/ |\n\n*Note: Sources 6 and 7 (EASA and IATA) returned navigation/security pages only in the source pool — no grounded fact spans were extractable from their content. They are listed for completeness and as leads for direct retrieval.*\n\n---\n\n## New Sources Identified\n\n| Source Name | URL | Tier estimate (1-7) | Why this source matters |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ICAO Assembly Resolution A42-22 | https://www.icao.int/environmental-protection/CORSIA/Pages/A42-22.aspx | 1 | Binding ICAO Assembly resolution establishing CORSIA's legal basis and phase structure; primary normative source for all CORSIA obligations. |\n| ICAO Annex 16 Volume IV (Environmental Technical Manual) | https://www.icao.int/environmental-protection/CORSIA/Pages/ETM-Volume-IV.aspx | 1 | Codifies CORSIA MRV Standards, eligible fuels monitoring and reporting requirements; essential for any section-level analysis of substantive obligations. |\n| CORSIA Central Registry (CCR) | https://www.icao.int/environmental-protection/CORSIA/Pages/CCR.aspx | 3 | ICAO's public data repository for state-submitted emissions data and EEU retirement records; enables carrier-level cross-referencing for Scope 3 reporting. |\n| IATA CORSIA Fact Sheet (direct retrieval needed) | https://www.iata.org/en/iata-repository/pressroom/fact-sheets/fact-sheet-corsia/ | 4 | IATA's industry interpretation of CORSIA obligations for airlines; key source for cost estimates and compliance pathway analysis. |\n| EASA CORSIA Implementation Page (direct retrieval needed) | https://www.easa.europa.eu/en/domains/environment/icao-carbon-offsetting-and-reduction-scheme-international-aviation-corsia | 2 | EASA is the EU's implementing authority for CORSIA via the EU ETS Directive; page content would ground EU-specific implementation obligations. |\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "4564882b-ccff-4af5-8add-d5ba58b8b878",
          "key": "12",
          "md": "## Intra-EEA Flights: EU ETS Only, Not CORSIA\n\nFor flights within the EEA (between EEA airports), \"only the EU ETS will continue to apply.\" CORSIA, as implemented via the EU ETS Directive, applies to international flights departing from or arriving at EEA airports. *Source: European Commission, \"Reducing emissions from aviation,\" undated. https://climate.ec.europa.eu/eu-action/transport-decarbonisation/reducing-emissions-aviation_en.\n\n*Operational implication:* For workspaces routing cargo on intra-European air lanes (e.g., intra-EEA legs of a multi-segment Europe-to-Asia shipment), the compliance cost environment is EU ETS, not CORSIA. This distinction matters for rate analysis and client emissions reporting by segment.\n\n## Voluntary vs. Mandatory Participation by State\n\n*Analytical inference:* The First Phase (2024–2026) is voluntary participation by states. Airlines have flights requiring offsetting only on routes between participating State pairs. *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n*Analytical inference:* Routes between a non-participating state and any other state are not subject to CORSIA offsetting in the First Phase. The list of participating states is maintained by ICAO. *Legal Confirmation Required:* Determining which specific routes operated by the workspace's carrier partners are subject to Phase 1 CORSIA offsetting requires cross-referencing the ICAO State participation list against carrier route networks — a carrier-specific legal and operational question.\n\n## CORSIA Eligible Fuel Use Reduces Offsetting Requirement\n\nAirlines that uplift CORSIA eligible fuels may reduce their offsetting obligations proportionally. This creates a carrier-level differentiation: carriers with higher SAF uptake face lower EEU purchase requirements and potentially lower compliance pass-through costs. *Source: ICAO CORSIA Eligible Fuels page, ICAO, November 2025. https://www.icao.int/CORSIA/corsia-eligible-fuels.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "54211d94-362e-4244-8701-4e2eb52da397",
          "key": "6",
          "md": "## MONITORING — EU ETS Aviation Scope Assessment (European Commission, Due July 2026)\n\n**Event type:** Legislative assessment with potential legislative proposal to follow.\n**Issuing body:** European Commission.\n**Expected date:** By July 2026. *Source: European Commission, Climate Action, \"Reducing emissions from aviation,\" undated. https://climate.ec.europa.eu/eu-action/transport-decarbonisation/reducing-emissions-aviation_en.\n\nThe Commission \"will carry out an assessment of whether more action is required for flights to and from Europe, in line with criteria set in the EU ETS Directive.\" The assessment will evaluate two scenarios: (a) a legislative proposal extending EU ETS scope to departing international flights while exempting incoming flights; or (b) maintaining intra-EEA scope if CORSIA \"is strengthened and has a high level of global participation and implementation.\" *Source: European Commission, \"Reducing emissions from aviation,\" undated. https://climate.ec.europa.eu/eu-action/transport-decarbonisation/reducing-emissions-aviation_en.\n\n**What the workspace should expect to need to decide:** If the Commission proposes EU ETS expansion to departing international flights, all carriers operating transatlantic and Asia-Pacific routes from EEA airports face additional compliance costs — on top of CORSIA — creating a compounded cost-pass-through risk for workspaces moving cargo on those lanes.\n\n*Re-check window:* August 2026.\n\n---\n\n## MONITORING — CORSIA Second Phase (2027–2029): Gold Standard and Verra Approved as EEU Suppliers\n\n**Event type:** Phase transition with expanded eligible emissions unit supply.\n**Issuing body:** ICAO.\n**Effective date of Second Phase:** 2027.\n\n\"Gold Standard has been approved by ICAO to supply eligible emission units for CORSIA's Second Phase, covering the period 2027 to 2029.\" *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325. Verra's Verified Carbon Standard (VCS) Program has also been approved \"to supply EEUs for use in CORSIA's Second Phase.\" *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n*Analytical inference:* Expanded EEU supply for Phase 2 may moderate per-unit offset costs relative to a supply-constrained scenario, but IATA's projection of \"nearly 2 billion EEUs through to 2035\" indicates volume demand will remain the dominant cost driver. *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n*Re-check window:* Q1 2027 (Phase 2 start).\n\n---\n\n## MONITORING — CORSIA Periodic Review\n\nICAO publishes a CORSIA Periodic Review process referenced on the CORSIA homepage. *Source: ICAO CORSIA homepage, ICAO, undated. https://icao.int/environmental-protection/CORSIA/Pages/default.aspx. *Specific date and scope of the next periodic review are not available from primary sources as of 2026-06-06.*\n\n---\n\n## MONITORING — CORSIA Eligible Fuels Documentation Updates (Ongoing; Last Updated November 2025)\n\nICAO's CORSIA eligible fuels documents are subject to Council amendment. The most recent updates to the Default Life Cycle Emissions Values (Eighth Edition) and the Methodology for Calculating Actual Life Cycle Emissions Values (Seventh Edition) were published November 2025. *Source: ICAO CORSIA Eligible Fuels page, ICAO, November 2025. https://www.icao.int/CORSIA/corsia-eligible-fuels.\n\n*Analytical inference:* SAF pathway expansions and revised lifecycle values directly affect which fuels airlines can claim as CORSIA eligible, affecting how much of their offsetting requirement is met through fuel use versus EEU purchases — and therefore the net cost passed to cargo customers.\n\n*Re-check window:* Following ICAO Council sessions, typically quarterly.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "58f2a294-85dd-4e96-a455-2c27b9113aae",
          "key": "record_facts",
          "md": "*Analytical inference:* [primary_deadline] No verbatim primary deadline statement was located in the captured source text for this record-grade item. A full-brief regrounding will re-examine this gap when this item upgrades from record to brief."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "6a3879a5-ad3d-49aa-b064-3fa8f9b6f30e",
          "key": "3",
          "md": "## COST ALERT — Phase 1 Compliance Costs Are Materialising Now; Expect Rate Pass-Through in 2026–2027\n\n**Action:** Brief commercial and operations leadership on the confirmed CORSIA offsetting cost trajectory for 2026 and prepare for air freight rate adjustments.\n\nIATA projects CORSIA compliance costs rising to $1.7 billion for 2026, from $1.3 billion for 2025. In the First Phase (2024–2026), IATA expects airlines to purchase upwards of 200 million Eligible Emission Units (EEUs) for cancellation and compliance by late 2027, \"costing an estimated $4-5 billion, and increasing to nearly 2 billion EEUs through to 2035.\" *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n*Operational implication:* For workspaces operating under carrier contracts or spot-rate agreements with airlines on Americas, Europe, or Asia lanes, cost pass-through from these compliance obligations is a present risk in 2026 billing cycles and into 2027.\n\n**Cost quantification status:** A precise per-kilogram or per-shipment pass-through figure is not publicly available as of 2026-06-06. Directional range: aggregate airline compliance cost is confirmed at $1.3–1.7 billion for 2025–2026. *Specific per-unit freight surcharge is not available from primary sources as of 2026-06-06.*\n\n---\n\n*Analytical inference:* ## ACTION REQUIRED — Phase 1 Compliance Deadline for Airlines: January 2028\n\n**Action:** Confirm with all primary air carriers on routes operated whether offsetting compliance for Phase 1 (2024–2026 emissions) will be reflected in surcharges before or after the January 2028 deadline, and whether any charges will be invoiced retrospectively.\n\nGold Standard confirms that \"further retirements are expected throughout 2026 and 2027 as airlines prepare to meet CORSIA's Phase 1 compliance deadline in January 2028.\" *Source: Gold Standard, 24 March 2026. https://www.goldstandard.org/news/first-large-scale-corsia-retirement-of-gold-standard-carbon-credits-by-a-commercial-airline.\n\n*Operational implication:* Carrier-side compliance spending concentrated in 2026–2027 creates a window in which rate adjustments, new surcharges, or contract renegotiations may be introduced on lanes the workspace relies upon for live events, fine art, and film and television cargo.\n\n---\n\n## MONITORING — EU ETS Scope Extension Assessment Due July 2026\n\n**Action:** Track the European Commission's July 2026 assessment outcome; it will determine whether EU ETS obligations for international flights expand beyond the current intra-EEA scope, and whether CORSIA implementation via EU ETS continues unchanged.\n\nThe European Commission \"will carry out an assessment of whether more action is required for flights to and from Europe, in line with criteria set in the EU ETS Directive\" by July 2026. *Source: European Commission, Climate Action, \"Reducing emissions from aviation,\" undated. https://climate.ec.europa.eu/eu-action/transport-decarbonisation/reducing-emissions-aviation_en.\n\n*Re-check window:* Review by 31 July 2026 for published Commission assessment output.\n\n---"
        },
