        {
          "section_id": "a6467d00-8965-4358-a9f6-31612072cc15",
          "key": "1",
          "md": "This document provides a workspace-anchored regulatory fact brief on CORSIA — the Carbon Offsetting and Reduction Scheme for International Aviation — as adopted by the International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO) under Assembly Resolution A42-22. It is written for operations where air freight is the primary transport mode, covering cargo verticals including live events, fine art, luxury goods, film and television, high-value automotive, and humanitarian freight, moving across the Americas, Europe, and Asia trade lanes.\n\n**Convention notes:**\n- Every factual claim is cited inline. Claims requiring interpretation are labeled *Analytical inference:*, *Industry interpretation:*, or *Operational implication:*. Legal determinations are routed to *Legal Confirmation Required:*.\n- Binding-verb assertions are either verbatim source quotes (FACT, cited) or carry one of the mandatory prefixed labels. No unlabeled obligation language appears anywhere in this document.\n- The workspace is referenced by role and operational profile. No company names, individual names, or proprietary product names appear.\n- Sections 5, 9, and 13 are omitted below with explanatory notes — the source pool does not provide sufficient grounded content to populate them honestly.\n- **Date of generation:** 2026-06-06.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "b554f365-baee-4487-afe7-0f6ad052110e",
          "key": "2",
          "md": "## What CORSIA Is\n\nCORSIA is described by ICAO as \"the first global market-based scheme that applies to a sector.\" *Source: ICAO CORSIA homepage, ICAO, undated. https://icao.int/environmental-protection/CORSIA/Pages/default.aspx.\n\nThe scheme's primary objective is stated as carbon neutral growth of international aviation — excluding domestic aviation. *Source: GreenAir News, \"ICAO releases first-ever growth factor for airlines' CORSIA offsetting requirements,\" GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\nCORSIA operates under ICAO Assembly Resolution A42-22 and is codified in Annex 16, Volume IV of the Chicago Convention. *Source: ICAO CORSIA homepage, ICAO, undated. https://icao.int/environmental-protection/CORSIA/Pages/default.aspx.\n\nCORSIA complements other aviation in-sector emissions reduction efforts such as \"technological innovations, operational improvements and sustainable aviation fuels to meet the ICAO aspirational goal of carbon neutral growth.\" *Source: ICAO CORSIA homepage, ICAO, undated. https://icao.int/environmental-protection/CORSIA/Pages/default.aspx.\n\n## Phase Structure\n\nCORSIA operates in three phases:\n\n- **Pilot Phase:** 2021–2023 (voluntary participation by states).\n- **First Phase:** 2024–2026 (voluntary participation; compliance deadline January 2028 for offsetting obligations arising from this phase). *Source: Gold Standard, \"First large-scale CORSIA retirement of Gold Standard carbon credits by a commercial airline,\" Gold Standard, 24 March 2026, updated 12 May 2026. https://www.goldstandard.org/news/first-large-scale-corsia-retirement-of-gold-standard-carbon-credits-by-a-commercial-airline.\n- **Second Phase:** 2027–2029 (wider mandatory participation expected). *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n## The Baseline\n\n*Analytical inference:* The baseline for the First Phase is set at 85% of total 2019 CO₂ emissions for all State pairs subject to offsetting requirements. The 2024 baseline figure was set at 305,522,071 tonnes of CO₂. Total 2024 emissions for all applicable State pairs, as reported by 126 States and estimated for 12 more, amounted to 361,159,641 tonnes. *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n*Analytical inference:* The 2024 Sector's Growth Factor was calculated as 0.15405257. *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n## Why This Applies to the Workspace\n\n*Analytical inference:* For workspaces whose primary transport mode is air freight, CORSIA is a first-order cost and compliance environment. CORSIA's obligations fall on aircraft operators (airlines), not on freight forwarders or shippers directly. However, airlines operating routes between CORSIA-participating State pairs carry offsetting cost burdens that are anticipated to pass through to freight rates and surcharges. IATA reports that \"the cost of compliance with the ICAO CORSIA carbon offsetting scheme is anticipated to grow to $1.7 billion for 2026, up from $1.3 billion for 2025.\" *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n*Operational implication:* Workspaces moving high-value, time-sensitive cargo — live events, fine art, film and television, luxury goods, high-value automotive, and humanitarian — by air across the Americas, Europe, and Asia lanes are directly exposed to CORSIA cost pass-through. The cost is not avoidable by modal shift within air; it is route- and airline-specific.\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* Whether any CORSIA-derived surcharge or fuel-related charge imposed by an airline carrier constitutes a regulated tariff element or a contractual pass-through under applicable aviation law is a legal determination requiring counsel review specific to each carrier relationship and jurisdiction.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "b5da2e5a-3d79-4527-a165-67e517a08682",
          "key": "7",
          "md": "## Decided Questions\n\n**Q1: Does CORSIA apply to domestic aviation?**\nDecided: No. The scheme's primary objective covers \"carbon neutral growth of international aviation — so excluding domestic aviation.\" *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n*Analytical inference:* **Q2: Does the First Phase offsetting requirement apply only to State pairs where both states participate?**\nDecided: Yes. \"Airlines with flights between participating countries, called State pairs, are required to offset emissions that exceed a baseline determined under ICAO Assembly Resolution A42-22.\" *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n**Q3: What is the First Phase compliance deadline for airlines?**\nDecided: January 2028. \"Airlines prepare to meet CORSIA's Phase 1 compliance deadline in January 2028.\" *Source: Gold Standard, 24 March 2026. https://www.goldstandard.org/news/first-large-scale-corsia-retirement-of-gold-standard-carbon-credits-by-a-commercial-airline.\n\n**Q4: Can airlines reduce their CORSIA offsetting requirements through SAF use?**\nDecided: Yes. \"CORSIA allows aircraft operators to reduce its offsetting requirements through the use of CORSIA eligible fuels, which include CORSIA sustainable aviation fuels and CORSIA lower carbon aviation fuels.\" *Source: ICAO CORSIA Eligible Fuels page, ICAO, November 2025. https://www.icao.int/CORSIA/corsia-eligible-fuels.\n\n**Q5: Is the workspace (as freight forwarder or cargo shipper) a direct CORSIA obligated party?**\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* Direct obligations under CORSIA fall on aircraft operators. Whether any national transposition — including EU ETS aviation provisions — creates derivative obligations on freight forwarders, shippers, or importers of record on specific routes is not determined from the source pool and requires legal review by jurisdiction.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "df37cefa-1f7c-490c-81f5-6d9d4e6906fb",
          "key": "10",
          "md": "## Direct Registration by the Workspace\n\n*Analytical inference:* As CORSIA obligations fall on aircraft operators rather than freight forwarders or cargo shippers, the workspace does not have a direct CORSIA registration or reporting obligation under the scheme as currently structured.\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* Whether any CORSIA-adjacent national regulation — including EU ETS aviation reporting requirements imposed on operators administered by EEA states — creates any secondary data-provision obligation on freight forwarders or shippers on covered routes is not determinable from the source pool. Legal review recommended for each jurisdiction where the workspace acts as shipper or forwarder of record.\n\n## Airline MRV and the CCR\n\n*Analytical inference:* Aircraft operators report to national aviation authorities, which submit data through the CORSIA Central Registry. \"Open sharing of data supports accountability and offers all stakeholders a clear view of collective progress for the robust implementation of the scheme.\" *Source: GreenAir News, 18 December 2025. https://www.greenairnews.com/?p=8325.\n\n*Operational implication:* Publicly available CCR data enables workspaces to cross-reference the offsetting volumes of carriers used on key lanes — a useful tool for commercial benchmarking and client sustainability reporting.\n\n## EU ETS Aviation MRV: Non-CO₂ Effects\n\nThe European Commission is establishing \"an MRV system for non-CO₂ aviation effects to apply from 1st January 2025, calculating CO₂ equivalent per flight through state-of-art approaches using flight information, aircraft and fuel properties, performance information and weather data. By the end of 2027, the Commission will deliver a report on the results and if appropriate, will make a legislative proposal to address non-CO₂ effects of aviation.\" *Source: European Commission, \"Reducing emissions from aviation,\" undated. https://climate.ec.europa.eu/eu-action/transport-decarbonisation/reducing-emissions-aviation_en.\n\n*Analytical inference:* If the 2027 report leads to a legislative proposal adding non-CO₂ effects to EU ETS obligations, airline cost burdens on EEA routes will increase beyond current CORSIA-only projections.\n\n---"
        }
