          "section_id": "1bd91f10-f34e-4cec-8111-0c689d3e64f1",
          "key": "10",
          "md": "### EV Registration Annual Fee — Commercial Fleets\n\nThe AFDC source states the $214.50 EV fee and $107.25 PHEV fee apply \"at the time of initial registration and annual registration renewal.\" *Source: AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina, U.S. Department of Energy AFDC. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc *Analytical inference:* Workspaces operating ZEV fleets registered in North Carolina should incorporate this fee line into vehicle total-cost-of-ownership calculations. At fleet scale (e.g., 50 ZEVs), annual registration fee exposure rises to approximately $10,725 annually for pure EVs — a quantifiable, predictable cost not present in ICE fleet registration.\n\n### NCDOT Performance Dashboard Reporting\n\nThe AFDC source states NCDOT reports EV, hybrid, and PHEV registrations on its performance dashboard monthly. *Source: AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina, U.S. Department of Energy AFDC. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc This is a state-agency reporting obligation, not a commercial-operator requirement.\n\n### DEQ Grant Program Reporting\n\nThe AFDC source notes DEQ's Level 2 Infrastructure Grant Program and Zero Emission Vehicle Direct Current Fast Charge Infrastructure Program provide funding for EV charging installation. *Source: AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina, U.S. Department of Energy AFDC. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc Grant recipients would be subject to program-specific reporting obligations. *Legal Confirmation Required:* specific reporting conditions for DEQ EV charging grant recipients require program review.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "1d3434ba-5a1e-4b18-8c25-690e0dc48121",
          "key": "12",
          "md": "### Alternative Fuel Tax Exemption\n\nThe AFDC source states: \"The retail sale, use, storage, and consumption of alternative fuels is exempt from the state retail sales and use tax.\" *Source: AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina, U.S. Department of Energy AFDC. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc *Analytical inference:* This exemption applies to electricity purchased for vehicle charging purposes. Workspaces operating depot charging facilities may benefit from this exemption on electricity consumed for fleet charging, reducing per-mile energy cost compared to a taxed-electricity scenario. *Legal Confirmation Required:* whether the exemption applies to electricity purchased at commercial depot charging installations (as distinct from retail public charging sales) requires tax counsel review.\n\n### EV and FCEV Emissions Inspection Exemption\n\nThe AFDC hydrogen source states: \"Qualified light-duty EVs and FCEVs are exempt from state emissions inspection requirements.\" *Source: AFDC Hydrogen Laws and Incentives in North Carolina, U.S. Department of Energy AFDC. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/HY?state=nc *Analytical inference:* This is an operating cost reduction for light-duty ZEV fleet operators in North Carolina — annual emissions inspection fees are eliminated per vehicle.\n\n### ACT Rule Off-Road Exclusion\n\nThe National Law Review source states: \"The ACT would not apply to off-road vehicles, such as farm equipment or construction vehicles.\" *Source: National Law Review — NC Executive Order 271 Analysis, Womble Bond Dickinson (US) LLP, November 29, 2022. https://natlawreview.com/article/new-north-carolina-executive-order-aims-to-increase-percentage-zero-emission-trucks *Analytical inference:* Workspaces operating specialized off-road cargo-handling equipment (e.g., ground support equipment, warehouse forklifts, terminal tractors) would not have been covered by the ACT rule even if enacted. This carve-out is relevant for operations at ports, airports, and logistics facilities.\n\n### EV Charging Station Public Utility Exclusion\n\nThe AFDC source states a charging station operator \"is not considered a public utility. Conditions apply.\" *Source: AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina, U.S. Department of Energy AFDC. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc *Analytical inference:* Workspaces that install and operate semi-public charging at freight facilities can resell electricity to driver or client vehicles without triggering public utility regulation, subject to the stated conditions. *Legal Confirmation Required:* the specific conditions and their application to commercial freight facility charging require legal counsel review.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "1ef53168-f18b-4dcd-ab11-871b19a73ebc",
          "key": "6",
          "md": "### Advanced Clean Trucks Rule — Status Uncertain MONITORING\n\n*Analytical inference:* EO 271 (October 25, 2022) directed DEQ to propose an ACT rule by May 15, 2023, per the National Law Review source: \"The NCDEQ is directed to submit rules for the ACT program no later than May 15, 2023.\" *Source: National Law Review — NC Executive Order 271 Analysis, Womble Bond Dickinson (US) LLP, November 29, 2022. https://natlawreview.com/article/new-north-carolina-executive-order-aims-to-increase-percentage-zero-emission-trucks However, as noted above, the Governor's Office source states \"In 2023, the General Assembly prohibited the Department of Environmental Quality from moving forward with Advanced Clean Trucks.\" *Source: Governor's Office Climate Page, NC Governor's Office. https://governor.nc.gov/issues/climate\n\n*Analytical inference:* The ACT rule — if reinstated — would have set manufacturer ZEV sales share requirements: the National Law Review source states \"manufacturers would be responsible for offering zero-emission vehicles sales shares of 10 – 13 percent in 2026, increasing to 40 – 75 percent in 2035, depending on vehicle size.\" *Source: National Law Review — NC Executive Order 271 Analysis, Womble Bond Dickinson (US) LLP, November 29, 2022. https://natlawreview.com/article/new-north-carolina-executive-order-aims-to-increase-percentage-zero-emission-trucks\n\n*Analytical inference:* Legislative override creates a re-activation risk: if a future gubernatorial or legislative action reinstates ACT, the vehicle availability and pricing implications for commercial fleet operators could materialize rapidly. Workspaces with long fleet replacement cycles (5–10 years) should monitor this closely. Re-check window: every NC General Assembly regular session (odd-year long sessions and even-year short sessions).\n\n### NEVI Phase 2 — Community Charging\n\nThe Advanced Energy source states: \"After the corridor infrastructure is developed, the NEVI funds can be used to support community charging.\" *Source: Advanced Energy NC Electric Transportation Transition, Advanced Energy, August 28, 2023. https://www.advancedenergy.org/news/north-carolinas-electric-transportation-transition Phase 1 corridor deployment is underway. Community charging deployment follows corridor completion. *Analytical inference:* Based on NEVI program structure, Phase 2 community charging is likely to become available for application in the 2025–2027 timeframe, based on FHWA's phased NEVI rollout nationally. Specific NC timing: stated schedule not published in source pool; estimate is labeled as analytical inference.\n\n### ZEV Infrastructure Needs Assessment\n\nThe AFDC source states: \"The North Carolina Department of Transportation must establish a North Carolina ZEV Infrastructure Needs Assessment in partnership with other agencies. The assessment must evaluate charging and fueling needs to support the adoption and implementation of the California Advanced Clean Trucks program and the achievement of state ZEV adoption and greenhouse gas emissions goals.\" *Source: AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina, U.S. Department of Energy AFDC. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc The Advanced Energy source notes this assessment was \"due in September 2023.\" *Source: Advanced Energy NC Electric Transportation Transition, Advanced Energy, August 28, 2023. https://www.advancedenergy.org/news/north-carolinas-electric-transportation-transition Whether the final assessment was published and its findings are not confirmed in the source pool. Research gap: the completed ZEV Infrastructure Needs Assessment document should be retrieved from NCDOT.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "22ffaf1e-d50e-40c3-b872-8d1a99f9de63",
          "key": "record_facts",
          "md": "*Analytical inference:* [jurisdictional_scope] No verbatim jurisdictional scope statement was located in the captured source text for this record-grade item. A full-brief regrounding will re-examine this gap when this item upgrades from record to brief."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "2b69bf5e-87fd-40e5-a638-9bb6fdf65ac7",
          "key": "14",
          "md": "| Date | Milestone | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| **October 29, 2018** | EO 80 issued. 40% GHG reduction by 2025 target established; NCDOT directed to develop ZEV Plan. | In force as of that date. |\n| **July 15, 2020** | North Carolina joined Multi-State MHD ZEV MOU. | In force as of that date. *Source: NCDOT Transportation Emission Reduction Projects Page. https://www.ncdot.gov/initiatives-policies/environmental/climate-change/Pages/electric-vehicles.aspx |\n| **September 2019** | NCDOT published Zero Emission Vehicle (ZEV) Plan. | Published as of that date. |\n| **January 7, 2022** | EO 246 issued. 50% GHG reduction by 2030, 1,250,000 ZEVs by 2030, net-zero by 2050 targets established; NCDOT directed to develop Clean Transportation Plan. | In force as of that date. *Source: NCDOT Clean Transportation Plan Page. https://www.ncdot.gov/initiatives-policies/environmental/climate-change/Pages/clean-transportation-plan.aspx |\n| **July 2022** | Multi-State MHD ZEV Task Force published multi-state action plan. | Published. *Source: AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc |\n| **October 25, 2022** | EO 271 issued; DEQ directed to propose ACT rule to Environmental Management Commission; ZEV Infrastructure Needs Assessment directed. | In force as of that date. *Source: Governor's Office Climate Page. https://governor.nc.gov/issues/climate |\n| **April 7, 2023** | NCDOT deadline to submit Clean Transportation Plan to Governor. | Plan released April 2023. *Source: AFDC Clean Transportation Plan Entry. https://afdc.energy.gov/laws/12830 |\n| **2023** | General Assembly prohibited DEQ from proceeding with Advanced Clean Trucks rulemaking. | In effect. ACT rule not in force. *Source: Governor's Office Climate Page. https://governor.nc.gov/issues/climate |\n| **March 28, 2024** | Governor Cooper announced EO 80 ZEV goal (80,000 registered ZEVs) achieved two years early. | Achieved. *Source: Governor's Office Climate Page. https://governor.nc.gov/issues/climate |\n| **2030 (target)** | EO 246 targets due: 50% GHG reduction below 2005 levels; 1,250,000 registered ZEVs; 50% of new vehicle sales ZEVs. | Active targets. As of August 2023, approximately 66,000 ZEVs registered against 1.25M target. *Source: Advanced Energy. https://www.advancedenergy.org/news/north-carolinas-electric-transportation-transition |\n| **2050 (target)** | Net-zero emissions statewide. | Long-horizon target under EO 246. *Source: Governor's Office Climate Page. https://governor.nc.gov/issues/climate |\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "48c09c3a-9fd8-40dd-9eb8-607f7e5c0580",
          "key": "3",
          "md": "**MONITORING — Advanced Clean Trucks Rulemaking Status Confirmation**\nThe ACT rulemaking was directed by EO 271 (October 25, 2022) and DEQ was charged to propose rules to the Environmental Management Commission. However, the sources note: \"In 2023, the General Assembly prohibited the Department of Environmental Quality from moving forward with Advanced Clean Trucks.\" *Source: Governor's Office Climate Page, NC Governor's Office. https://governor.nc.gov/issues/climate Workspaces operating medium- and heavy-duty vehicle fleets in North Carolina should confirm the current legal status of the ACT rule with NC DEQ before making fleet procurement decisions premised on the ACT structure. Re-check window: Q3 2025 legislative session outcomes.\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* whether the General Assembly prohibition fully extinguishes DEQ's ACT rulemaking authority or only suspends it pending legislative action requires legal counsel review.\n\n**MONITORING — EO 80 ZEV Goal Achievement and Post-2025 State Fleet Procurement**\nThe source states: \"On March 28, 2024, Governor Cooper announced that North Carolina had achieved Executive Order 80's zero-emission vehicle goals two years early.\" *Source: Governor's Office Climate Page, NC Governor's Office. https://governor.nc.gov/issues/climate The EO 246 goal of 1,250,000 registered ZEVs by 2030 is the active target. The Advanced Energy source notes North Carolina was home to \"about 66,000\" ZEVs as of August 2023, against a 1.25 million target. *Source: Advanced Energy NC Electric Transportation Transition, Advanced Energy, August 28, 2023. https://www.advancedenergy.org/news/north-carolinas-electric-transportation-transition The gap is substantial. Workspaces with state-agency clients or public-sector contract dependencies should monitor state fleet electrification progress, as state agency ZEV procurement commitments directly affect vehicle availability on the statewide term contract.\n\n**MONITORING — NEVI Charging Infrastructure Deployment Along Freight Corridors**\nThe Advanced Energy source states NCDOT proposed 39 stations meeting NEVI requirements, with DC fast charging stations located \"no more than one mile from Alternative Fuel Corridors and no more than 50 miles apart from each other. Stations must contain at least four charging ports each capable of delivering 150 kilowatts simultaneously.\" *Source: Advanced Energy NC Electric Transportation Transition, Advanced Energy, August 28, 2023. https://www.advancedenergy.org/news/north-carolinas-electric-transportation-transition Workspaces operating road freight through North Carolina should track NEVI corridor deployment progress to inform EV-capable equipment procurement timing on I-40, I-85, and I-77 corridors.\n\n**COMPETITIVE EDGE — State Fleet ZEV Term Contract Access**\nThe DOA source states the statewide term contract \"2613A – Electric Vehicle Charging Station Equipment, Accessories, Installation, and Infrastructure\" was updated to include six vendors with approximately 17 different brands of charging stations. State-contracted vehicles include Ford Mustang Mach-E, F-150 Lightning, E-Transit 350 Cargo Van, and Chevrolet Blazer EV. *Source: NC DOA Zero-Emission Vehicles Page, NC Department of Administration. https://www.doa.nc.gov/about/sustainability/zero-emission-vehicles Workspaces managing state-contracted fleet operations can access these procurement vehicles. The state contract provides a reference pricing benchmark for ZEV acquisition planning.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "517d54e5-c6d0-4fed-8f4b-98774dc1d487",
          "key": "13",
          "md": "### Electric Transport Refrigeration Units (eTRUs)\n\nThe Advanced Energy source notes a pilot in North Carolina \"examining electric transport refrigeration units. This is a hybrid technology, in which the refrigeration unit runs on diesel when on the road but on electricity, by plugging in, when stationed and idling at distribution centers. This electric application reduces emissions, noise and costs for operators.\" *Source: Advanced Energy NC Electric Transportation Transition, Advanced Energy, August 28, 2023. https://www.advancedenergy.org/news/north-carolinas-electric-transportation-transition *Analytical inference:* For workspaces operating temperature-controlled freight in North Carolina (pharmaceuticals, food, biological samples), eTRU electrification at depot dwell points is a near-term emissions reduction option that does not require full truck electrification — it addresses the idling emissions component, which is a significant fraction of distribution-center air quality impact.\n\n### Carbon Reduction Program — Road Freight Funding\n\nThe NCDOT source states: \"The Carbon Reduction program provides funds for projects that reduce carbon dioxide emissions from various modes of transportation. Sixty-five percent of the state's distribution is obligated to projects based on population. The remaining 35 percent can be disbursed to projects anywhere throughout the state.\" *Source: NCDOT Transportation Emission Reduction Projects Page, NCDOT, updated January 6, 2025. https://www.ncdot.gov/initiatives-policies/environmental/climate-change/Pages/electric-vehicles.aspx *Analytical inference:* Workspaces operating freight services in lower-population areas of North Carolina should note that 35% of Carbon Reduction Program funds are available statewide — this is a potential funding mechanism for rural depot charging or alternative fuel infrastructure that would not be prioritized under the population-weighted allocation.\n\n### Diesel Emissions Reduction Act Funding\n\nThe NCDOT Clean Transportation Plan source lists the Diesel Emissions Reduction Act as a funding stream that \"can ensure existing dollars do as much as possible to support the clean transportation transition.\" *Source: NCDOT N.C. Clean Transportation Plan Page, NCDOT. https://www.ncdot.gov/initiatives-policies/environmental/climate-change/Pages/clean-transportation-plan.aspx *Analytical inference:* Workspaces operating diesel fleets in North Carolina may be eligible for DERA funding for fleet upgrades (engine retrofits, DPF installations, or vehicle replacement). This is a cost-mitigation pathway during the transition period before ZEV options become commercially viable for heavy freight.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "73a75e94-3d73-4ce1-bc6a-f0b7eabc67eb",
          "key": "2",
          "md": "### What the Directives Are\n\n**Executive Order 80** — issued October 29, 2018 — the source states: \"Governor Cooper issued Executive Order 80, which directed bold action on climate change and the transition to a clean energy economy.\" EO 80 established goals to reduce statewide greenhouse gas emissions to 40% below 2005 levels by 2025, increase registered zero-emission vehicles to at least 80,000, and reduce energy consumption in state-owned buildings by 40% from FY 2002–2003 levels. It directed NCDOT to develop a Zero Emission Vehicles Plan and DEQ to develop a Clean Energy Plan. *Source: Governor's Office Climate Page, NC Governor's Office. https://governor.nc.gov/issues/climate\n\n**Executive Order 246** — issued January 7, 2022 — the source states: \"Executive Order 246 established new goals for North Carolina to: Reduce statewide greenhouse gas emissions by 50% below 2005 levels by 2030 and achieve net-zero emissions as soon as possible, but no later than 2050; and Increase the total number of registered, zero-emission vehicles to at least 1,250,000 by 2030 and increase the sale of zero-emission vehicles so that 50 percent of in-state sales of new vehicles are zero-emission by 2030.\" *Source: Governor's Office Climate Page, NC Governor's Office. https://governor.nc.gov/issues/climate\n\nThe AFDC corroborates the EO 246 ZEV goal: \"GHG emissions must be at least 50% below 2005 levels by 2030; Achieve net-zero emissions by 2050; Register at least 1,250,000 ZEVs by 2030; and, Increase the share of new passenger vehicle sales to 50% ZEVs by 2030.\" *Source: AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina, U.S. Department of Energy AFDC. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc\n\n### Transportation Sector Baseline\n\nThe source states: \"In 2018, North Carolina's transportation sector contributed 36 percent of the state's total greenhouse gas emissions.\" *Source: NCDOT Transportation Emission Reduction Projects Page, NCDOT, updated January 6, 2025. https://www.ncdot.gov/initiatives-policies/environmental/climate-change/Pages/electric-vehicles.aspx\n\n### Why It Applies to the Workspace\n\n*Analytical inference:* Workspaces operating commercial vehicle fleets in North Carolina — including road freight carriers, last-mile delivery operators, intermodal drayage providers, and freight forwarders managing client fleets — sit directly in the trajectory of these directives. EO 80 and EO 246 have produced concrete downstream instruments (the Clean Transportation Plan, the Advanced Clean Trucks rulemaking, the NEVI program, and the Multi-State MHD ZEV MOU) that directly govern the commercial vehicle equipment market in North Carolina. While state-agency fleet obligations are direct, the commercial freight sector faces market-structure consequences: vehicle availability constraints, charging infrastructure maturation timelines, and client ESG reporting requirements that flow from the EO framework.\n\n*Analytical inference:* For workspaces moving time-sensitive cargo (live events, film and TV, humanitarian) by road through North Carolina, the medium- and heavy-duty electrification trajectory directly affects fleet refresh planning windows and depot infrastructure decisions on corridors including I-40, I-85, and I-77.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "75848be2-cbf7-40f7-a353-e0064d9343dc",
          "key": "5",
          "md": "### The N.C. Clean Transportation Plan (Released April 2023)\n\nThe NCDOT source states: \"The N.C. Clean Transportation Plan is a guidance document that provides a coordinated strategy for accelerating decarbonization in the transportation sector. The plan outlines how North Carolina can prepare for a clean transportation future and provide equitable outcomes for everyone.\" *Source: NCDOT N.C. Clean Transportation Plan Page, NCDOT. https://www.ncdot.gov/initiatives-policies/environmental/climate-change/Pages/clean-transportation-plan.aspx\n\nThe plan was co-created with local, regional and state agencies, transportation providers, nonprofit organizations, social justice and equity-focused groups, environmentally focused groups, academic partners, clean cities coalitions, advocacy groups, utility providers, and private companies. *Source: NCDOT N.C. Clean Transportation Plan Page, NCDOT. https://www.ncdot.gov/initiatives-policies/environmental/climate-change/Pages/clean-transportation-plan.aspx\n\n*Analytical inference:* The GovTech source elaborates on key recommendations: NCDOT should form \"a dedicated team to help the state create a transportation system that relies less on fossil fuels\"; create \"a Clean Transportation Interagency Task Force, including representatives from the state Commerce, Environmental Quality and Health and Human Services departments, to coordinate efforts to carry out the plan and oversee the electrification of state cars, trucks and buses\"; and \"review how it prioritizes transportation projects to take into account greenhouse gas emissions.\" *Source: GovTech North Carolina DOT Outlines Plan for Cutting Emissions, GovTech/Charlotte Observer, March 2, 2023. https://www.govtech.com/fs/north-carolina-dot-outlines-plan-for-cutting-emissions\n\nThe AFDC Clean Transportation Plan entry states the plan was enacted January 7, 2022 (EO 246 date) and covers EVs and Hydrogen Fuel Cells. *Source: AFDC Clean Transportation Plan Entry, U.S. Department of Energy AFDC. https://afdc.energy.gov/laws/12830\n\n**Section-by-section application to workspace:**\n\n- **Governance recommendations:** The plan calls for a dedicated clean transportation team at NCDOT and an interagency task force. *Analytical inference:* Workspaces seeking to engage with state transportation procurement or influence charging infrastructure siting on freight corridors should identify NCDOT's clean transportation team as the primary engagement point. The statewide initiatives senior engineer position (filled as of August 2023 per Advanced Energy source) is the operational contact.\n\n- **Funding/Finance recommendations:** The plan directs NCDOT to maximize use of the Congestion Mitigation and Air Quality program, the Carbon Reduction Program, and the Diesel Emissions Reduction Act. *Source: NCDOT N.C. Clean Transportation Plan Page, NCDOT. https://www.ncdot.gov/initiatives-policies/environmental/climate-change/Pages/clean-transportation-plan.aspx *Analytical inference:* Workspaces operating diesel fleets in North Carolina may have access to Diesel Emissions Reduction Act funding for fleet upgrades — this is a procurement opportunity, not a compliance obligation.\n\n- **Infrastructure recommendations:** The plan directs evaluation and deployment of \"clean transportation infrastructure to support all types of fleet vehicles and applications.\" *Source: NCDOT N.C. Clean Transportation Plan Page, NCDOT. https://www.ncdot.gov/initiatives-policies/environmental/climate-change/Pages/clean-transportation-plan.aspx *Analytical inference:* For workspaces with depot operations in North Carolina, this signals that charging infrastructure grant programs will be available to support depot electrification.\n\n- **Medium- and Heavy-Duty ZEV infrastructure:** The Advanced Energy source states NCDOT anticipates needing \"25,000 Level 2 and 30,000 DC fast chargers by 2030, and 100,000 Level 2 and 60,000 DC fast chargers by 2035\" to meet ACT program requirements. *Source: Advanced Energy NC Electric Transportation Transition, Advanced Energy, August 28, 2023. https://www.advancedenergy.org/news/north-carolinas-electric-transportation-transition The current infrastructure is substantially below this level, representing a planning gap for freight operators.\n\n### Multi-State MHD ZEV Memorandum of Understanding\n\nThe AFDC source states North Carolina is a signatory to a multi-state MOU on MHD ZEV deployment: \"the action plan includes strategies and recommendations to accomplish the goals of the MOU, including limiting all new MHD vehicle sales in the signatory states to ZEVs by 2050, accelerating the deployment of MHD ZEVs, and ensuring MHD ZEV deployment also benefits disadvantaged communities.\" *Source: AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina, U.S. Department of Energy AFDC. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc The action plan was published in July 2022.\n\n*Analytical inference:* For workspaces operating medium- and heavy-duty vehicle fleets on routes through multiple MOU signatory states (including Virginia, Maryland, and New York alongside North Carolina), the long-horizon implication is that fleet electrification is a multi-state trajectory, not just a North Carolina-specific signal.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "a8623197-42c4-4837-86d0-c61f4787dd56",
          "key": "7",
          "md": "| Question | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Do EO 80/246 directly impose obligations on private commercial freight operators? | *Legal Confirmation Required:* EOs are directed at state agencies; private-sector implications flow through downstream regulatory instruments. |\n| Is the Advanced Clean Trucks rule in force in North Carolina? | FACT: General Assembly prohibited DEQ from proceeding (2023). Rule not in force as of this brief. *Source: Governor's Office Climate Page. https://governor.nc.gov/issues/climate |\n| Are the 1,250,000 ZEV and 50% new-vehicle-sales targets legally binding on private parties? | *Legal Confirmation Required:* These are stated as goals in EO 246; their enforceability on private market actors (vs. state agencies) requires legal counsel review. |\n| Does the Multi-State MHD ZEV MOU create enforceable obligations on commercial operators? | *Legal Confirmation Required:* The MOU is a state-level policy commitment; legal force on commercial operators in member states requires counsel review. |\n| Does the EV annual fee apply to commercially registered ZEVs? | FACT: \"The owner of an EV that is exclusively powered by electricity must pay a fee of $214.50 and the owner of a plug-in hybrid electric vehicle must pay a fee of $107.25 in addition to any other required registration fees at the time of initial registration and annual registration renewal.\" *Source: AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc *Legal Confirmation Required:* Whether this applies to commercially registered fleet vehicles requires DMV confirmation. |\n| Can EV charging station operators resell electricity without becoming a public utility? | FACT: \"A person who uses electric vehicle supply equipment to resell electricity to the public for the purposes of fueling an electric vehicle is not considered a public utility. Conditions apply.\" *Source: AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc Conditions require legal review. |\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "bc391f54-7684-419f-9e99-53b38fe202eb",
          "key": "15",
          "md": "*Analytical inference:* | # | Title | Type | URL |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Governor's Office Climate Page | Regulator/Government (Tier 2) | https://governor.nc.gov/issues/climate |\n| 2 | NCDOT N.C. Clean Transportation Plan Page | Regulator/Government (Tier 2) | https://www.ncdot.gov/initiatives-policies/environmental/climate-change/Pages/clean-transportation-plan.aspx |\n| 3 | AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina | Government Agency (Tier 2) | https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc |\n| 4 | AFDC Hydrogen Laws and Incentives in North Carolina | Government Agency (Tier 2) | https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/HY?state=nc |\n| 5 | AFDC Clean Transportation Plan Entry | Government Agency (Tier 2) | https://afdc.energy.gov/laws/12830 |\n| 6 | Advanced Energy NC Electric Transportation Transition | Industry/Nonprofit (Tier 4) | https://www.advancedenergy.org/news/north-carolinas-electric-transportation-transition |\n| 7 | NCDOT Transportation Emission Reduction Projects Page | Government Agency (Tier 2) | https://www.ncdot.gov/initiatives-policies/environmental/climate-change/Pages/electric-vehicles.aspx |\n| 8 | NC DOA Zero-Emission Vehicles Page | Government Agency (Tier 2) | https://www.doa.nc.gov/about/sustainability/zero-emission-vehicles |\n| 9 | NCDOT Climate Change and Clean Transportation Page | Government Agency (Tier 2) | https://ncdot.gov/initiatives-policies/environmental/climate-change/Pages/default.aspx |\n| 10 | National Law Review — NC EO 271 Analysis | Legal industry analysis (Tier 5) | https://natlawreview.com/article/new-north-carolina-executive-order-aims-to-increase-percentage-zero-emission-trucks |\n| 11 | GovTech — North Carolina DOT Outlines Plan for Cutting Emissions | News (Tier 5) | https://www.govtech.com/fs/north-carolina-dot-outlines-plan-for-cutting-emissions |\n| 12 | NCDOT Statewide Freight Plan | Government Agency (Tier 2) | https://connect.ncdot.gov/projects/planning/Statewide-Freight-Plan/Pages/default.aspx |\n| 13 | DEQ Advanced Clean Trucks Final Stakeholder Webinar | Government Agency (Tier 2) | https://www.deq.nc.gov/news/events/advanced-clean-trucks-final-stakeholder-webinar |\n| 14 | Southern Alliance for Clean Energy — EO 246 Analysis | Advocacy/NGO (Tier 6) | https://cleanenergy.org/news/governor-coopers-executive-order-commits-nc-to-address-climate-change-and-build-an-equitable-clean-energy-and-clean-transportation-economy/ |\n| 15 | Heavy Duty Trucking — NC Adopts Advanced Clean Trucks Rule | Industry news (Tier 5) | https://www.truckinginfo.com/10185644/north-carolina-adopts-advanced-clean-trucks-rule |\n| 16 | Sierra Club NC — NC's Clean Transportation Future | Advocacy (Tier 6) | https://www.sierraclub.org/north-carolina/blog/2022/03/weiss-ncs-clean-transportation-future-must-include-all |\n\n---"
