        {
          "section_id": "d0e57251-c4ac-4f26-a2e6-bdab2e665370",
          "key": "4",
          "md": "EO 80 and EO 246 are structured as directives to state agencies, not as statutory obligations on private commercial entities. The compliance chain has two distinct layers:\n\n**Layer 1 — Direct state agency obligations.** The AFDC source states: \"State-owned vehicle fleets must prioritize ZEVs in the purchase or lease of new vehicles and use ZEVs for agency travel when feasible. Cabinet agencies must prioritize ZEVs in the purchase or lease of new medium- and heavy-duty (MHD) vehicles with a gross vehicle weight rating greater than 8,500 pounds.\" *Source: AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina, U.S. Department of Energy AFDC. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc This obligation falls on state agencies, not private operators.\n\n**Layer 2 — Commercial sector market structure effects.** The AFDC source states: \"North Carolina established a goal that at least 75% of new or replacement state government light-duty cars and trucks with a gross vehicle weight rating of 8,500 pounds or less must be AFVs or low emission vehicles.\" *Source: AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina, U.S. Department of Energy AFDC. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc This is a state government goal, not a commercial mandate.\n\n*Analytical inference:* Workspaces in the role of freight carrier or fleet operator in North Carolina are not directly obligated by EO 80 or EO 246. Their compliance-chain position is as commercial market participants affected by (a) the state's downstream regulatory instruments (ACT rule, NEVI deployment, MHD ZEV MOU), (b) client ESG requirements that cite EO 246 targets, and (c) equipment availability shifts driven by manufacturer sales targets that may emerge from the ACT framework if reinstated.\n\n*Analytical inference:* Workspaces in the role of state contractor providing transportation services to NC agencies face indirect exposure — state agencies prioritizing ZEV use may require ZEV-capable transport options in future procurement specifications.\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* The extent to which state transportation procurement contracts will incorporate EO 246 ZEV requirements as contract conditions, and whether private carriers delivering to state facilities face fleet-composition requirements, requires legal counsel review.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "e8ff42ba-c303-4f12-b656-d6d148fa57ee",
          "key": "8",
          "md": "### State Agency Fleet Requirements (Direct Obligations on State Entities)\n\nThe AFDC source states these requirements apply to state-owned fleets:\n\n**ZEV prioritization:** \"State-owned vehicle fleets must prioritize ZEVs in the purchase or lease of new vehicles and use ZEVs for agency travel when feasible. Cabinet agencies must prioritize ZEVs in the purchase or lease of new medium- and heavy-duty (MHD) vehicles with a gross vehicle weight rating greater than 8,500 pounds.\" *Source: AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina, U.S. Department of Energy AFDC. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc\n\n**75% AFV acquisition goal:** \"North Carolina established a goal that at least 75% of new or replacement state government light-duty cars and trucks with a gross vehicle weight rating of 8,500 pounds or less must be AFVs or low emission vehicles.\" *Source: AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina, U.S. Department of Energy AFDC. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc\n\n**Petroleum displacement planning:** \"State-owned vehicle fleets must implement petroleum displacement plans to increase the use of alternative fuels and fuel-efficient vehicles.\" *Source: AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina, U.S. Department of Energy AFDC. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc\n\n**Motor Fleet ZEV Plan:** The Department of Administration developed the NC Motor Fleet ZEV Plan, which \"identifies the types of trips for which ZEV-use is feasible, recommends infrastructure necessary to support ZEV use, and develops ZEV procurement options and strategies.\" *Source: AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina, U.S. Department of Energy AFDC. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc\n\n### DOA Current Fleet Status\n\nThe DOA source states: \"The current ZEV fleet assigned to state agencies is 79 vehicles strong and composed of Chevy Bolts, Ford E-Transit Vans and a Ford Lightning.\" *Source: NC DOA Zero-Emission Vehicles Page, NC Department of Administration. https://www.doa.nc.gov/about/sustainability/zero-emission-vehicles This is substantially below the scale implied by the 75% AFV acquisition goal, signaling ongoing procurement pressure.\n\n### Clean Transportation Plan — NCDOT Obligations\n\nThe AFDC entry states: \"The North Carolina Department of Transportation (DOT) must develop and submit a state-wide Clean Transportation Plan (Plan) by April 7, 2023.\" *Source: AFDC Clean Transportation Plan Entry, U.S. Department of Energy AFDC. https://afdc.energy.gov/laws/12830 The plan was released in April 2023 per multiple sources, satisfying this requirement.\n\n### EV Registration Tracking\n\nThe AFDC source states: \"The North Carolina Department of Transportation (NCDOT) will report EV, hybrid electric vehicle, and plug-in hybrid electric vehicle registrations on its performance dashboard. Data must include the number of monthly new registrations, monthly registration renewals, and the cumulative number of new registrations.\" *Source: AFDC Electricity Laws and Incentives in North Carolina, U.S. Department of Energy AFDC. https://afdc.energy.gov/fuels/laws/ELEC?state=nc\n\n### Penalty / Enforcement Provision\n\n*Specific penalty provision not available from primary sources as of June 2025.* Executive orders in North Carolina direct state agencies and do not contain the same civil penalty structures as statutes. No penalty provision for non-compliance by state agencies with EO 80 or EO 246 is identified in the source pool. No penalty applicable to private-sector entities under these EOs has been identified in the source pool. *Legal Confirmation Required:* the enforcement mechanism for state agency non-compliance with EO directives and any commercial-sector liability requires legal counsel review.\n\n---\n\n*No content for Product-Specific Compliance Status as of June 2025: EO 80 and EO 246 do not establish product-level compliance requirements applicable to workspace products. The directives are fleet and emissions-target instruments, not product standards.*\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "ea73cd29-4df9-486e-9ff9-3e6d9169a537",
          "key": "1",
          "md": "This brief analyzes Executive Order 80 (issued October 29, 2018) and Executive Order 246 (issued January 7, 2022) as they apply to the transportation sector in North Carolina, with particular focus on the freight, fleet, and commercial vehicle dimensions of implementation. The document synthesizes across the NC Governor's Office, NCDOT, AFDC, NC DEQ, and industry sources cited in the source pool.\n\n**Scope convention:** Executive Orders in North Carolina are gubernatorial directives that bind state agencies. They do not constitute statutes and do not directly impose enforceable penalties on private-sector operators in the manner of enacted law. The analysis below distinguishes between state-agency obligations (which are clear from the EO texts) and commercial-sector implications (which are regulatory trajectory and procurement signals, labeled accordingly). *Legal Confirmation Required:* whether specific EO provisions impose enforceable obligations on private freight operators, carriers, or logistics service providers requires legal counsel review.\n\n**Jurisdictional scope:** North Carolina statewide. The directives apply to state agencies and direct state fleet operations. Market-signal effects extend to commercial carriers, freight operators, and vehicle manufacturers operating in the state.\n\n**Required-slot disclosure:** The \"penalty\" slot for executive orders of this type is addressed explicitly — see Section 8 and the gap label below.\n\n*Source: Governor's Office Climate Page, NC Governor's Office, accessed 2025. https://governor.nc.gov/issues/climate\n*Source: NCDOT Climate Change and Clean Transportation Page, NCDOT, updated January 6, 2025. https://ncdot.gov/initiatives-policies/environmental/climate-change/Pages/default.aspx\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "feffd441-20e3-42c2-8af4-92a427da9fde",
          "key": "11",
          "md": "### Charging Infrastructure Planning for Freight Depots\n\n*Analytical inference:* The NCDOT infrastructure projections — \"25,000 Level 2 and 30,000 DC fast chargers by 2030, and 100,000 Level 2 and 60,000 DC fast chargers by 2035\" for the MHD sector — represent the public charging network. Workspaces operating road freight depots in North Carolina should not assume public charging will be adequate for operational requirements. The Advanced Energy source notes that in the MHD sector, \"just-in-time delivery that transporters must meet\" means \"guaranteed charging at specific times and places, suggesting that larger-scale depot charging would likely be preferred over public charging.\" *Source: Advanced Energy NC Electric Transportation Transition, Advanced Energy, August 28, 2023. https://www.advancedenergy.org/news/north-carolinas-electric-transportation-transition\n\n*Operational implication:* Workspaces planning freight depot electrification in North Carolina should initiate utility grid capacity assessments before committing to ZEV fleet purchase. Duke Energy's Charger Prep Credit program \"helps customers fund behind-the-meter electrical infrastructure, such as panel upgrades and new conduit.\" The Advanced Energy source states the program \"has already served more than 4,000 homes\" with applicability to \"public, fleet, multifamily and workplace charging.\" *Source: Advanced Energy NC Electric Transportation Transition, Advanced Energy, August 28, 2023. https://www.advancedenergy.org/news/north-carolinas-electric-transportation-transition\n\n### NEVI Corridor Integration — Route Planning\n\n*Analytical inference:* NEVI Phase 1 is building out DC fast charging along Alternative Fuel Corridors in North Carolina. The Advanced Energy source states stations are located \"no more than one mile from Alternative Fuel Corridors and no more than 50 miles apart from each other. Stations must contain at least four charging ports each capable of delivering 150 kilowatts simultaneously.\" *Source: Advanced Energy NC Electric Transportation Transition, Advanced Energy, August 28, 2023. https://www.advancedenergy.org/news/north-carolinas-electric-transportation-transition *Operational implication:* 150 kW is adequate for Class 4–6 commercial vehicles but is below the 350–1,000 kW charging capabilities emerging for Class 8 freight trucks. Workspaces evaluating North Carolina route electrification for heavy freight should not assume current NEVI corridor infrastructure is adequate for Class 8 operations.\n\n### State Term Contract — Procurement System Access\n\nThe DOA source states the statewide term contract \"2613A – Electric Vehicle Charging Station Equipment, Accessories, Installation, and Infrastructure\" was updated to include six vendors with approximately 17 different brands. *Source: NC DOA Zero-Emission Vehicles Page, NC Department of Administration. https://www.doa.nc.gov/about/sustainability/zero-emission-vehicles *Analytical inference:* Workspaces eligible to use state term contracts (e.g., as state contractors) can leverage this procurement vehicle for charging station installation at lower transaction cost.\n\n### MHD ZEV Charging Demand Forecast — Grid Planning\n\nThe Advanced Energy source states: \"one preliminary NCDOT estimate found that vehicle charging, most of which could take place overnight, would call for 3.5 million megawatt-hours per year by 2035.\" *Source: Advanced Energy NC Electric Transportation Transition, Advanced Energy, August 28, 2023. https://www.advancedenergy.org/news/north-carolinas-electric-transportation-transition *Analytical inference:* Workspaces planning large-scale depot charging in North Carolina should anticipate constrained utility interconnection timelines in high-freight-density corridors (Charlotte, Greensboro, Statesville), where grid demand from MHD electrification will be concentrated.\n\n### Key MHD Freight Corridors Identified\n\nThe Advanced Energy source identifies the highest-traffic MHD areas for charging infrastructure planning as: \"Charlotte, along the I-77 and I-85 interchange; Statesville, along the I-77 and I-40 interchange; Greensboro, along the I-40 and I-85 interchange; and Mount Airy, along the I-77 and I-74 interchange.\" *Source: Advanced Energy NC Electric Transportation Transition, Advanced Energy, August 28, 2023. https://www.advancedenergy.org/news/north-carolinas-electric-transportation-transition\n\n---"
        }
      ],
