          "text": "NCDOT: Climate Change &amp; Clean Energy Turn on more accessible mode Turn off more accessible mode --> skip to main Search It looks like your browser does not have JavaScript enabled. Please turn on JavaScript and try again. --> Menu NCDOT Alerts How to turn on browser translation Search Close Menu NCDOT About About Back Our Mission Our People Boards &amp; Offices How We Operate News &amp; Events News &amp; Events Back News Releases Public Meetings &amp; Events Social Media Channels Public Records Annual Reports Media Inquiries Divisions Divisions Back Aviation DMV Ferry Global TransPark Highways Integrated Mobility Ports Authority Rail Turnpike Authority Initiatives &amp; Policies Initiatives &amp; Policies Back Improving Transportation Safety Students &amp; Youth Environmental Access for All Federal Competitive Grants Travel &amp; Maps Travel &amp; Maps Back Traffic &amp; Travel Maps Ferry Tickets &amp; Services Toll Roads Boating Train Service Projects &amp; Studies Projects &amp; Studies Back Projects Projects Back Current Projects Hurricane Helene Recovery Completed Projects Unfunded Projects Studies Studies Back Long-Range Studies Related Resources Related Resources Back Funding Projects Building N.C. Roads Property Owner Resources Public Meetings Other Links Other Links Back Construction Progress Report Project Progress Report Careers Contact NCDMV Open Today All DMV offices are OPEN today, June 19, 2026. Info alert link Find an office near you Fraud Alert NCDMV is aware of multiple fraudulent text scams happening now requesting payment for fees, fines or tolls. NCDMV will never request payment by text. Please report any text you receive as spam and delete it. Warning alert link ADA Digital Accessibility The N.C. Department of Transportation is committed to ensuring that digital content on our applications and websites is accessible to the public. If you need assistance accessing our content, please contact us . Success alert link Close Next Prev 1 2 3 NCDOT --> Search It looks like your browser does not have JavaScript enabled. Please turn on JavaScript and try again. --> NC.gov Translation Real Time Traffic 3 Site Map About About Our Mission Our People Boards &amp; Offices How We Operate News &amp; Events News &amp; Events News Releases Public Meetings &amp; Events Social Media Channels Public Records Annual Reports Media Inquiries Divisions Divisions Aviation DMV Ferry Global TransPark Highways Integrated Mobility Ports Authority Rail Turnpike Authority Initiatives &amp; Policies Initiatives &amp; Policies Improving Transportation Safety Students &amp; Youth Environmental Access for All Federal Competitive Grants Travel &amp; Maps Travel &amp; Maps Traffic &amp; Travel Maps Ferry Tickets &amp; Services Toll Roads Boating Train Service Projects Projects &amp; Studies Projects Current Projects Hurricane Helene Recovery Completed Projects Unfunded Projects Studies Long-Range Studies Related Resources Funding Projects Building N.C. Roads Property Owner Resources Public Meetings Other Links Construction Progress Report Project Progress Report Careers Contact JavaScript Required JavaScript must be enabled to use some features of this site. For further assistance, call us at 1-877-DOT-4YOU ( 1-877-368-4968 ). For DMV questions, call us at 919-715-7000 . Our mailing address is 1501 Mail Service Center, Raleigh NC 27699-1501. Home Initiatives &amp; Policies Environmental Climate Change &amp; Clean Transportation Expand Climate Change &amp; Clean Transportation JavaScript must be enabled to use some features of this site. Please do one of the following: Reload Page View Site Map Email NCDOT Page Content ​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​ On Oct. 29, 2018, former&nbsp;Gov. Roy Cooper signed into law&nbsp; Executive Order No. 80 (EO 80) , “North Carolina’s Commitment to Address Climate Change and Transition to a Clean Energy Economy.” EO 80 calls to reduce greenhouse gas emission to 40 percent&nbsp;below 2005 levels by 2025. In response to EO 80, the N.C. Department of Transportation&nbsp;has started formulating strategies that seek to reduce emissions from the transportation sector to adapt to a changing environment.&nbsp;These strategies include the&nbsp; North Carolina Zero Emission Vehicle (ZEV) Plan​ &nbsp;and the&nbsp; Multi-State Medium- and Heavy-Duty Zero Emission Vehicle Memorandum of Understanding . In addition, the department has worked with stakeholders to prepare a&nbsp; Vehicle Miles Traveled (VMT) Reduction Study &nbsp;and other&nbsp; transportation emission reduction strategies​ . NCDOT is&nbsp;also leveraging&nbsp;its existing long-range planning efforts, such as&nbsp; NC Moves 2050 , to identify short-term, near-term and longer-term initiatives that promote and facilitate the reduction of carbon emissions across all the agency’s modes of transportation. In January 2022, Gov. Cooper signed Executive Order 246 (EO 246), “North Carolina’s Transformation to a Clean, Equitable Economy.” EO 246 builds upon EO 80 and calls for a 50 percent reduction in economy-wide greenhouse gas emissions by 2030. It also sets a transportation-specific goal to increase the total number of registered zero-emission vehicles to at least 1,250,000 by 2030 and increase the sale of zero-emission vehicles so that 50 percent of all in-state vehicle sales are zero-emission by 2030. EO 246 also directs NCDOT to develop a Clean Transportation Plan​ .​ ​Resilience Planning NCDOT recognizes global climate change brings increased risk of extreme weather events, such as hurricanes and flooding. The occurrence of more severe weather poses a significant challenge to NCDOT’s mission of connecting people, products, and places safely and efficiently​​. Extreme weather potentially threatens the state’s transportation infrastructure, from the mountains to the coast. That is why the department is constantly working on its ability to anticipate and prepare for extreme events. NCDOT continues to be resilient​ in the face of violent weather, responding to any disruptions and rapidly recovering from them. ​Much of the department’s work has been focused on assessing vulnerabilities exposed by recent weather events and then considering the best strategies for responding and for adopting new highway designs that improve the state’s transportation network. Related Transportation Emission Reduction Projects Webpage Multi-State Collaboratives Webpage Vehicle Miles Traveled (VMT) Reduction Study Webpage ZEV Registration Data Webpage Education &amp; Events Webpage Executive Order 246 PDF • 1MB • March 9, 2022 Contact Heather J. Hildebrandt Statewide Initiatives Supervisor Send Message (919) 707-0964 1554 Mail Service Center Raleigh, NC 27699-1554 Last updated Jan. 6, 2025 Initiatives &amp; Policies Adopt-A-Highway Climate Change &amp; Clean Transportation Currently selected Transportation Emission Reduction Projects N.C. Zero Emission Vehicle (ZEV) Plan ZEV Registration Data Multi-State Collaboratives Vehicle Miles Traveled Reduction Study National Electric Vehicle Infrastructure (NEVI) Program N.C. Clean Transportation Plan Education &amp; Events N.C. Clean Transportation Plan Work Groups News Releases Litter Management Reducing Noise Pollution Roadside Beautification Sponsor-A-Highway Stormwater Management Vegetation Management Wildflower Program Environmental Justice Back to top --> Contact N.C. Department of Transportation 1501 Mail Service Center Raleigh, NC 27699-1501 Send Message 1-877-DOT-4YOU (1-877-368-4968) --> Locate contact information for NCDOT employees, local offices, and more. View Staff Directory NCDOT Divisions Aviation Bicycle &amp; Pedestrian DMV Ferry Global TransPark Highways Ports Public Transportation Rail Turnpike Social Follow Us: Facebook Twitter YouTube Flickr Instagram NC.gov Home Employee Directory Accessibility Terms of Use Privacy Policy NC OpenBook NCDOT Go to NCDOT Home Page"
