          "section_id": "345791e4-6691-4704-9fbf-40a2bce308eb",
          "key": "5",
          "md": "This brief does not resolve:\n\n- **Specific IEA primary report quantitative findings** — processing share percentages, country-level refining capacity figures, and lifecycle emissions data from the IEA 2022 report are not span-grounded here because the primary HTML source was blocked. Re-check: retrieve the IEA PDF at `https://iea.blob.core.windows.net/assets/4eb8c252-76b1-4710-8f5e-867e751c8dda/GlobalSupplyChainsofEVBatteries.pdf to ground these claims.\n- **IEA 2024 sustainability follow-up content** — recycling targets, ESG standards, and lifecycle emissions findings from the December 2024 report were blocked at retrieval. Re-check: `https://www.iea.org/reports/ev-battery-supply-chain-sustainability.\n- **IEA 2026 EV Outlook manufacturing capacity and chemistry data** — the >4 TWh capacity figure and LFP dominance data cited in the corroborating source list could not be span-grounded. Re-check: `https://www.iea.org/reports/global-ev-outlook-2026/electric-vehicle-batteries.\n- **China's specific cell production share percentage** — the ScienceDirect article was blocked. Re-check: `https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0301420725002971.\n- **Current dangerous goods regulatory requirements** for large-format EV battery pack shipments by air and ocean. *Legal Confirmation Required:* consult carrier DG desks and legal counsel before committing capacity on battery cargo lanes.\n- **Whether CBAM product scope will expand to cover battery precursor materials** — this is an open regulatory question as of mid-2026. Monitor EU Commission CBAM review schedule.\n\n*Analytical inference:* Re-check window for this brief: **Q3 2026**, by which point the IEA 2026 EV Outlook should be fully published and retrievable, and the IEA PDF direct link should be testable for access. At that point, the FACT claims in this brief should be upgraded from RMI-corroborated to primary-IEA-grounded where the spans align.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "5f8a97d9-e30d-4a45-a472-b0d703a49f3d",
          "key": "6",
          "md": "| # | Title | Issuing Body | Date | Type | URL |\n|---|-------|-------------|------|------|-----|\n| 1 | The EV Battery Supply Chain Explained | RMI (Rocky Mountain Institute) | May 5, 2023 | Tier 4 — Independent nonprofit analysis | https://rmi.org/the-ev-battery-supply-chain-explained/ |\n| 2 | Global Supply Chains of EV Batteries (HTML — blocked) | IEA | 2022 | Tier 2 — Intergovernmental body (not retrievable) | https://iea.org/reports/global-supply-chains-of-ev-batteries |\n| 3 | EV Battery Supply Chain Sustainability (blocked) | IEA | December 2024 | Tier 2 — Intergovernmental body (not retrievable) | https://www.iea.org/reports/ev-battery-supply-chain-sustainability |\n| 4 | Assessing Critical Mineral Occurrence in Battery Technologies (blocked) | ScienceDirect / Elsevier | 2025 | Tier 6 — Peer-reviewed academic (not retrievable) | https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0301420725002971 |\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "773ab418-85c4-43ad-b2da-8337fbf33eb5",
          "key": "4",
          "md": "### What the Workspace Can Credibly Say\n\n- Battery supply chains generate freight volumes that cross multiple modes and hemispheres before a single vehicle is completed; air freight plays a critical role in time-sensitive component recovery when supply nodes are disrupted.\n- Geographic concentration of battery mineral processing creates documented disruption risk — publicly stated by both the IEA and US government representatives — making supply chain resilience a client priority with a direct logistics dimension.\n- The IRA is actively redirecting battery supply chain investment toward North America; workspaces with air and road capacity on US domestic battery corridors are positioned to serve that shift.\n\n### Claims to Avoid\n\n- Do not cite the IEA primary report's specific quantitative figures (processing share percentages, refining capacity numbers) in client conversations without first retrieving and reading the actual IEA PDF. The HTML source was blocked at retrieval; specific numbers from that report are not span-grounded here.\n- Do not cite China's cell production share as a specific percentage (e.g., 75%) without retrieving the ScienceDirect source; that figure was not span-grounded from an available block.\n- Do not characterise the IRA's trade posture as \"decoupling from China\" — the publicly stated US government position, per the available source, is \"de-risking,\" not decoupling.\n\n**FACT:** \"A more accurate assessment is provided by US Trade Representative Katherine Tai, who labels the current administration's approach 'de-risking.'\" *Source: RMI, \"The EV Battery Supply Chain Explained,\" May 5, 2023. https://rmi.org/the-ev-battery-supply-chain-explained/\n\n### Questions to Pose to Automotive and Technology Clients\n\n- Which battery chemistry does your supply chain currently depend on, and have you assessed how LFP share growth changes your upstream mineral sourcing geography?\n- What is your current approach to transport-leg emissions data for battery component shipments, given EU Battery Regulation passport requirements coming into scope?\n- Have you identified alternative routing for battery components if a major China-based refining node is disrupted?\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "ab42d5d2-eb1d-46b7-a6f5-08c099421b1d",
          "key": "3",
          "md": "### Routing and Capacity Decisions — Americas Corridor Reorientation\n\n*Analytical inference:* The IRA domestic content requirements are an active policy mechanism redirecting investment toward North American battery supply chain nodes. Workspaces that develop air and road capacity on US domestic corridors connecting emerging battery manufacturing clusters (e.g., Southeast US, upper Midwest) to port gateways and automotive OEM facilities are positioned ahead of the volume shift rather than reacting to it.\n\n*Analytical inference:* The IEA Global EV Outlook 2026 (listed as a corroborating source, not retrieved as a source block) is reported to contain data on manufacturing capacity exceeding 4 TWh and LFP chemistry dominance trends. If confirmed on retrieval, these figures would indicate the volume scale of cell production that generates downstream logistics demand. Re-check window: Q3 2026, when the IEA 2026 EV Outlook data should be retrievable.\n\n### Supplier and Client Data Requests — Scope 3 Emissions Reporting\n\n*Analytical inference:* The IEA 2024 sustainability follow-up (blocked at retrieval) is reported to analyse lifecycle emissions and ESG standards across the battery supply chain. Automotive OEM clients and battery manufacturers subject to CSRD or SEC climate disclosure requirements will increasingly request Scope 3 freight emissions data from their logistics providers. Workspaces without structured emissions data collection on battery-related freight legs will face client-qualification risk as these disclosure obligations mature.\n\n**No-Vacuum Connection — EU Battery Regulation (Regulation (EU) 2023/1542):** *Analytical inference:* The EU Battery Regulation, which entered force in 2023 and is being phased in through 2030, requires carbon footprint declarations and supply chain due diligence for batteries placed on the EU market. Freight forwarders moving EV battery components into the EU may face client requests for transport-leg emissions data to populate battery passport declarations. This is a reporting infrastructure decision, not only a compliance observation.\n\n**No-Vacuum Connection — EU CBAM:** *Analytical inference:* To the extent that battery precursor materials (e.g., processed nickel, aluminium) imported into the EU fall within CBAM's expanding product scope, importers will require embedded carbon data from upstream supply chain stages. Workspaces acting as customs brokers or import agents for battery material shipments should monitor CBAM product scope expansion.\n\n### Technology Shift Risk — Chemistry Diversification\n\n**FACT:** \"A change in materials needed due to new technologies: Battery chemistries and designs are changing quickly; many of them use alternative and more abundant materials. These changes will affect the supply chain network and the countries and companies involved.\" *Source: RMI, \"The EV Battery Supply Chain Explained,\" May 5, 2023. https://rmi.org/the-ev-battery-supply-chain-explained/\n\n*Analytical inference:* LFP (lithium iron phosphate) chemistry, which uses no cobalt or nickel, reduces exposure to the Democratic Republic of Congo and Indonesian supply nodes. If LFP continues to gain share (as the IEA 2026 EV Outlook reportedly indicates), ocean freight lanes servicing DRC cobalt and Indonesian nickel refinery output may see lower growth than currently assumed, while lithium carbonate/hydroxide lanes from South America and Australia grow. Route planning for battery-adjacent cargo should be updated as chemistry data is confirmed.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c25df85f-2df5-4b8c-b9b3-65cfae7206e0",
          "key": "1",
          "md": "### Headline Finding: Extreme Geographic Concentration Creates Systemic Freight and Supply-Chain Exposure\n\nThe EV battery supply chain spans four stages — upstream mining of lithium, cobalt, manganese, nickel, and graphite; midstream refining and cathode/anode material production; downstream cell assembly and battery pack manufacturing; and end-of-life reuse and recycling.\n\n**FACT:** \"The EV battery supply chain is dispersed around the world — battery minerals travel an average of 50,000 miles from extraction to battery cell production.\" *Source: RMI, \"The EV Battery Supply Chain Explained,\" May 5, 2023. https://rmi.org/the-ev-battery-supply-chain-explained/\n\n**FACT:** \"much of the mineral supply is concentrated in just a few countries. This dispersion and concentration make the global supply chain vulnerable to disruptions.\" *Source: RMI, \"The EV Battery Supply Chain Explained,\" May 5, 2023. https://rmi.org/the-ev-battery-supply-chain-explained/\n\n**FACT:** \"China currently dominates the supply chain. As broader geopolitical issues affect economic and trade relationships, the stability of the global supply chain is increasingly at risk when extracting, refining, processing, and assembling an outsized share of EV battery components occurs in any single country.\" *Source: RMI, \"The EV Battery Supply Chain Explained,\" May 5, 2023. https://rmi.org/the-ev-battery-supply-chain-explained/\n\n*Specific [China percentage share of cell production] not available from primary source blocks as of 2026-06-05. The ScienceDirect article (pii/S0301420725002971) is cited as a candidate source for a 75% figure but was blocked by Cloudflare; that figure is not span-grounded here and cannot be asserted as FACT.*\n\n### Disruption Typology Sourced from Available Block\n\n**FACT:** Disruption categories documented include: \"Extreme weather (e.g., hurricanes, tornadoes, and earthquakes that impact energy inputs and disrupt infrastructure like pipelines and shipping routes) Geopolitics (e.g., the war between Russia and Ukraine) Changing trade alliances between countries or regions Corporate consolidation.\" *Source: RMI, \"The EV Battery Supply Chain Explained,\" May 5, 2023. https://rmi.org/the-ev-battery-supply-chain-explained/\n\n**FACT:** \"These disruptions can result in bottlenecks and negatively affect the rest of the battery supply chain; they can also impact economies, cause delays for suppliers, increase transportation costs, force employers to cut jobs, discourage investment, and hinder transportation decarbonization.\" *Source: RMI, \"The EV Battery Supply Chain Explained,\" May 5, 2023. https://rmi.org/the-ev-battery-supply-chain-explained/\n\n### Scale of Demand Trajectory\n\n**FACT:** \"there are about 2.5 million EVs on US roads; this number will need to increase to 44 million by 2030 if we are to reach net-zero emissions.\" *Source: RMI, \"The EV Battery Supply Chain Explained,\" May 5, 2023. https://rmi.org/the-ev-battery-supply-chain-explained/\n\n**FACT:** \"S&P Global Mobility forecasts EV sales in the United States alone could reach 40 percent of total passenger car sales by 2030.\" *Source: RMI, \"The EV Battery Supply Chain Explained,\" May 5, 2023. https://rmi.org/the-ev-battery-supply-chain-explained/\n\n### Policy and Investment Context\n\n**FACT:** \"in just the few short months since the Inflation Reduction Act was passed, the United States has seen more than $40 billion worth of new investment announcements across the battery supply chain.\" *Source: RMI, \"The EV Battery Supply Chain Explained,\" May 5, 2023. https://rmi.org/the-ev-battery-supply-chain-explained/\n\n**FACT:** The IRA \"requires that an increased percentage of EV battery supply chain activities take place in North America to be eligible for certain tax credits.\" *Source: RMI, \"The EV Battery Supply Chain Explained,\" May 5, 2023. https://rmi.org/the-ev-battery-supply-chain-explained/\n\n### Methodology and Scope Limits\n\nThe RMI article (May 2023) is an independent nonprofit synthesis, classified Tier 4 (industry body / think-tank analysis). It corroborates the IEA 2022 primary report but is not the primary report itself. The IEA primary HTML page and the IEA 2024 sustainability follow-up were both blocked by Cloudflare at retrieval time; their specific quantitative claims and policy recommendations cannot be span-grounded in this brief. The ScienceDirect peer-reviewed article was similarly blocked. *Specific [IEA primary report quantitative findings — lithium/cobalt processing shares, regional refining capacity figures, lifecycle emissions data]* not available from primary source blocks as of 2026-06-05.\n\n*Analytical inference:* The IEA PDF (https://iea.blob.core.windows.net/assets/4eb8c252-76b1-4710-8f5e-867e751c8dda/GlobalSupplyChainsofEVBatteries.pdf) is the most likely source for granular supply-chain concentration data and should be retrieved on re-check; it is listed under New Sources Identified.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "dc3b3402-121c-498b-a91c-9c513e87cdba",
          "key": "2",
          "md": "### Transport Volume and Route Concentration\n\n*Analytical inference:* The documented 50,000-mile average mineral travel distance means a single EV battery's component materials transit multiple ocean freight legs, road drayage segments, and potentially air freight for time-sensitive or high-value components before final assembly. For workspaces moving automotive components, electronics, or manufactured goods along trans-Pacific, Europe–Asia, and Americas trade lanes, this creates both volume opportunity and disruption exposure on the same corridors.\n\n*Analytical inference:* The concentration of midstream refining in China — and the IRA's explicit push to shift a greater share of processing to North America — is a structural route-shift signal. Freight volumes historically moving China → North America for battery materials and cells will be partially redistributed toward intra-North American and Australia/Africa → North America corridors as diversification investment lands. Workspaces with air and ocean capacity on these emerging corridors are positioned to capture reoriented flows.\n\n### Disruption Risk as a Freight Volatility Driver\n\n**FACT:** \"Corporate consolidation: Today, when one of the many companies involved in the battery supply chain experiences a disruption, others are affected. As EV demand rises, it's likely that there will be a few big players that will oversee more parts of the process. If one (or more) of these companies experience disruptions, the effects will be greater.\" *Source: RMI, \"The EV Battery Supply Chain Explained,\" May 5, 2023. https://rmi.org/the-ev-battery-supply-chain-explained/\n\n*Operational implication:* As battery supply chain consolidation increases single-node dependency, a disruption at a major cell manufacturer or mineral refiner creates immediate air freight demand spikes for expedited component movement — a mode where workspaces with air-primary capability carry a structural advantage over ocean-only operators.\n\n### Dangerous Goods Classification Relevance\n\n*Analytical inference:* Lithium-ion battery cells and packs are classified as dangerous goods under IATA DGR and IMDG regulations. As EV battery shipment volumes grow along the corridors identified in the research, workspaces handling automotive and technology verticals face increasing frequency of DG compliance requirements — documentation, packaging certification, carrier approval, and incident liability — on both air and ocean modes. This is a resourcing and training implication, not only a volume opportunity.\n\n*Specific [current IATA or IMDG dangerous goods reclassification timelines for large-format EV battery shipments]* not available from primary source blocks as of 2026-06-05. *Legal Confirmation Required:* current DG classification requirements for large-format EV battery packs shipped as cargo (vs. in-vehicle) should be confirmed with counsel and the relevant carrier's DG acceptance desk before committing capacity.\n\n---"
        },
        {
