        {
          "section_id": "19121156-db01-4937-a18f-47e9e74429a7",
          "key": "5",
          "md": "## UAE Long-Term Strategy (LTS) 2024 — Transport Sector Provisions\n\n**FACT:** The Climate Action Tracker confirms: \"The UAE's LTS, published in 2024, includes targets and current policy projections for all major sectors of the economy, and detail both existing and planned measures to achieve them.\" The LTS covers transport alongside power, industry, buildings, agriculture, and waste. *Source: Climate Action Tracker, UAE Policies and Action, 15 November 2024. https://climateactiontracker.org/countries/uae/policies-action/\n\n**FACT:** The 2024 LTS includes \"additional information on its power sector plan, including a demand forecast of 211 TWh by 2030 and a grid coefficient of 0.27 tCO2e/MWh, a 51% reduction from the 2019 baseline level of 0.55 tCO2e/MWh.\" *Source: Climate Action Tracker, UAE Policies and Action, 15 November 2024. https://climateactiontracker.org/countries/uae/policies-action/\n\n*Analytical inference:* A falling grid emissions intensity in the UAE (from 0.55 to a target of 0.27 tCO2e/MWh by 2030) is directly relevant to freight logistics operations that electrify road vehicles or warehousing — the emissions benefit of electrification improves as the grid decarbonises, making early electrification commitments more favourable over time.\n\n## UAE NDC 2023 — Transport Sector Provisions\n\n**FACT:** \"The UAE's 2023 NDC update sets a target to reduce electricity emissions by 11% below 2019 levels by 2030.\" *Source: Climate Action Tracker, UAE Policies and Action, 15 November 2024. https://climateactiontracker.org/countries/uae/policies-action/\n\n**FACT:** The NDC includes a sectoral target: \"Under this sectoral target, emissions from electricity are expected to be ~68 MtCO2e in 2030.\" *Source: Climate Action Tracker, UAE Policies and Action, 15 November 2024. https://climateactiontracker.org/countries/uae/policies-action/\n\n*Specific transport-sector sub-targets within the 2023 NDC (e.g., road freight vehicle standards, aviation fuel mandates, maritime emissions targets) are not available from primary corpus sources as of 6 June 2026.* The CAT source describes NDC transport coverage at the sectoral level but does not reproduce specific transport sub-targets.\n\n## LTS Power Sector Planned Policies — Operational Relevance\n\n*Analytical inference:* \"As part of its LTS, the UAE plans to roll out additional policies to support the deployment of renewable energy. These include CO2 emissions targets or caps for power producers through regulators at the emirate level, incentives for distributed power generation such as net metering, installing PV solar in public buildings, and tariff revisions to facilitate load balancing.\" *Source: Climate Action Tracker, UAE Policies and Action, 15 November 2024. https://climateactiontracker.org/countries/uae/policies-action/\n\n*Operational implication:* Emirate-level CO2 caps for power producers will affect the electricity price and carbon content of grid power used in UAE logistics facilities and EV charging, creating operational cost variability by emirate.\n\n## CCS Reliance — Critical Qualification for Transport Sector Credibility\n\n*Analytical inference:* **FACT:** \"The UAE plans to rely heavily on carbon capture and storage (CCS) technologies especially after 2030 and towards its 2050 target, which undermines the credibility of its climate policies and targets.\" *Source: Climate Action Tracker, UAE Policies and Action, 15 November 2024. https://climateactiontracker.org/countries/uae/policies-action/\n\n*Analytical inference:* A national strategy that relies on post-2030 CCS rather than fuel-switching for heavy industry may signal that transport sector mandates (fleet electrification, alternative fuel blending) will be weaker or later-arriving than in comparable jurisdictions (EU, UK, Singapore), giving UAE-based operations a longer adaptation window but also less regulatory certainty on direction.\n\n---"
        },
