          "key": "3",
          "md": "**Severity: MONITORING**\n\nNo binding transport-sector compliance deadlines with immediate (30-day) legal exposure are identifiable from the corpus. The initiative is a strategic framework at the national policy level. However, the following forward-positioned actions are warranted given the trajectory.\n\n### 1. Map Current Emissions Reporting Baseline Against UAE LTS Transport Benchmarks (30–60 days)\n\nAssess what GHG emissions data is currently captured for UAE-based transport operations across road, air, and ocean modes.\n\n*Operational implication:* The UAE's 2024 LTS includes \"a sectoral breakdown and detailed information at the measure level\" for transport. *Source: Climate Action Tracker, UAE Policies and Action, 15 November 2024. https://climateactiontracker.org/countries/uae/policies-action/ Workspaces without current Scope 1 and Scope 3 emissions baselines for UAE operations will be unprepared when reporting obligations are formalised through implementing instruments.\n\n### 2. Engage UAE Federal and Emirate-Level Counterparts on Transport Sector Implementation (60 days)\n\nEngage with UAE Ministry of Energy and Infrastructure and, where relevant, Abu Dhabi Department of Energy and Dubai's Road and Transport Authority to identify forthcoming transport-sector implementing regulations.\n\n*Analytical inference:* The gap between the UAE's LTS framework targets and current policy outcomes — the CAT notes the UAE \"is set to miss its latest 2030 emissions reduction target\" under current policies — *Source: Climate Action Tracker, UAE Policies and Action, 15 November 2024. https://climateactiontracker.org/countries/uae/policies-action/ — increases the probability of accelerated implementing regulation in the near term to close the gap.\n\n### 3. Assess Fleet and Fuel Exposure for UAE Road Operations (60–90 days)\n\nAssess the fossil fuel dependency profile of UAE road transport operations (owned or contracted vehicles) against the trajectory implied by the UAE Energy Strategy 2050 targets.\n\n*Operational implication:* The UAE's Energy Strategy 2050, as referenced in the NDC and LTS, includes a target to produce \"at least 30% of its electricity through renewable sources by 2030.\" *Source: Climate Action Tracker, UAE Policies and Action, 15 November 2024. https://climateactiontracker.org/countries/uae/policies-action/ Fleet operators dependent on fossil diesel in UAE operations face upstream fuel cost and regulatory transition risk as the strategy is implemented.\n\n---"
