          "key": "4",
          "md": "The UAE Net Zero 2050 Transport Sector framework positions compliance obligations at multiple supply-chain nodes, depending on the implementing instrument. Based on the corpus, the following role-level analysis applies:\n\n**Transport Operators / Fleet Operators:** The LTS includes sectoral projections and measures for the transport sector. *Analytical inference:* Road freight fleet operators — whether operating owned vehicles or contracting haulage — are the most directly exposed actors to any vehicle emissions standards or fuel mandate instruments flowing from the framework.\n\n**Freight Forwarders and Logistics Intermediaries:** As intermediaries, freight forwarders' compliance exposure depends on whether implementing instruments impose obligations on the organiser of transport (the forwarder) or on the direct operator of transport (the carrier/hauler).\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* Whether the workspace, in its role as freight forwarder arranging road, air, or ocean transport in the UAE, is an obligated party under any transport-sector implementing instrument flowing from the Net Zero 2050 framework — as distinct from the underlying carrier — requires legal review of each specific implementing instrument as published.\n\n**Shippers and Cargo Principals:** Where implementing instruments impose Scope 3 emissions disclosure on cargo owners, freight forwarders providing logistics services will face downstream demand for activity-based emissions data from clients.\n\n*Operational implication:* The UAE LTS explicitly addresses the transport sector with \"detailed information at the measure level,\" *Source: Climate Action Tracker, UAE Policies and Action, 15 November 2024. https://climateactiontracker.org/countries/uae/policies-action/ meaning that clients of freight forwarding services operating in the UAE may face their own disclosure obligations that create upstream data requests.\n\n---"
