          "key": "13",
          "md": "## CAT Assessment of UAE Policy Gap\n\n**FACT:** \"The CAT rates the UAE's policies and actions as 'Insufficient' when compared with modelled domestic emissions pathways. The 'Insufficient' rating indicates that the UAE's climate policies and action in 2030 need substantial improvements to be consistent with limiting warming to 1.5°C. If all countries were to follow the UAE's approach, warming would reach over 2°C and up to 3°C.\" *Source: Climate Action Tracker, UAE Policies and Action, 15 November 2024. https://climateactiontracker.org/countries/uae/policies-action/\n\n*Analytical inference:* An \"Insufficient\" CAT rating in the context of a 15–40% gap to the 2030 NDC target creates political and reputational pressure for the UAE government to accelerate implementing instruments, particularly in the transport sector, where concrete measures can be publicly demonstrated. This is a signal — not a confirmed event — that new transport mandates may arrive on a compressed timeline relative to the LTS's stated 2030 and 2050 milestones.\n\n## Green Shipping Corridors and UAE Port Activity\n\n*Analytical inference:* The UAE continues to invest in offshore gas production. \"In October 2023 for the Hail and Ghasha Offshore Development project. This project aims to produce 1.5 billion standard cubic feet per day (bscfd) of gas before the end of the decade.\" *Source: Climate Action Tracker, UAE Policies and Action, 15 November 2024. https://climateactiontracker.org/countries/uae/policies-action/\n\n*Analytical inference:* The UAE's dual position as a fossil fuel producer and a Net Zero 2050 signatory creates a structural tension that affects the credibility of maritime and aviation decarbonisation commitments. Workspaces relying on UAE-originating alternative fuel supply chains (LNG, hydrogen, ammonia) for green shipping corridors should assess the UAE's production trajectory for those fuels separately from its emissions reduction commitments.\n\n## USD 100bn PACE Commitment — Renewables Relevance\n\n*Analytical inference:* **FACT:** \"In November 2022, the UAE signed the Partnership for Accelerating Clean Energy (PACE) with the US, committing to mobilise USD 100bn to support the development of 100 GW of renewables both domestically and abroad by 2035.\" *Source: Climate Action Tracker, UAE Policies and Action, 15 November 2024. https://climateactiontracker.org/countries/uae/policies-action/\n\n*Analytical inference:* If a material share of this USD 100bn flows into UAE domestic renewable power and EV charging infrastructure, it accelerates the timeline for viable fleet electrification in UAE road freight. However, the corpus notes it \"is not yet clear which technologies will be supported under the 'clean energy' concept,\" reducing the confidence of this inference.\n\n---"
