          "md": "## Emissions Data Infrastructure\n\nAssess current emissions data capture for all UAE transport modes (road, air, ocean). The UAE LTS transport sector provisions include \"detailed information at the measure level,\" *Source: Climate Action Tracker, UAE Policies and Action, 15 November 2024. https://climateactiontracker.org/countries/uae/policies-action/ implying that when implementing instruments follow, they will be operationally specific.\n\n*Operational implication:* Workspaces operating on spend-based or distance-based Scope 3 emissions estimation without carrier-level activity data will face a material gap between current data quality and what transport-sector reporting instruments are likely to require when formalised, based on the trajectory of comparable jurisdictions.\n\n**Gap from current baseline:** Most freight forwarders operating in the UAE without mandatory reporting requirements will not have activity-based emissions data at shipment level for UAE road, air, or ocean operations. This represents a foundational gap.\n\n**Deadline:** *Specific deadline for a UAE mandatory transport emissions reporting system is not available from primary corpus sources as of 6 June 2026.*\n\n## Fleet and Fuel Transition Readiness\n\nFor workspaces operating owned or contracted road freight vehicles in the UAE, assess current fleet profile against the UAE Energy Strategy 2050 renewable and electrification trajectory.\n\n*Analytical inference:* The UAE's renewables expansion target (19.8 GW by 2030, 33 TWh generation) will progressively improve the economic case for fleet electrification in road freight. Workspaces with long-term UAE fleet contracts should seek to include provisions that allow for vehicle type substitution as EV charging infrastructure develops.\n\n## Grid Emissions Factor Tracking\n\n*Analytical inference:* Track the UAE grid emissions factor annually. The current trajectory (0.55 tCO2e/MWh in 2019, target 0.27 tCO2e/MWh by 2030) affects Scope 2 emissions calculations for UAE warehouse and facility operations.\n\n*Operational implication:* A Scope 2 emissions calculation for UAE facilities that uses a static or outdated grid factor will overstate or understate actual emissions depending on the year, affecting the accuracy of client sustainability reports and any future regulatory submissions.\n\n---"
