        {
          "section_id": "0f63d3bb-0070-4873-9449-ce40423ccb46",
          "key": "8",
          "md": "### 8.1 Eligible Activities (Rule 4(2))\n\nThe enacted rules specify eight activities. Each is reproduced verbatim from the enacted text:\n\n(i) \"tree plantation — to promote activities for increasing the green cover across the country\"\n(ii) \"water management — to promote water conservation, water harvesting and water use efficiency or water savings, including treatment and reuse of wastewater\"\n(iii) \"sustainable agriculture — to promote natural and regenerative agricultural practices and land restoration to improve productivity, soil health and nutritional value of food produced\"\n(iv) \"waste management — to promote circularity, sustainable and improved practices for waste management, including collection, segregation, and environmentally sound management\"\n(v) \"air pollution reduction — to promote measures for reducing air pollution and other pollution abatement activities\"\n(vi) \"mangrove conservation and restoration — to promote measures for conservation and restoration of mangroves\"\n(vii) \"ecomark label development — to encourage manufacturers to obtain ecomark label for their goods and services\"\n(viii) \"sustainable building and infrastructure — to encourage the construction of sustainable buildings and other infrastructure using environment friendly technologies and materials\"\n\n*Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 4(2), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf\n\n*Analytical inference:* Of these eight, categories (iv) waste management, (v) air pollution reduction, and (viii) sustainable building and infrastructure are the most operationally proximate for freight and logistics workspaces operating in India. Category (ii) water management is relevant for sites with significant water use or wastewater treatment. Categories (i), (iii), (vi), and (vii) are less directly linked to core freight operations but may be relevant for workspaces with landholdings or manufacturing clients seeking to offset.\n\n### 8.2 Credit Calculation Basis (Rule 5(2))\n\nThe calculation basis is stated in Rule 5(2): \"The calculation of green credit in respect of any activity undertaken shall be based on equivalence of resource requirement, parity of scale, scope, size and other relevant parameters required to achieve the desired environmental outcome.\" *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 5(2), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf The specific equivalence formulae for each activity are to be notified separately under Rule 5(3) and are not yet published.\n\n### 8.3 Non-Tradability Qualification (Rule 2(3))\n\nCredits generated to fulfil any statutory obligation are not tradable. This is an express carve-out in Rule 2(3) and applies regardless of the activity category. *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 2(3) proviso, Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf\n\n### 8.4 Administrator Responsibilities (Rule 7(3))\n\nThe Administrator's substantive responsibilities include: developing guidelines and procedures for programme implementation (Rule 7(3)(a)); developing methodologies, registration process, and measurement, reporting, and verification mechanisms (Rule 7(3)(b)); establishing methodologies for issuance of green credit including digital green credit (Rule 7(3)(c)); developing guidelines for the registry and trading platform (Rule 7(3)(d)); establishing or designating the Green Credit Registry and trading platform service provider (Rule 7(3)(e)); developing guidelines for the GCP portal, knowledge and data platform, and fees from registered entities (Rule 7(3)(f)); developing guidelines for annual returns and progress reports (Rule 7(3)(g)); developing guidelines for market stability mechanism (Rule 7(3)(h)); and regulating trading of green credit certificates and safeguarding sellers' and buyers' interests (Rule 7(3)(i)). All guidelines and methodologies require Central Government approval (Rule 7(4)). *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 7(3) and 7(4), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf\n\n### 8.5 Audit Requirements (Rule 15)\n\nThe activities of the Administrator, designated agency, Registry, trading platform, and knowledge and data platform are subject to audit \"within a period of one year at the end of every third financial year by independent auditors to be appointed by the Central Government on the recommendation of the Steering Committee\" (Rule 15(1)). The audit report is submitted to the Administrator (Rule 15(2)). The Administrator submits an action taken report to the Central Government \"within a period of six months from the date of the receipt of the audit report\" (Rule 15(3)). *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 15, Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "12b09872-0573-43c7-b3a0-11bc57889a4f",
          "key": "11",
          "md": "### 11.1 Digital Registration Infrastructure\n\n*Operational implication:* To register activities under Rule 4(4), the workspace requires access to the central government portal www.moefcc-gcp.in and the ability to submit electronic applications. This is a low-friction requirement for workspaces already operating digital procurement and compliance systems, but requires identification of the responsible internal function (sustainability, operations, or compliance team) and assignment of portal credentials.\n\n### 11.2 Activity Documentation and Evidence\n\nThe designated agency verification process (Rule 4(5)–4(6)) is conducted \"in accordance with the guideline made in this behalf.\" *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 4(6), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf Specific [documentation requirements for verification] not available from primary sources as of this generation date (guidelines not yet published). *Operational implication:* Workspaces that wish to register activities upon methodology publication should begin internal data collection on relevant activities now — water consumption, waste volumes, emission reduction initiatives, building energy performance — so that evidence packages can be assembled when guidelines are published.\n\n### 11.3 Trading Platform Access\n\n*Operational implication:* When the trading platform is established under Rule 11, workspaces holding Green Credit certificates will require access to the platform to execute sales. The platform is being developed by ICFRE along with experts. *Source: PIB Press Release, MoEF&CC, 13 October 2023. https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleaseIframePage.aspx?PRID=1967476 Specific [trading platform technology requirements or access conditions] not available from primary sources as of this generation date.\n\n### 11.4 Dual-Credit Tracking (Carbon + Green)\n\n*Operational implication:* For workspaces that also engage with India's Carbon Credit Trading Scheme, 2023 (under the Energy Conservation Act, 2001), the possibility of dual credits from a single activity (acknowledged in the GCP preamble) creates a requirement for activity-level tracking granular enough to support separate registration under both schemes. Whether a single infrastructure investment (e.g., a warehouse rooftop solar installation) generates credits under both schemes requires verification once methodology guidelines are published.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "1625813c-3a91-4d14-9745-21b65662f320",
          "key": "4",
          "md": "The Green Credit Rules, 2023 impose no mandatory compliance obligation on any entity. Participation is stated in Rule 14(1) as follows: \"The participation to the Green Credit programme under these rules shall be based on voluntary participation.\" *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 14(1), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf\n\nThe compliance chain for credit generation runs: eligible activity undertaken → registration with Administrator via central government website → verification by designated agency → report submitted to Administrator → Green Credit certificate issued → credit listed on trading platform.\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* Whether a freight forwarding workspace, in its capacity as operator of Indian warehouses, freight stations, or transport hubs, constitutes a \"person or entity\" eligible to register activities under Rule 4(1) and receive Green Credits — and whether any specific activities undertaken at those sites qualify under the enacted categories — is a factual and legal determination that requires verification against individual site operations and published methodologies when notified.\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* Whether a workspace acting as a freight forwarder purchasing Green Credits on behalf of clients, or holding credits as a tradable asset, triggers any securities, commodities, or financial regulation under Indian law requires counsel review beyond the scope of the GCP rules themselves.\n\nThe Indian Council of Forestry Research and Education (ICFRE) is designated as Administrator under Rule 7(1). *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 7(1), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf ICFRE is described as \"a Society registered under the Societies Registration Act, 1860 (21 of 1860) vide Registration No.596/1990-91, dated the 12th March, 1991 and an autonomous body as declared by the then Ministry of Environment and Forests, Government of India vide Resolution No.1-8/89-RT, dated the 22nd June, 1990.\"\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "270b7c7a-5116-4057-905e-c1be17683888",
          "key": "2",
          "md": "**Effective date:** The rules came into force on the date of their publication in the Official Gazette. The Gazette notification is dated 12 October 2023. *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 1(2), Gazette of India Extraordinary, Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf\n\n**Jurisdictional scope:** The rules apply at national level across India and are made under the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 (29 of 1986), Sections 3, 6, and 25. *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, preamble and Rule 1, Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf\n\n**What the programme is:** The Green Credit Rules, 2023 establish a voluntary, domestic, market-based mechanism that awards \"Green Credits\" — defined in Rule 3(1)(c) as \"a singular unit of an incentive provided for a specified activity, delivering a positive impact on the environment\" — to individuals, companies, industries, local bodies, and other entities that undertake specified environmental activities. Green Credits are tradable on a domestic market platform. *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rules 2(1) and 3(1)(c), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf\n\n**Critical programme qualifier — non-tradability of compliance credits:** Rule 2(3) contains an express carve-out: \"the green credit generated or procured to fulfil any obligation in compliance of any law for the time being in force shall not be tradable.\" This is a material qualification: Green Credits earned through mandatory regulatory compliance cannot enter the trading market. *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 2(3) proviso, Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf\n\n**Relationship to the Carbon Credit Trading Scheme:** The rules state that the Green Credit programme \"is independent of the carbon credit under the Carbon Credit Trading Scheme, 2023 made under the Energy Conservation Act, 2001 (52 of 2001)\" and that \"an activity generating green credit under Green Credit programme may also get carbon credit from the same activity under the said Scheme.\" *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, preamble, Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf Dual-credit stacking from a single activity is therefore contemplated by the enacted text.\n\n**Why it applies to freight and logistics workspaces:** The eight eligible activity categories in Rule 4(2) include waste management, water management, sustainable building and infrastructure, and air pollution reduction — activities that intersect with warehouse, depot, and freight station operations. The programme is open to \"any person or entity\" (Rule 4(1)), and participation is voluntary (Rule 14(1)). *Analytical inference:* For workspaces operating warehouses, freight stations, or transport hubs in India, or managing afforestation or water-conservation activities on operational landholdings, the programme creates a pathway to generate tradable credits from activities that may already be underway, or that could be layered onto existing capital expenditure. Whether any specific workspace entity qualifies as an eligible participant and which activities would generate credits requires verification against published methodologies once notified (see Section 6).\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "3093b215-3080-4fec-978a-1527aa901129",
          "key": "5",
          "md": "*No authoritative guidance documents (methodology notifications, designated agency guidelines, trading platform operating rules) have been enacted and published as of this generation date under Rule 5(3) or Rule 7(3). The enacted rules explicitly anticipate their future publication. This section is omitted pending notification of those instruments.*\n\n*Note on initial phase:* The PIB press release of 13 October 2023 states that \"In its initial phase, the GCP focuses on two key activities: water conservation and afforestation. Draft methodologies for awarding Green Credits have been developed and will be notified for stakeholder consultation.\" *Source: PIB Press Release, Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change, 13 October 2023. https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleaseIframePage.aspx?PRID=1967476 This is a government announcement, not an enacted guidance document; it is addressed in Section 6.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "391f3ba7-2a10-44c6-b956-ecee670e1dd3",
          "key": "10",
          "md": "### 10.1 Activity Registration (Rule 4(3) and 4(4))\n\nA person or entity seeking Green Credits is required under Rule 4(3) to \"register the activity with the Administrator for any activity referred to in sub-rule (2) undertaken by him for grant of green credit.\" Rule 4(4) states: \"An application for registration under sub-rule (3) shall be made to the Administrator electronically through a website established by the Central Government for that purpose.\" *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rules 4(3) and 4(4), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf The dedicated portal is www.moefcc-gcp.in. *Source: PIB Press Release, MoEF&CC, 13 October 2023. https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleaseIframePage.aspx?PRID=1967476\n\n### 10.2 Verification and Certificate Issuance (Rules 4(5)–4(7))\n\nOn receipt of the application, the Administrator causes the activity to be verified by a designated agency (Rule 4(5)). The designated agency submits a verification report (Rule 4(6)). On receipt of the verification report, the Administrator grants a Green Credit certificate (Rule 4(7)). *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rules 4(5)–4(7), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf\n\n### 10.3 Annual Returns by Designated Agency (Rule 13(3))\n\nRule 13(3) states: \"The designated agency shall file annual returns to the Administrator in accordance with the guidelines.\" *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 13(3), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf No corresponding annual return obligation is imposed on credit-generating entities (applicants) in the enacted text; this obligation falls on the designated agency.\n\n### 10.4 Registry Records (Rule 10)\n\nThe Green Credit Registry, established by the Administrator or a designated agency, maintains an electronic database containing \"common data elements relevant to the registration and issuance of green credit\" and performs: registration of activities and issuance of green credit; accurate accounting of issuance; and maintenance of a secure database with required security protocols (Rule 10(3)). *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 10, Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf\n\n### 10.5 Gaps\n\nSpecific [required data fields for registration application, format of Green Credit certificate, fee structure for registered entities] not available from primary sources as of this generation date. Rule 7(3)(f) anticipates guidelines for fees from registered entities but they have not been published.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "433e46a4-9e5f-4bb8-acbe-aba4eba2361d",
          "key": "7",
          "md": "**Question 1: Does the workspace's India-based operational activity fall within one of the eight eligible categories in Rule 4(2)?**\nThe eight categories are: (i) tree plantation, (ii) water management, (iii) sustainable agriculture, (iv) waste management, (v) air pollution reduction, (vi) mangrove conservation and restoration, (vii) ecomark label development, and (viii) sustainable building and infrastructure. *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 4(2), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf *Legal Confirmation Required:* Whether specific logistics-site activities (e.g., wastewater treatment at a freight station, warehouse energy efficiency, vehicle fleet emission reduction) qualify under the enacted categories — particularly \"air pollution reduction\" (Rule 4(2)(v)) or \"waste management\" (Rule 4(2)(iv)) — requires verification against published methodologies when notified.\n\n**Question 2: Are Green Credits generated under mandatory compliance obligations tradable?**\nDecided by the enacted text: Rule 2(3) states expressly that \"the green credit generated or procured to fulfil any obligation in compliance of any law for the time being in force shall not be tradable.\" *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 2(3) proviso, Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf\n\n**Question 3: Can the same activity generate both Green Credits and Carbon Credits?**\nDecided by the enacted text: The preamble states that \"an activity generating green credit under Green Credit programme may also get carbon credit from the same activity under the said Scheme.\" The Carbon Credit Trading Scheme, 2023 is made under the Energy Conservation Act, 2001 (52 of 2001) and is a separate instrument. *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, preamble, Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf *Legal Confirmation Required:* The conditions and procedures for dual-credit stacking from a single activity require analysis of both the GCP rules and the Carbon Credit Trading Scheme, 2023 together.\n\n**Question 4: Is self-verification available for small projects?**\nThe PIB press release states \"with self-verification for small projects.\" *Source: PIB Press Release, MoEF&CC, 13 October 2023. https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleaseIframePage.aspx?PRID=1967476 Rule 7(3)(d) refers to \"self-certification or third-party certification\" as options. *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 7(3)(d), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf Specific [threshold or definition of \"small project\" for self-verification] not available from primary sources as of this generation date.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "52d9fed2-4187-4854-9c31-1f675bdbc36c",
          "key": "1",
          "md": "This document analyses the Green Credit Rules, 2023, notified by the Government of India on 12 October 2023 under the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986. It covers the programme's structure, eligible activities, registration and verification procedures, governance architecture, and operational requirements as enacted in the Official Gazette. The document is intended for workspaces operating in freight forwarding, logistics, and transport-adjacent supply chains who may seek to participate in, or whose clients may ask about, India's domestic voluntary green credit market. Convention note: all section references are to the Green Credit Rules, 2023 unless stated otherwise. The document reflects the regulatory text as enacted; anticipated guidance and pending methodologies are addressed in Section 6.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "5ac6c5af-8587-4885-a896-3a02766da38a",
          "key": "12",
          "md": "### 12.1 Non-Tradability of Compliance Credits\n\nThe express exemption in Rule 2(3) — \"the green credit generated or procured to fulfil any obligation in compliance of any law for the time being in force shall not be tradable\" — creates a category of credits that are generated but cannot be monetised on the trading platform. *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 2(3) proviso, Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf *Analytical inference:* For workspaces whose India operations already undertake environmental activities to comply with, for example, the Environment Protection Act, 1986, the Solid Waste Management Rules, or other environmental statutes, those compliance activities will not generate tradable Green Credits even if they would otherwise qualify under Rule 4(2). Only genuinely voluntary, supra-compliance activities are eligible for tradable credits.\n\n### 12.2 Self-Certification for Small Projects\n\nRule 7(3)(d) contemplates \"self-certification or third-party certification for the registration of an activity for issuance of green credits.\" *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 7(3)(d), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf The PIB press release confirms \"self-verification for small projects.\" *Source: PIB Press Release, MoEF&CC, 13 October 2023. https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleaseIframePage.aspx?PRID=1967476 Specific [threshold defining \"small project\" for self-verification eligibility] not available from primary sources as of this generation date.\n\n### 12.3 Voluntary Nature — No Participation Obligation\n\nRule 14(1) states that participation \"shall be based on voluntary participation.\" *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 14(1), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf There is no penalty for non-participation and no mandatory enrolment for any entity type.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "7164a214-4ce2-4754-abb4-ae858b40d675",
          "key": "3",
          "md": "**MONITORING — Register for the GCP portal and track methodology publication**\n\nAssess whether any India-based operational sites (warehouses, depots, freight stations, vehicle parks) currently undertake any of the eight eligible activities listed in Rule 4(2). *Operational implication:* Until activity-specific methodologies are notified by the Central Government (Rule 5(1) and 5(3) provide that methodologies \"shall be notified by the Central Government on the recommendation of the Administrator\"), the credit quantum per activity cannot be calculated, and no registration for credit generation can be practically completed. The dedicated portal www.moefcc-gcp.in is live. *Source: GCP official website, ICFRE / MoEF&CC, accessed 2026. https://www.moefcc-gcp.in/about/aboutGCP\n\n**MONITORING — Map client demand for Green Credit documentation**\n\nEngage commercial leadership and key client accounts to assess whether any existing or prospective clients — particularly those with India ESG reporting obligations — are likely to seek Green Credit documentation from logistics partners. *Operational implication:* The programme is voluntary, but *Industry interpretation:* Green Credits are emerging as a domestic ESG procurement signal in India following the Prime Minister's G20 2023 statement on green credit framework development. *Source: GCP official website, ICFRE / MoEF&CC, https://www.moefcc-gcp.in/about/aboutGCP\n\n**MONITORING — Verify non-tradability exposure before purchasing credits**\n\nBefore any commercial decision to purchase Green Credits on the trading platform (once established), verify through legal counsel that the credits being acquired were not generated to fulfil a statutory compliance obligation, as such credits are expressly non-tradable under Rule 2(3). *Legal Confirmation Required:* Whether credits offered on the trading platform will carry provenance documentation sufficient to demonstrate voluntary origin is a legal and operational question that cannot be resolved from the enacted text alone.\n\n**Primary compliance deadline:** Specific [primary compliance deadline or enforcement date] not available from primary sources as of this generation date. The programme is voluntary; no mandatory compliance deadline applies to participation. The rules came into force 12 October 2023. *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rules 1(2) and 14(1), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf\n\n**Penalty summary:** Specific [penalty provision for non-participation or breach of GCP rules] not available from primary sources as of this generation date. The enacted text does not include a dedicated penalty clause for non-participation (participation is voluntary). *Legal Confirmation Required:* Whether the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986's general enforcement powers extend to GCP-specific violations — for example, fraudulent credit claims or trading of non-tradable credits — requires legal confirmation. The rules do reference that the Administrator may \"take preventive and corrective actions to prevent fraud or mistrust\" (Rule 7(3)(i)), but no specific financial penalty quantum is stated in the enacted rules.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "8243179a-aa73-417e-8c9c-1410621b7962",
          "key": "15",
          "md": "| # | Title | Type | Issuing Body | Date | URL |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Green Credit Rules, 2023 — Gazette of India Extraordinary, S.O. 4458(E) | Binding law | Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change, Government of India | 12 October 2023 | https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf |\n| 2 | PIB Press Release: Notification issued for Green Credit Program (GCP) and Ecomark scheme | Government announcement | Press Information Bureau, MoEF&CC | 13 October 2023 | https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleaseIframePage.aspx?PRID=1967476 |\n| 3 | GCP Official Website — About GCP | Government programme portal | ICFRE / MoEF&CC | Accessed 2026 | https://www.moefcc-gcp.in/about/aboutGCP |\n| 4 | Centre notifies green credit programme | News reporting | Down to Earth, Shagun | 14 October 2023 | https://www.downtoearth.org.in/governance/centre-notifies-green-credit-programme-92305 |\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "cddff5ed-547c-47fb-a5e7-632a6891f026",
          "key": "14",
          "md": "| Date | Milestone | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| 26 June 2023 | Draft Green Credit Programme Implementation Rules, 2023 published in Gazette for public consultation (S.O. 2779(E)) | In force — past |\n| 12 October 2023 | Green Credit Rules, 2023 notified in Gazette of India Extraordinary (S.O. 4458(E)); rules enter force | In force as of 12 October 2023 |\n| 13 October 2023 | PIB notification; Ecomark Scheme also notified separately; GCP portal www.moefcc-gcp.in activated | In force as of 13 October 2023 |\n| Pending — no date notified | Activity-specific methodology notifications for water conservation and afforestation (initial phase) | Pending — re-check quarterly |\n| Pending — no date notified | Activity-specific methodology notifications for remaining six activity categories (waste management, air pollution reduction, sustainable buildings, etc.) | Pending — re-check quarterly |\n| Pending — no date notified | Designated agency designations under Rule 13(1) | Pending |\n| Pending — no date notified | Green Credit Registry and trading platform launch under Rules 10–11 | Pending — ICFRE developing with experts |\n| Every third financial year (from programme commencement) | Independent audit of Administrator, designated agency, Registry, trading platform, and knowledge and data platform (Rule 15(1)) | Recurring; first cycle not yet triggered |\n\n*Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rules 1(2), 5(1), 5(3), 7(3), 10, 11, 15, Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf; PIB Press Release, MoEF&CC, 13 October 2023. https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleaseIframePage.aspx?PRID=1967476\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "dff4bf5a-86d8-41ff-8277-80a074864747",
          "key": "6",
          "md": "**MONITORING — Activity-specific methodology notifications (Rule 5(1) and 5(3))**\n\nThe rules state that \"The methodology for calculating the green credit in respect of any activity undertaken shall be such as may be notified by the Central Government on the recommendation of the Administrator\" (Rule 5(1)) and that \"The methodologies for generating and issuing green credits for activities specified under rule (4) shall be notified by the Central Government\" (Rule 5(3)). *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rules 5(1) and 5(3), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf\n\nThe PIB press release states: \"Draft methodologies for awarding Green Credits have been developed and will be notified for stakeholder consultation. These methodologies set benchmarks for each activity/process, to ensure environmental impact and fungibility across sectors.\" *Source: PIB Press Release, MoEF&CC, 13 October 2023. https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleaseIframePage.aspx?PRID=1967476 Specific [date for methodology notification] not available from primary sources as of this generation date. *Analytical inference:* Based on the phased implementation described in the PIB release, methodologies for water conservation and afforestation are the first to be expected; logistics-relevant methodologies (waste management, air pollution reduction, sustainable buildings) are likely to follow in subsequent phases, but no timeline has been publicly announced.\n\nRe-check window: Monitor the GCP portal (https://www.moefcc-gcp.in) and the Official Gazette quarterly for methodology notifications that would activate registration eligibility for logistics-site activities.\n\n**MONITORING — Designated agency designation (Rule 13(1))**\n\nThe rules require the Administrator to designate entities as \"designated agencies\" for verification purposes \"in accordance with the guidelines approved by the Central Government\" (Rule 13(1)). *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 13(1), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf Specific [list of designated agencies] not available from primary sources as of this generation date.\n\n**MONITORING — Trading platform establishment (Rule 11(1))**\n\nThe rules state that \"The Administrator shall establish and maintain a trading platform with the approval of the Central Government\" (Rule 11(1)). *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rule 11(1), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf The PIB release describes a Green Credit Registry and trading platform \"being developed by ICFRE along with experts.\" *Source: PIB Press Release, MoEF&CC, 13 October 2023. https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleaseIframePage.aspx?PRID=1967476 Specific [trading platform launch date] not available from primary sources as of this generation date.\n\n**MONITORING — Steering Committee constitution and demand-generation recommendations (Rule 8)**\n\nThe Steering Committee, to be constituted by the Central Government, is tasked with recommending \"measures to generate demand for green credit in the country\" (Rule 14(2)) and reviewing the programme periodically. *Source: Green Credit Rules, 2023, Rules 8 and 14(2), Gazette of India Extraordinary, 12 October 2023. https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2023/249377.pdf Demand-generation recommendations — which could include procurement mandates or preferential treatment in government contracting — are a pending output that could materially change the commercial case for participation.\n\n---"
        }
      ],
