      "sections": [
        {
          "section_id": "48ef81aa-0921-4655-ade3-bb558f3908e7",
          "key": "3",
          "md": "## COST ALERT — Crude Forward Curve vs. Current Spot\n\n**WTI futures curve as of 5 June 2026 (CME Group):**\n\n| Contract Month | Last Price ($/bbl) | Change vs. Prior Settle |\n|---|---|---|\n| Jul 2026 (CLN6) | $90.15 | −$2.89 (−3.11%) |\n| Aug 2026 (CLQ6) | $87.67 | −$2.33 (−2.59%) |\n| Sep 2026 (CLU6) | $85.10 | −$1.84 (−2.12%) |\n| Oct 2026 (CLV6) | $82.70 | −$1.55 (−1.84%) |\n| Nov 2026 (CLX6) | $80.85 | −$1.30 (−1.58%) |\n| Dec 2026 (CLZ6) | $79.37 | −$1.09 (−1.35%) |\n| Jun 2027 (CLM7) | $74.82 | −$0.60 (−0.80%) |\n| Dec 2027 (CLZ7) | $72.77 | −$0.47 (−0.64%) |\n\n*Source: CME Group Crude Oil Futures Quotes, Last Updated 5 June 2026 14:39 CT.* https://www.cmegroup.com/markets/energy/crude-oil/light-sweet-crude.quotes.html\n\n*Analytical inference:* The forward curve is in clear contango — near-month WTI at $90.15 declining to $72.77 by December 2027. This structure prices in the market's expectation of easing from the current Middle East supply shock over a 12–18 month horizon. For workspaces managing multi-leg contracts with fuel-surcharge clauses indexed to spot, a shift to forward-curve-linked pricing (locking in H2 2026 rates in the $79–$85 range) could provide material downside protection relative to current spot levels, but introduces upside risk if the Strait of Hormuz remains disrupted longer than currently assumed.\n\n**EIA STEO Price Forecast vs. current spot (as of May 2026 STEO):**\n\nThe EIA's May 2026 STEO projects: Brent crude oil averaging **$95/barrel for full-year 2026** and **$79/barrel in 2027**. *Source: EIA Short-Term Energy Outlook, Release Date 12 May 2026.* https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/\n\nThe STEO notes: \"The Brent crude oil spot price increased sharply in April, reaching a high of $138 per barrel (b) on April 7 and averaging $117/b for the month, as the de facto closure of the Strait of Hormuz tightened global oil supplies.\" *Source: EIA STEO, Release Date 12 May 2026.* https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/\n\n*Analytical inference:* The EIA's $95/barrel 2026 annual average implies that the recent softening from the April peak ($117/b) and the current $92–$98 spot range are broadly on-track with the forecast baseline. The gap between the April spike and the current spot level represents genuine cost relief compared to mid-crisis pricing. However, the EIA's 2026 forecast is $14/barrel above the 2025 average of $69/barrel, meaning year-on-year fuel cost comparison benchmarks remain significantly elevated for any workspace using 2025 actuals as budget reference.\n\n**STEO Gasoline Retail Forecast:**\nThe EIA projects gasoline retail prices averaging **$3.88/gallon in 2026** and **$3.62/gallon in 2027**. *Source: EIA STEO, Release Date 12 May 2026.* https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/\n\n*Operational implication:* For road fleet operations where fuel cost is passed through at retail pump price rather than spot, the $3.88/gallon 2026 projection sets the planning baseline for domestic ground transport cost modeling. Current Gulf Coast spot at $3.042/gallon (conventional regular) sits below the retail forecast, consistent with the standard refining, distribution, and tax margin between spot and pump.\n\n**Brent vs. WTI structural relationship:**\n\n*Analytical inference:* \"around 75% of the world's traded crude is priced relative to Brent (directly and indirectly), including Dubai, Urals, and West African crudes.\" *Source: ICE Insights — What are the differences between ICE Brent and NYMEX WTI Futures?, ICE/Mike Wittner, Published October 2024.* https://www.ice.com/insights/energy/what-are-the-differences-between-ice-brent-and-nymex-wti-futures\n\n*Analytical inference:* Workspaces operating on European and Asian trade lanes will find that carrier fuel surcharges on those routes are predominantly Brent-indexed. The current Brent premium over WTI (approximately $2–$3/barrel) is narrower than it has historically been, reducing the cost differential between U.S.-origin and international carriers as a fuel cost driver. This is not a sufficient basis alone for carrier selection but affects fuel-surcharge comparison between U.S.-domestic and international air freight providers.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "6a0c813d-4f09-4269-b64b-157b1647e679",
          "key": "8",
          "md": "| # | Title | Type | URL | Date |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | EIA Spot Prices for Crude Oil and Petroleum Products | Tier 2 — U.S. Government Statistical Agency | https://eia.gov/dnav/pet/pet_pri_spt_s1_d.htm | Release Date 3 June 2026 |\n| 2 | EIA Short-Term Energy Outlook, May 2026 | Tier 2 — U.S. Government Statistical Agency Forecast | https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/ | Release Date 12 May 2026; Forecast Completed 7 May 2026 |\n| 3 | Crude Oil Prices: WTI — Cushing, Oklahoma (DCOILWTICO), FRED, St. Louis Fed | Tier 2 — Federal Reserve Bank data distribution of EIA series | https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/DCOILWTICO | Updated 3 June 2026 |\n| 4 | CME Group Crude Oil Futures Quotes (CLN6–CLZ7) | Tier 3 — Exchange market data (reference only; not for validation against real-time feeds per CME disclaimer) | https://www.cmegroup.com/markets/energy/crude-oil/light-sweet-crude.quotes.html | Last Updated 5 June 2026 14:39 CT |\n| 5 | ICE Insights — What are the differences between ICE Brent and NYMEX WTI Futures? | Tier 4 — Industry body/exchange research | https://www.ice.com/insights/energy/what-are-the-differences-between-ice-brent-and-nymex-wti-futures | Published October 2024 |\n\n---\n\n## New Sources Identified\n\n| Source Name | URL | Tier estimate (1–7) | Why this source matters |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| EIA Today in Energy – Daily Prices (Refinitiv/CME sourced) | https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/prices.php | 2 | Provides daily updated petroleum product prices in a consolidated dashboard format; useful for rapid spot-price verification without navigating the full dnav table, and sourced from Refinitiv/CME, making it cross-referenceable against the CME futures quotes used in this brief. |\n| EIA Open Data API – Petroleum Prices Catalogue | https://www.eia.gov/opendata/index.php | 2 | Enables programmatic access to the same EIA daily spot-price series (DCOILWTICO, jet fuel, diesel, gasoline) used in this brief; critical for any workspace building automated fuel-surcharge monitoring, budget-model feeds, or SAF premium tracking against a conventional jet fuel baseline. |\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "7d2830f1-5377-4098-8f9c-2a41fd78e74e",
          "key": "4",
          "md": "## COST ALERT — Middle East Supply Shock and Route Exposure\n\n**Strait of Hormuz disruption — EIA assessment:**\n\n\"We assess that Iraq, Saudi Arabia, Kuwait, the UAE, Qatar, and Bahrain collectively shut in 10.5 million barrels per day (b/d) of crude oil production in April.\" *Source: EIA STEO, Release Date 12 May 2026.* https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/\n\n\"This report assumes that the Strait of Hormuz remains effectively closed until late May, with shipping traffic beginning to pick up in June. Oil shipments through the strait, however, will not likely reach pre-conflict levels until later this year, and we expect some oil production in the Middle East to remain disrupted over that period.\" *Source: EIA STEO, Release Date 12 May 2026.* https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/\n\n\"We expect global oil inventories will fall by an average of 8.5 million b/d in the second quarter of 2026 (2Q26), keeping Brent prices around $106/b in May and June.\" *Source: EIA STEO, Release Date 12 May 2026.* https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/\n\n*Analytical inference:* The EIA's May STEO assumed $106/b Brent in May–June; the actual spot data as of 29 May–2 June ($92–$98/b) suggests the market has softened faster than the STEO projected — either reflecting faster-than-forecast Hormuz recovery or greater demand-side weakness. Workspaces using the STEO as a planning input should note the downward divergence and monitor the June 2026 STEO release (announced for 9 June 2026) for a revised price path.\n\n**UAE departure from OPEC:**\n\n\"The UAE announced its departure from OPEC, effective May 1, 2026. Our May STEO incorporates that change. OPEC production numbers in this outlook exclude data from the UAE, both for historical and forecast periods. Because the UAE held spare crude oil production capacity, we now expect OPEC's spare capacity to average 2.5 million b/d in 2027, compared with our previous forecast of 3.8 million b/d.\" *Source: EIA STEO, Release Date 12 May 2026.* https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/\n\n*Analytical inference:* The UAE's exit from OPEC, combined with reduced OPEC spare capacity, structurally reduces the cartel's ability to buffer future supply shocks. For freight operations with significant Middle East routing or Gulf hub transshipment (e.g., Dubai as an air hub for Asia–Europe lanes), this reduces the reliability of the historical \"OPEC buffer\" assumption in carrier cost models. The reduction from 3.8 million b/d to 2.5 million b/d of spare capacity by 2027 is a supply-side risk for fuel prices beyond the current crisis window.\n\n**LNG and natural gas — secondary relevance for freight operations:**\n\n\"U.S. liquefied natural gas (LNG) export capacity grew by about 0.9 billion cubic feet per day (Bcf/d) in April, led by the first shipment from Golden Pass LNG's Train 1 and additional output from Corpus Christi Stage 3.\" *Source: EIA STEO, Release Date 12 May 2026.* https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/\n\n*Analytical inference:* For freight operations with warehouse or terminal energy costs tied to natural gas tariffs, the EIA's Henry Hub projection of $3.50/MMBtu in 2026 (down from a previous forecast of $3.67/MMBtu) represents a modest cost relief relative to earlier projections. \"Global LNG prices remain elevated as a result of reduced flows through the Strait of Hormuz, with a wide spread between U.S. domestic natural gas prices and international markets.\" *Source: EIA STEO, Release Date 12 May 2026.* https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/ *Analytical inference:* This spread is relevant for workspaces operating facilities or partners in European or Asian markets where energy-intensive warehouse operations are priced against international gas benchmarks rather than Henry Hub.\n\n**Propane — regional signal:**\n\n\"U.S. propane inventories reached record highs in late 2025, and we expect they will remain above average throughout this year as production growth continues to outpace increases in demand.\" *Source: EIA STEO, Release Date 12 May 2026.* https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/\n\nMont Belvieu propane spot: **$0.843/gallon on 2 June 2026**. *Source: EIA Spot Prices, U.S. Energy Information Administration, Release Date 3 June 2026.* https://eia.gov/dnav/pet/pet_pri_spt_s1_d.htm\n\n*Operational implication:* Record U.S. propane inventories and low Mont Belvieu pricing are relevant to operations using propane-powered ground support equipment (GSE) at domestic air cargo facilities. This is the one petroleum product category where the supply shock has not materially elevated costs.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "9c75a384-b333-4c7c-9e16-0d2ae11d7445",
          "key": "6",
          "md": "**What the workspace can credibly say:**\n\n1. **\"Jet fuel spot prices have fallen significantly from the April 2026 peak.\"** This is grounded: Brent peaked at $138/barrel on 7 April 2026 and is currently trading near $98/barrel. *Source: EIA STEO, May 2026.* https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/ The workspace can accurately describe a 30%+ decline from the crisis peak when discussing current fuel surcharge trajectories with clients.\n\n2. **\"The forward curve suggests further moderation through 2026–2027.\"** The CME WTI curve prices December 2026 at $79.37/barrel and December 2027 at $72.77/barrel — both sourced facts. *Source: CME Group Crude Oil Futures Quotes, 5 June 2026.* https://www.cmegroup.com/markets/energy/crude-oil/light-sweet-crude.quotes.html *Analytical inference:* This is a legitimate data point for client conversations about medium-term surcharge trajectory, with the caveat that the curve reflects current market expectations, not a guarantee, and that the EIA's May STEO was projecting higher May–June prices than have materialised — revisions are likely.\n\n3. **\"The current elevated prices reflect a specific geopolitical event, not a structural supply shift.\"** The EIA's STEO is explicit that the Strait of Hormuz closure drove the April spike, and it forecasts prices declining to $89/barrel in Q4 2026 as production recovers. *Source: EIA STEO, May 2026.* https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/ This framing is supported and avoids overclaiming about long-run price trajectories.\n\n**What to avoid saying:**\n\n- *Do not claim* that fuel surcharges will fall immediately or on a specific schedule — carrier surcharge mechanisms vary and are not sourced in this brief.\n- *Do not claim* that the Strait of Hormuz is \"fully open\" — the EIA's May 2026 STEO stated it \"assumes that the Strait of Hormuz remains effectively closed until late May, with shipping traffic beginning to pick up in June\" but \"will not likely reach pre-conflict levels until later this year.\" *Source: EIA STEO, May 2026.* https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/ The post-May reopening status is not confirmed by a source in this brief's pool; clients in the energy or shipping sector may have more current information.\n- *Do not claim* that Brent and WTI prices are interchangeable for surcharge purposes — carriers use different benchmarks by route and mode. Treat the two as proxies, not equivalents, in client conversations.\n\n**Pitfall: using the STEO May 2026 price forecast as current:**\n\nThe STEO was completed 7 May 2026 and projected $106/b Brent for May–June; actual spot has come in below that. *Analytical inference:* Citing the STEO forecast as current pricing will overstate fuel costs to clients; use the EIA daily spot data (most recent release 3 June 2026) for current-state claims, and the STEO for directional medium-term framing only.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c2bcda17-f6f6-48ff-ad12-50c22de7ee72",
          "key": "1",
          "md": "## COST ALERT — Jet Fuel (Air-Primary Mode)\n\n**Kerosene-Type Jet Fuel — U.S. Gulf Coast spot price:**\n\nThe most recent daily data shows significant intraweek volatility. The U.S. Gulf Coast jet fuel spot price stood at **$3.987/gallon on 22 May 2026**, then fell sharply to **$3.253/gallon on 27 May**, recovered to **$3.304/gallon on 28 May**, and settled at **$3.237/gallon on 29 May**, before rebounding to **$3.569/gallon on 2 June 2026**.\n\n*Source: EIA Spot Prices for Crude Oil and Petroleum Products, U.S. Energy Information Administration, Release Date 3 June 2026.* https://eia.gov/dnav/pet/pet_pri_spt_s1_d.htm\n\n*Operational implication:* A $0.75/gallon intraweek range (22 May to 2 June) on the primary jet-fuel pricing benchmark signals that aviation fuel surcharges built on weekly or monthly averages are currently lagging actual spot exposure. For workspaces operating on air as the primary mode across transatlantic or transpacific lanes, this range creates material fuel-surcharge timing risk — carriers are buying fuel at daily spot; surcharge pass-through mechanisms typically clear on a T+30 or T+60 schedule.\n\n**Crude Oil Benchmarks — week of 29 May–2 June 2026:**\n\n- WTI — Cushing, Oklahoma: **$91.16/barrel on 29 May**, **$95.96/barrel on 2 June 2026**. *Source: EIA Spot Prices, U.S. Energy Information Administration, Release Date 3 June 2026.* https://eia.gov/dnav/pet/pet_pri_spt_s1_d.htm\n\n- Brent — Europe: **$92.88/barrel on 29 May**, **$98.29/barrel on 2 June 2026**. *Source: EIA Spot Prices, U.S. Energy Information Administration, Release Date 3 June 2026.* https://eia.gov/dnav/pet/pet_pri_spt_s1_d.htm\n\n*Analytical inference:* The WTI–Brent spread (approximately $2–$2.50/barrel as of 2 June 2026) is narrower than historical norms, which *Analytical inference:* reflects the integration of WTI Midland into the Brent basket since June 2023, reducing the traditional structural discount that landlocked Cushing delivery imposed. For workspaces pricing fuel on Brent-linked carrier surcharges on European lanes, this narrowed spread reduces the historical surcharge arbitrage between U.S.-origin and Europe-origin shipments.\n\n**Diesel (Road-Secondary Mode) — U.S. spot prices:**\n\nUltra-Low-Sulfur No. 2 Diesel Fuel spot prices as of 2 June 2026:\n- New York Harbor: **$3.656/gallon**\n- U.S. Gulf Coast: **$3.634/gallon**\n- Los Angeles: **$4.079/gallon**\n\n*Source: EIA Spot Prices for Crude Oil and Petroleum Products, U.S. Energy Information Administration, Release Date 3 June 2026.* https://eia.gov/dnav/pet/pet_pri_spt_s1_d.htm\n\n*Operational implication:* The Los Angeles diesel price premium over Gulf Coast ($0.445/gallon as of 2 June 2026) directly affects drayage and last-mile road costs on West Coast port egress — significant for ocean-to-air transshipment operations at LAX or Long Beach where road legs connect the two modes.\n\n**No. 2 Heating Oil — New York Harbor:**\n$3.551/gallon as of 2 June 2026. *Source: EIA Spot Prices, U.S. Energy Information Administration, Release Date 3 June 2026.* https://eia.gov/dnav/pet/pet_pri_spt_s1_d.htm\n\n*Analytical inference:* Heating oil spot is a proxy for distillate pricing more broadly and sits $0.10/gallon below NY Harbor ULSD, consistent with standard cetane and sulfur quality differentials. No independent operational consequence beyond the diesel figures above.\n\n**Conventional Gasoline — current spot:**\n- New York Harbor Regular: $3.187/gallon on 2 June 2026\n- U.S. Gulf Coast Regular: $3.042/gallon on 2 June 2026\n\n*Source: EIA Spot Prices, U.S. Energy Information Administration, Release Date 3 June 2026.* https://eia.gov/dnav/pet/pet_pri_spt_s1_d.htm\n\n*Analytical inference:* Gasoline prices are relevant for ground transport fleet operators and for fuel management at non-commercial vehicle depots; the Gulf Coast price at $3.042/gallon represents the lowest-cost regional baseline for U.S. domestic road operations as of this date.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "cf5b4046-b078-4991-927e-368c4cbe007e",
          "key": "7",
          "md": "## MONITORING — EIA June 2026 STEO\n\n**Next STEO release: 9 June 2026** (announced date). *Source: EIA STEO page, next release date stated as June 9, 2026.* https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/\n\n*Analytical inference:* The May STEO was based on data completed 7 May 2026 and assumed Hormuz closure through late May. Given that actual Brent spot has come in $8–$14/barrel below the May STEO projection for this period, the June release will almost certainly revise the 2026 Brent annual average downward. This revision will shift the planning baseline for fuel cost assumptions, SAF pricing models (where SAF premiums are expressed as multiples of conventional jet fuel), and carrier surcharge projections. **Re-check window: 9 June 2026.**\n\n## MONITORING — EIA Weekly Spot Price Release\n\n**Next weekly EIA spot data release: 10 June 2026.** *Source: EIA Spot Prices page, \"Next Release Date: 6/10/2026.\"* https://eia.gov/dnav/pet/pet_pri_spt_s1_d.htm\n\n## MONITORING — Strait of Hormuz Production Recovery\n\nThe EIA's May STEO assumed: \"Oil shipments through the strait, however, will not likely reach pre-conflict levels until later this year, and we expect some oil production in the Middle East to remain disrupted over that period.\" *Source: EIA STEO, Release Date 12 May 2026.* https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/\n\n*Analytical inference:* The pace of Hormuz recovery is the single largest variable driving fuel cost trajectory for H2 2026. The EIA projects Brent falling to $89/b in Q4 2026 and $79/b in 2027 on a recovery assumption; a slower recovery would support higher prices. Workspaces with air freight contracts renewing in Q3 2026 should monitor recovery pace as a trigger for renegotiating fuel surcharge mechanisms. No sourced date for full recovery is available in the provided source pool.\n\n## MONITORING — OPEC Spare Capacity Reduction Following UAE Departure\n\nThe EIA now expects OPEC spare capacity to average **2.5 million b/d in 2027**, down from a previous forecast of **3.8 million b/d**, following the UAE's departure from OPEC effective 1 May 2026. *Source: EIA STEO, Release Date 12 May 2026.* https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/\n\n*Analytical inference:* Reduced spare capacity structurally increases oil price volatility sensitivity to future supply disruptions. This is a background condition — not an immediate trigger — but it changes the risk profile of long-duration freight contracts with fixed or capped fuel surcharge clauses extending into 2027.\n\n## MONITORING — CME WTI Volatility Index (CVOL)\n\nThe CME Group WTI CVOL (30-day implied volatility on WTI crude options) stood at **60.9465** as of 5 June 2026, with a change of **−3.1203** on the day. *Source: CME Group Crude Oil Futures Quotes, Last Updated 5 June 2026.* https://www.cmegroup.com/markets/energy/crude-oil/light-sweet-crude.quotes.html\n\n*Analytical inference:* A CVOL of 60.9 is elevated relative to typical calm-market levels (historically in the 20–35 range), indicating that options markets are pricing significant uncertainty in WTI prices over the next 30 days. This elevated implied volatility is relevant context for any workspace considering fuel surcharge lock-in or forward purchasing decisions — the cost of certainty (hedging premium) is high in this environment.\n\n---"
        }
