          "section_id": "3c69ba0d-7dda-4eca-9e79-fe36d02a899b",
          "key": "1",
          "md": "**MONITORING**\n\n*Specific deployment figures, fleet size, terminal capacity, charter terms, and bunkering pilot results not available from primary source blocks as of 2025-05-27. The SOURCE blocks returned cookie consent infrastructure text only; no article body content was retrievable.*\n\n*Analytical inference:* Based on the corroborating source metadata provided with this item (not yet span-grounded), Yara Clean Ammonia appears to be operating as an integrated ammonia supply and logistics entity, combining production, shipping, and terminal infrastructure, with activity spanning European import terminals, ammonia-fueled vessel charters, and live bunkering trials. The specific figures attached to these activities — fleet count, terminal throughput, charter delivery windows, pilot transfer volumes — require retrieval of the actual press release and AEA profile content before they can be stated as FACTs.\n\n*Analytical inference:* The NYK time-charter corroborator is described in the prompt metadata as the \"world's first time-charter agreement for an ammonia-fueled medium gas carrier,\" with a delivery date referenced as November 2026. This is not span-groundable from the SOURCE blocks. It is registered here as a research gap and as a high-priority retrieval target, because first-mover charter structures of this type set commercial precedents that cascade into port call planning, bunkering contract terms, and Scope 3 reporting methodology for ocean freight customers.\n\n*Analytical inference:* The Brunsbüttel terminal corroborator is described as covering an October 2024 opening with approximately 3 Mt/yr import capacity, positioned as a key node in European hydrogen economy infrastructure. This is not span-groundable. It is registered as a research gap and high-priority retrieval target because European import terminal capacity directly affects whether ammonia as a marine fuel reaches commercial scale on northern European trade lanes within IMO 2030 compliance windows.\n\n*Analytical inference:* The Pilbara bunkering pilot corroborator is described as covering the world's first ship-to-ship ammonia bunkering transfer at anchorage, at Port Dampier, Western Australia, with GCMD, announced June 2025. This is not span-groundable. It is registered as a research gap and high-priority retrieval target because successful STS bunkering at anchorage is the operational precondition for ammonia as a drop-in bunker option on Asia-Pacific trade lanes — without it, the fuel remains port-berth-only, which limits commercial deployment severely.\n\n*Analytical inference:* The Air Products partnership corroborator describes advanced negotiations covering a Louisiana Clean Energy Complex and NEOM partnership, with approximately 2.8 Mt/yr low-carbon ammonia and a 25-year offtake target, with FID being targeted. This is not span-groundable due to the 403 error on the Air Products source. It is registered as a research gap and high-priority retrieval target because a 25-year, 2.8 Mt/yr offtake at FID stage would represent one of the largest long-term low-carbon ammonia supply commitments publicly announced, with direct implications for whether supply-side certainty exists to underpin vessel and bunkering infrastructure investment.\n\n*Specific deployment figures (fleet size, terminal throughput, charter delivery confirmation, pilot transfer volumes, FID date, offtake volumes) not available from primary source blocks as of 2025-05-27.*\n\n---"
