      "sections": [
        {
          "section_id": "034afb76-f6eb-4562-86e0-f862adee547e",
          "key": "2",
          "md": "**The regulatory landscape — four instruments:**\n\n**Instrument 1 — Renewable Energy Standards (Metro Council Legislation, 2019)**\n\"In 2019, Metro Council passed legislation establishing renewable energy standards for Metro Government, specifying phased percentages of carbon-free energy usage within Metro's energy supply portfolio, reaching 100% total carbon-free energy resources in 2041.\" *Source: Sustainability (Mayor's Office), Metro Nashville, nashville.gov/departments/mayor/sustainability-resilience-environment/sustainability.*\n\nEffective date (Instrument 1): The legislation was passed in 2019 and is in force. *Source: Sustainability (Mayor's Office), Metro Nashville.*\n\n**Instrument 2 — LEED Mandatory Certification for New Metro Government Buildings**\n\"New Metro Government buildings pursue green building certifications under the Leadership in Energy and Environmental Design (LEED) program.\" *Source: Sustainability (Mayor's Office), Metro Nashville.* The ACEEE City Scorecard confirms: \"New constructed municipal buildings are required to meet LEED certification requirements.\" *Source: Nashville City Scorecard, ACEEE, database.aceee.org/city/nashville-tn.*\n\nEffective date (Instrument 2): Specific enactment date not available from primary sources as of May 2026. *Operational implication:* The requirement is described as active (\"are required\"), indicating it is currently in force.\n\n**Instrument 3 — Energy Benchmarking Program (2022 Rollout)**\nIn 2022, Metro Government conducted energy benchmarking across 335 facilities. \"Benchmarking entails collecting basic information about each building (e.g., functional type, square footage, age) and logging one year's worth of energy consumption (including electricity, natural gas, steam, and chilled water). Dividing energy use, measured in kBTUs, by square feet yields a facility's Energy Use Intensity (EUI).\" *Source: Metro Government Energy Benchmarking, General Services, nashville.gov/departments/general-services/news/metro-government-energy-benchmarking.*\n\n**Instrument 4 — Building Code Upgrade (November 2020)**\n\"In November 2020, the Metro Council unanimously adopted legislation upgrading Nashville's building code standards. The new standards dramatically improve energy efficiency, reduce environmental impact and the City's carbon footprint.\" *Source: Sustainability (Mayor's Office), Metro Nashville.*\n\nAdditionally: \"Nashville has adopted the 2018 International Building Codes.\" Commercial properties and residential properties both fall under the 2018 International Building Codes. \"The city's zEPI score for its commercial energy code is 49.5\" and \"the city's zEPI score for its residential energy code is 57.75.\" *Source: Nashville City Scorecard, ACEEE.*\n\n**Primary deadline (closest in time):** The 100% carbon-free energy target is 2041, with \"stairstep goals each year until then.\" *Source: Energy Sustainability in Metro Nashville Government, General Services, nashville.gov/departments/general-services/sustainability/socket/energy.* Specific intermediate annual stairstep percentages not available from primary sources as of May 2026.\n\n**Jurisdictional scope:** These programs apply within the Metropolitan Government of Nashville and Davidson County. The benchmarking program and LEED requirements apply to Metro Government–owned and –operated facilities. The 2018 IBC energy code applies to commercial and residential construction within Nashville's jurisdiction. *Source: Metro Government Energy Benchmarking, General Services; Nashville City Scorecard, ACEEE.*\n\n**Why this applies to workspaces operating in Nashville:**\n\n*Operational implication:* Any workspace occupying, leasing, or managing facilities within Metro Nashville's real estate portfolio — or constructing new facilities subject to Nashville's building code — is operationally affected by these instruments. For workspaces in freight, logistics, or warehousing, new construction triggers 2018 IBC energy code compliance, and any Metro-owned facility may be subject to the benchmarking and ESRF upgrade cycle.\n\n*Operational implication:* The ACEEE notes Nashville \"is considering a commercial benchmarking program and is gathering stakeholder input.\" *Source: Nashville City Scorecard, ACEEE.* This forward signal means privately held commercial buildings — including logistics hubs, distribution centres, and terminal facilities not owned by Metro — may face future benchmarking obligations as a separate, forthcoming instrument. See Section 6.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "33889a99-b4dc-43aa-a53a-6fb9d099578a",
          "key": "13",
          "md": "**ENERGY STAR Portfolio Manager as benchmarking standard:**\n\"Nearly 25% of U.S. commercial building space is already actively benchmarking in Portfolio Manager, making it the industry-leading benchmarking tool. It also serves as the national benchmarking tool in Canada.\" *Source: Benchmark Your Building With Portfolio Manager, ENERGY STAR.*\n\n*Analytical inference:* Portfolio Manager's dominant market position means that workspaces adopting it for Metro compliance will also be positioned for compliance with most U.S. city-level benchmarking ordinances that have adopted it, reducing the marginal cost of multi-city compliance readiness.\n\n**Community solar as an alternative to on-site solar:**\n\"Music City Solar is Nashville's first solar park. With 17,020 panels available for subscription to participating customers, NES is making steps in offering clean, efficient, maintenance-free energy.\" Metro General Services \"subscribes to 510 Music City Solar panels. Energy generated offsets a portion of the usage at Metro's Historic Courthouse.\" *Source: Energy Sustainability in Metro Nashville Government, General Services.*\n\n*Operational implication:* For workspaces that cannot install rooftop solar (e.g., leased facilities, facilities with structural constraints), subscription to Music City Solar panels through Nashville Electric Service represents an alternative path to renewable energy offset. This is particularly relevant for workspaces needing to reduce Scope 2 emissions for client reporting.\n\n**Geo-exchange systems:**\n\"Geo-exchange (also known as geothermal) systems use heat pumps to circulate water through a series of underground pipes, allowing the near-constant underground temperatures (around 50-60 °F) to help cool the building in the summer and help heat it in the winter.\" Several Metro General Services facilities, including the Donelson Library, utilise geo-exchange systems. *Source: Energy Sustainability in Metro Nashville Government, General Services.*\n\n*Analytical inference:* For new logistics or warehouse construction in Nashville with suitable subsurface conditions, geo-exchange systems represent an energy efficiency technology available in the local market, consistent with Nashville's building efficiency goals.\n\n**CBECS as national benchmarking reference:**\nThe U.S. Energy Information Administration publishes CBECS, which Metro uses to compare facility EUI to national averages. *Source: Metro Government Energy Benchmarking, General Services.* *Analytical inference:* For freight and logistics workspaces operating warehouse, distribution, or terminal facilities in Nashville, CBECS warehouse sector averages provide the relevant peer comparison group.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "3e828e15-4998-4708-a1d1-c90e7d230e9a",
          "key": "11",
          "md": "**Benchmarking data infrastructure:**\nFor workspaces occupying Metro-owned buildings or preparing for a commercial benchmarking obligation, the following operational system requirements apply:\n\n- **Utility data collection:** Benchmarking requires logging \"one year's worth of energy consumption (including electricity, natural gas, steam, and chilled water).\" *Source: Metro Government Energy Benchmarking, General Services.* *Operational implication:* Workspaces that do not currently receive itemised utility bills per building or sub-meter individual spaces will need to establish metering or sub-metering infrastructure before they can participate in or comply with benchmarking.\n- **Portfolio Manager account:** ENERGY STAR Portfolio Manager is the designated tool. *Analytical inference:* Workspaces with multiple Nashville facilities should consolidate them into a single Portfolio Manager account to enable cross-portfolio EUI comparison, consistent with Metro's own approach. *Source: Benchmark Your Building With Portfolio Manager, ENERGY STAR.*\n- **EUI calculation capability:** The workspace should be able to compute EUI (kBTU/sq ft) per facility and compare to CBECS national averages. *Operational implication:* Facilities with 24/7 operations or high-energy-intensity uses will require context documentation (occupancy hours, process loads) to normalise EUI comparisons.\n\n**Energy management system:**\nMetro's General Services \"began working across multiple Metro real estate portfolios to engage in energy benchmarking\" and collectively deploys \"an energy management system, tracking of energy metrics, design and implementation of energy savings projects, energy conservation measures, energy audits, infrastructure retrofits, automated systems, utility expense management, and building retro-commissioning.\" *Source: Sustainability (Mayor's Office), Metro Nashville.*\n\n*Operational implication:* For workspaces occupying Metro facilities, the building energy management system is Metro-operated. The workspace's operational gap is ensuring that space-level data (occupancy hours, plug loads, process energy) is available for submission to Metro's benchmarking cycle if requested.\n\n**LEED documentation (new construction):**\nAny new Metro Government facility construction in which the workspace is involved (as developer, designer, or contractor) will require LEED documentation aligned to the required certification level. *Operational implication:* LEED documentation is a design-phase and construction-phase obligation; retrofitting documentation post-construction is significantly more expensive.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "493ef254-672b-4068-9dd0-b090599cd50b",
          "key": "record_facts",
          "md": "[primary_deadline] No verbatim primary deadline statement was located in the captured source text for this record-grade item. A full-brief regrounding will re-examine this gap when this item upgrades from record to brief."
