        {
          "section_id": "675bf1d6-0a00-4225-adea-5f8ab1834de3",
          "key": "8",
          "md": "### 8a. Renewable Portfolio Standard — Metro Government Facilities\n\nThe RPS, passed in 2019, requires Metro Government to achieve 100% total carbon-free energy resources in 2041, with stairstep percentage goals each year. *Source: Sustainability (Mayor's Office), Metro Nashville; Energy Sustainability in Metro Nashville Government, General Services.*\n\nSpecific annual stairstep percentages not available from primary sources as of May 2026. The enacted ordinance text is the authoritative source for the full trajectory.\n\nMetro's current solar footprint: \"Metro's total solar footprint across 30 installations currently totals 5.52 megawatts.\" *Source: Sustainability (Mayor's Office), Metro Nashville.* General Services \"boasts 870,000 kWh of generation annually across 12 buildings.\" *Source: Sustainability (Mayor's Office), Metro Nashville.*\n\nLarge-scale solar commitment: Metro is participating in TVA's Green Invest program \"through an investment for 40 MW of a planned 200 MW solar farm near Tullahoma, TN.\" *Source: Energy Sustainability in Metro Nashville Government, General Services.*\n\n*Operational implication:* For workspaces within Metro-owned facilities, the RPS trajectory directly affects the carbon intensity of electricity supplied to those buildings over the 2024–2041 window, which is relevant to Scope 2 emissions accounting and any client-facing sustainability disclosures.\n\n### 8b. LEED Certification — New Metro Government Buildings\n\n\"New constructed municipal buildings are required to meet LEED certification requirements.\" *Source: Nashville City Scorecard, ACEEE.* LEED is \"developed by the U.S. Green Building Council\" and is \"the most widely used green building rating system in the world.\" *Source: Sustainability (Mayor's Office), Metro Nashville.*\n\nQualification: The requirement applies to new Metro Government buildings. The level of LEED certification required (Certified, Silver, Gold, Platinum) is not specified in the corpus sources. *Specific certification level required not available from primary sources as of May 2026.*\n\n### 8c. Energy Benchmarking — Methodology and Scope\n\nScope (2022 program): 335 facilities, representing 20.3 million square feet, with total energy consumption of \"nearly 1.3 billion kBTUs.\" Participating departments included Metro Nashville Public Schools, General Services, Farmers Market, Fairgrounds, Parks and Recreation, Metro Action Commission, Nashville Fire Department, Davidson County Sheriff's Office, WeGo, Nashville Department of Transportation, and Nashville Public Library. *Source: Metro Government Energy Benchmarking, General Services.*\n\nMethodology: \"Benchmarking entails collecting basic information about each building (e.g., functional type, square footage, age) and logging one year's worth of energy consumption (including electricity, natural gas, steam, and chilled water). Dividing energy use, measured in kBTUs, by square feet yields a facility's Energy Use Intensity (EUI).\" *Source: Metro Government Energy Benchmarking, General Services.*\n\nComparison standard: EUI is benchmarked against the CBECS national average published by the U.S. Energy Information Administration. *Source: Metro Government Energy Benchmarking, General Services.*\n\nTool: ENERGY STAR Portfolio Manager is the designated benchmarking platform. *Source: Metro Government Energy Benchmarking, General Services.*\n\n*Operational implication:* Higher relative EUI \"could be due to a number of factors, including aging HVAC equipment, problems with the building envelope, 24/7 or afterhours usage, and more.\" *Source: Metro Government Energy Benchmarking, General Services.* Freight and logistics operations involving 24/7 usage or high-bay conditioning are particularly vulnerable to elevated EUI relative to CBECS averages for similar building types.\n\n### 8d. Energy Savings Revolving Fund (ESRF)\n\n\"Metro Nashville Capital Spending Plans have included $2 million for General Services' Energy Savings Revolving Fund (ESRF). The fund is used to perform energy efficiency upgrades in Metro buildings. More than thirty-five facilities have received facility-wide lighting and/or HVAC replacements to date. Utility cost savings can then be re-invested into additional retrofit projects.\" *Source: Energy Sustainability in Metro Nashville Government, General Services.*\n\n*Operational implication:* For workspaces in Metro-owned facilities, ESRF-funded retrofits (LED lighting, HVAC replacement) represent a near-term operational disruption risk and a medium-term energy cost reduction opportunity. Workspaces should coordinate with Metro's General Services on retrofit scheduling.\n\n### 8e. 2018 International Building Code — Commercial Energy Provisions\n\n*Analytical inference:* \"Commercial properties must comply with the 2018 International Building Codes.\" zEPI score for commercial: 49.5. Compliance is verified through \"plan review and performance testing.\" *Source: Nashville City Scorecard, ACEEE.*\n\n*Analytical inference:* The city \"has not passed an ordinance mandating new construction be solar-ready.\" *Source: Nashville City Scorecard, ACEEE.* \"The city has not adopted policies requiring buildings to be EV-ready, but is considering such legislation.\" *Source: Nashville City Scorecard, ACEEE.*\n\n*Analytical inference:* \"The city is prohibited from adopting electrification policies by the state.\" *Source: Nashville City Scorecard, ACEEE.*\n\n*Analytical inference:* The state-level prohibition on electrification policies limits Nashville's ability to mandate all-electric construction, which caps the energy code's decarbonisation reach for new commercial buildings.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "684dbbec-b174-4541-a90f-684fe258990a",
          "key": "3",
          "md": "**MONITORING — Commercial Benchmarking Stakeholder Engagement**\n\nEngage Nashville's commercial benchmarking consultation process now. *Operational implication:* The ACEEE Scorecard records that \"Nashville is considering a commercial benchmarking program and is gathering stakeholder input.\" *Source: Nashville City Scorecard, ACEEE, database.aceee.org/city/nashville-tn.* For workspaces operating owned or leased commercial facilities in Davidson County, participating in stakeholder engagement before the program is adopted allows positioning on exemption thresholds, reporting timelines, and data privacy protections.\n\nAction: Identify the Metro office administering stakeholder input (General Services or Mayor's Office Sustainability division) and register participation within 30 days. *Analytical inference:* Early engagement typically produces more favorable threshold calibration for large-facility operators than post-adoption advocacy.\n\n---\n\n**MONITORING — 2018 IBC Energy Code Compliance for New Construction**\n\nVerify that any planned Nashville facility construction or major renovation meets 2018 International Building Code energy standards. *Operational implication:* \"Commercial properties must comply with the 2018 International Building Codes\" with a zEPI score of 49.5, and the city \"requires plan review and performance testing to verify code compliance.\" *Source: Nashville City Scorecard, ACEEE.* Failure to design to these standards before permit submission delays construction timelines.\n\nAction: Commission an energy-code compliance review by a Nashville-qualified energy consultant before schematic design is finalised on any new Nashville facility.\n\n---\n\n**MONITORING — Annual Benchmarking Participation (Metro-Occupied Facilities)**\n\nFor workspaces operating within Metro Government–owned buildings, confirm whether the facility is included in the annual benchmarking cycle. \"As Metro Government continues to track energy use year upon year, trends will emerge.\" *Source: Metro Government Energy Benchmarking, General Services, nashville.gov/departments/general-services/news/metro-government-energy-benchmarking.*\n\nAction: Assess which facilities in the Nashville portfolio are Metro-owned and thus subject to EUI benchmarking and ESRF upgrade prioritisation. Operational disruption from HVAC replacement or LED retrofits may affect scheduling.\n\n---\n\n**Penalty summary:** Specific financial penalty provisions for non-compliance with Nashville's benchmarking program or LEED requirement for Metro buildings are not available from primary sources as of May 2026. The ACEEE notes no penalty enforcement mechanism has been identified for the current benchmarking program. *Legal Confirmation Required:* whether private occupants of Metro facilities face any direct financial penalty for energy-related non-compliance under existing Metro ordinances requires legal review.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "76e5789f-0a6a-4f22-93f2-1823a6b07ac5",
          "key": "4",
          "md": "The compliance chain has two distinct tracks:\n\n**Track A — Metro Government as building owner-operator:**\nMetro Government owns and operates 335+ facilities. The benchmarking obligation, LEED requirement for new construction, and Renewable Portfolio Standard sit with Metro as the obligated entity. \"General Services staff guided each agency as to how to collect data, enter it into Energy Star Portfolio Manager, and analyze the results.\" *Source: Metro Government Energy Benchmarking, General Services.*\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* whether a workspace that is a tenant, lessee, or contractor operating within a Metro Government facility bears any direct legal obligation under Metro's benchmarking or RPS legislation, or whether the obligation rests exclusively with Metro as building owner, requires counsel review.\n\n*Analytical inference:* **Track B — Private commercial building owners and developers in Nashville:**\nNew construction must comply with 2018 IBC energy codes, enforced through plan review and performance testing. \"Nashville also requires residential building developers to use certified third parties to conduct blower door and duct blast tests.\" *Source: Nashville City Scorecard, ACEEE.*\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* whether a workspace operating as developer, building owner, or tenant in a non-Metro commercial building in Nashville faces any current benchmarking obligation — as opposed to the proposed forthcoming commercial program — requires legal review against current Metro Code.\n\n*Analytical inference:* **Track C — Incentive and financing programs (voluntary access):**\n\"In 2023, Nashville also adopted a local C-PACER program, which enables access to low-interest and long-term financing for commercial energy efficiency, renewable energy, and resiliency improvements.\" *Source: Nashville City Scorecard, ACEEE.* This is a voluntary access mechanism, not a compliance obligation.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "8138a996-8e22-4aee-82c0-d59fb38d2c52",
          "key": "10",
          "md": "**ENERGY STAR Portfolio Manager registration (Metro Government facilities):**\nMetro Government facilities participating in benchmarking are entered into ENERGY STAR Portfolio Manager. \"General Services staff guided each agency as to how to collect data, enter it into Energy Star Portfolio Manager, and analyze the results.\" *Source: Metro Government Energy Benchmarking, General Services.*\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* whether non-Metro entities occupying Metro buildings face any direct registration obligation in Portfolio Manager, or whether Metro General Services holds the reporting obligation as building owner, requires legal review.\n\n**Commercial benchmarking (forthcoming):**\nThe proposed commercial benchmarking program has not yet been adopted. Specific registration format, reporting deadline, and frequency are not available from primary sources as of May 2026. *Operational implication:* When adopted, a commercial benchmarking ordinance would likely require annual Portfolio Manager data submission for covered buildings. Re-check window: Q4 2026.\n\n**C-PACER program:**\nAdopted in 2023. Specific registration or reporting requirements under the C-PACER program are not available from primary sources as of May 2026.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "bb02656a-d3e9-431b-b289-5e7e964ee550",
