        {
          "section_id": "cdb2855e-b94c-49f1-8987-2fb8be3e097f",
          "key": "1",
          "md": "This document synthesises Metro Nashville and Davidson County's building energy programs as they apply to workspaces operating or occupying facilities within Nashville's jurisdiction. It covers four interlocking regulatory instruments and programmatic frameworks: (1) Metro Council renewable energy legislation for government buildings, (2) LEED mandatory certification for new Metro Government construction, (3) Metro-wide energy benchmarking using ENERGY STAR Portfolio Manager, and (4) the Energy Savings Revolving Fund (ESRF) and associated efficiency-upgrade program.\n\nConvention notes: where a requirement falls on Metro Government as building owner-operator, the workspace's exposure depends on its tenancy or operational relationship to those facilities. *Legal Confirmation Required:* whether any private-sector tenant or operator within a Metro-owned building faces direct compliance obligations, as distinct from Metro's own obligations as building owner, requires legal review. Dates cited reflect published Metro Government sources as of April–May 2023 and December 2025.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "d45ffe87-7621-48c7-8797-ca79b2f0daeb",
          "key": "7",
          "md": "**Q1: Does the Metro Government benchmarking obligation apply to privately owned commercial buildings in Nashville today?**\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* The current program covers Metro-owned facilities. Whether any existing Metro ordinance extends benchmarking obligations to privately owned commercial buildings — prior to the adoption of the proposed commercial benchmarking program — requires legal review of current Metro Code.\n\n**Q2: Does the 2018 IBC energy code apply to tenant fit-outs and interior renovations, or only to new construction and major renovations?**\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* The corpus describes the code as applying to new construction; the scope of its application to tenant improvements requires review of the specific IBC provisions as adopted by Nashville.\n\n**Q3: Does the C-PACER program impose any ongoing performance obligations as a condition of financing?**\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* C-PACER financing is described as enabling access to financing for energy efficiency improvements; whether it carries ongoing reporting or performance covenants requires review of the specific program agreement terms.\n\n**Q4: Is the ENERGY STAR score of 75 required for any Metro facility, or is ENERGY STAR certification voluntary?**\nFACT: ENERGY STAR Portfolio Manager states that \"if your building receives an ENERGY STAR score of 75 or higher, it may be eligible for ENERGY STAR certification.\" *Source: ENERGY STAR Portfolio Manager, energystar.gov.* This frames certification as eligibility-based and elective, not mandated. *Legal Confirmation Required:* whether Metro Nashville's own ordinances impose an ENERGY STAR certification requirement on any category of Metro facility requires review of the enacted legislation text.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "da8ab516-6095-4fb2-910b-97e6ab89b3e2",
          "key": "6",
          "md": "MONITORING — **Commercial Benchmarking Ordinance (Pre-adoption, stakeholder input phase)**\n\n*Analytical inference:* \"Nashville is considering a commercial benchmarking program and is gathering stakeholder input.\" *Source: Nashville City Scorecard, ACEEE, database.aceee.org/city/nashville-tn.* Last updated September 2023.\n\n- **Event type:** Municipal ordinance adoption\n- **Issuing body:** Metro Nashville Council (General Services / Mayor's Office Sustainability division as programme leads)\n- **Expected date:** Specific anticipated adoption date not available from primary sources as of May 2026.\n- *Analytical inference:* Based on comparable U.S. municipal benchmarking ordinance timelines (stakeholder input to final adoption typically 12–24 months), adoption could occur in 2025–2026, though Nashville's pace is unconfirmed. Re-check window: Q4 2026.\n- **Impact:** A commercial benchmarking ordinance would impose annual energy data reporting obligations on private commercial building owners in Davidson County, potentially including logistics facilities, warehouses, and office buildings above a defined square-footage threshold.\n- **What to expect to decide:** Whether to participate proactively in stakeholder input to shape threshold calibration (building size, use-type exemptions), reporting format, and enforcement timeline.\n\nMONITORING — **Green Invest Solar Farm (Operational Date 2026)**\n\"Once operational in 2026, this installation will account for about 13% of Metro's annual power supply.\" *Source: Sustainability (Mayor's Office), Metro Nashville.* This is the 40 MW component of the Green Invest Program. *Analytical inference:* When operational, this will advance Metro's RPS trajectory and may trigger reassessment of the annual stairstep percentage targets.\n\n---"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "f86e016b-c67d-4cb3-a1fc-ef2bda37b4bd",
          "key": "5",
          "md": "*No single consolidated authoritative guidance document governing all four instruments has been identified as of May 2026. The four instruments each derive from separate legislative or programmatic sources. Relevant analysis per instrument is provided below.*\n\n**Instrument 1 — RPS (2019 legislation):**\nThe legislation specifies \"phased percentages of carbon-free energy usage within Metro's energy supply portfolio, reaching 100% total carbon-free energy resources in 2041.\" *Source: Sustainability (Mayor's Office), Metro Nashville.* Metro's Renewable Portfolio Standard \"requires that Metro Government utilize 100% tier-one renewable energy by 2041 with stairstep goals each year until then.\" *Source: Energy Sustainability in Metro Nashville Government, General Services.*\n\n*Analytical inference:* The \"stairstep goals each year\" language implies annual intermediate targets, but the specific percentages per year are not reproduced in the available corpus sources. This is a research gap; the enacted ordinance text (not reproduced in corpus) would contain the full trajectory.\n\n**Instrument 2 — LEED Requirement:**\n\"New constructed municipal buildings are required to meet LEED certification requirements.\" The density bonus program in the Central Business District scales with LEED certification stringency: \"Nashville offers a density bonus for developments in the Central Business District if the projects achieve LEED certification. The density bonus scales with the stringency of the certification.\" *Source: Nashville City Scorecard, ACEEE.*\n\n**Instrument 3 — Benchmarking using ENERGY STAR Portfolio Manager:**\nENERGY STAR Portfolio Manager is \"an interactive resource management tool that enables you to benchmark the energy use of any type of building, all in a secure online environment. Nearly 25% of U.S. commercial building space is already actively benchmarking in Portfolio Manager, making it the industry-leading benchmarking tool.\" *Source: Benchmark Your Building With Portfolio Manager, ENERGY STAR, energystar.gov/buildings/facility-owners-and-managers/existing-buildings/use-portfolio-manager.*\n\nThe EUI metric is calculated as: energy use in kBTUs divided by square footage. \"The U.S. Energy Information Administration publishes the Commercial Buildings Energy Consumption Survey (CBECS), which allows Metro Government to compare our buildings' EUI to a national average.\" *Source: Metro Government Energy Benchmarking, General Services.*\n\n**ENERGY STAR Score:** \"The ENERGY STAR score compares your building's energy performance to similar buildings nationwide, normalized for weather and operating characteristics. A score of 50 represents median performance. A higher score is better than average; lower is worse.\" Buildings achieving a score of 75 or higher may be eligible for ENERGY STAR certification. *Source: ENERGY STAR Portfolio Manager, energystar.gov.*\n\n*Analytical inference:* **Instrument 4 — 2018 IBC / Building Code:**\n\"In November 2020, the Metro Council unanimously adopted legislation upgrading Nashville's building code standards.\" The city \"requires plan review and performance testing to verify code compliance.\" The codes department \"has partnered with a private company to provide training to developers, owners, tradespeople, contractors, and designers.\" Pre-submittal meetings with developers, architects, and owners are available to discuss code compliance including energy code. *Source: Nashville City Scorecard, ACEEE.*\n\n---"
        }
